hadiths[653]{hadithnumber,text}:
"11957","ʿIṣmah ibn Qays al-Sulamī, a Companion of the Messenger of Allah ﷺ, reports from the Prophet ﷺ that he used to seek refuge from the trial of the East, and that when he was asked about the trial of the West he described it as even greater. > **""That is greater and greater.""**"
"11958","In another narration from the same Companion it is reported that the Prophet ﷺ used to seek refuge during his prayer from the trial of the West."
"11959","Al-Qāsim reports that ʿAbd Allāh ibn Masʿūd, raḍiya Allāhu ʿanhu, forbade anyone to ask Allah for refuge from trial in absolute terms, since every person necessarily encounters trial, and instructed that whoever seeks refuge should seek it from the misguiding aspects of trials, for Allah Almighty says that one's wealth and children are but a trial."
"11960","Abū Hurayrah, raḍiya Allāhu ʿanhu, reports that the Messenger of Allah ﷺ commanded seeking refuge with Allah from the onset of the seventieth year and from the rule of inexperienced youths, and further declared that the world would not end until it fell into the hands of the vilest of the vile. > **""Seek refuge with Allah from the head of the seventieth year and from the rule of children.""** > **""The world will not pass away until it belongs to the vilest son of the vilest.""**"
"11961","ʿUqbah ibn ʿĀmir, raḍiya Allāhu ʿanhu, reports that the Messenger of Allah ﷺ used to supplicate for refuge from evil days, evil nights, evil hours, evil companions, and evil neighbours in the lasting abode. > **""O Allah, I seek refuge in You from an evil day, an evil night, an evil hour, an evil companion, and an evil neighbour in the abode of residence.""**"
"11962","Abū al-Dardāʾ, raḍiya Allāhu ʿanhu, reports from the Prophet ﷺ that everything diminishes except evil, for it only keeps increasing. > **""Everything decreases except evil, for it is increased therein.""**"
"11963","Ibn ʿAbbās, raḍiya Allāhu ʿanhumā, reports that the Messenger of Allah ﷺ warned against contentious disputation with people, since it buries honour and exposes hidden faults. > **""Beware of disputing with people, for it buries honour and exposes faults.""**"
"11964","Jābir ibn ʿAtīk, raḍiya Allāhu ʿanhu, reports that ʿAbd Allāh ibn ʿUmar came to the Banū Muʿāwiyah quarter of the Anṣār and asked where the Messenger of Allah ﷺ had prayed in their mosque and what three things he had supplicated for there. Jābir indicated the spot and answered that the Prophet ﷺ had asked that no enemy from outside them be given dominance over them and that they not be destroyed through famine, both of which were granted, and that he had asked that their violence not be set among themselves, which was withheld. Ibn ʿUmar confirmed his truthfulness and observed that mutual bloodshed would therefore continue until the Day of Resurrection."
"11965","Shaddād ibn Aws, raḍiya Allāhu ʿanhu, reports that the Prophet ﷺ described how the earth was folded for him until he saw its easts and wests, that his community's dominion would reach what was folded for him, and that he was granted the white and red treasures. He then recounted asking his Lord not to destroy his community by general famine, not to empower an outside enemy to annihilate them, and not to divide them into factions tasting one another's violence, and receiving the reply that the divine decree is never repelled: the first two were granted, but some of them would destroy, kill, and enslave others. The narration adds his warning that he feared only misguiding leaders for his community and that once the sword was laid among them it would never be lifted until the Day of Resurrection. > **""Indeed, Allah folded the earth for me and I saw its easts and wests, and the dominion of my community will reach what was folded for me. I was granted the two treasures, the white and the red. I asked my Lord Almighty not to destroy my community by a general famine, not to empower an enemy over them who would annihilate them generally, not to divide them into factions and make some of them taste the violence of others. He said: O Muḥammad, when I decree a decree it is never repelled. I have granted you for your community that I will not destroy them by a general famine, nor empower an enemy from outside them who would annihilate them generally, until some of them destroy others, some kill others, and some enslave others.""** > **""I fear for my community only the misguiding leaders, and when the sword is placed among my community it will never be lifted from them until the Day of Resurrection.""**"
"11966","Abū Baṣrah al-Ghifārī, a Companion of the Messenger of Allah ﷺ, reports the Prophet's ﷺ account of asking his Lord for four things and being granted three but refused one: that his community never unite upon misguidance, granted; that they not be destroyed by famine as earlier nations were, granted; that no enemy be given dominance over them, granted; but that they not be split into factions tasting one another's violence, refused. > **""I asked my Lord Almighty for four things; He granted me three and withheld one. I asked Allah Almighty that my community never unite upon misguidance, and He granted it to me. I asked Allah Almighty not to destroy them by famine as He destroyed the nations before them, and He granted it to me. I asked Allah Almighty not to give an enemy dominance over them, and He granted it to me. I asked Allah Almighty not to divide them into factions and make some of them taste the violence of others, and He withheld it from me.""**"
"11967","Abū Hurayrah, raḍiya Allāhu ʿanhu, reports from the Prophet ﷺ that he asked his Lord for four qualities on behalf of his community and was granted three but refused one: that they not fall into disbelief all at once, granted; that no enemy from outside them be empowered over them, granted; that they not be punished as earlier nations were punished, granted; but that their violence not be set among themselves, refused. > **""I asked my Lord for my community four qualities; He granted me three and withheld one. I asked Him that my community not disbelieve all at once, and He granted it to me. I asked Him not to empower an enemy from outside them over them, and He granted it to me. I asked Him not to punish them as He punished the nations before them, and He granted it to me. I asked Him not to set their violence among themselves, and He withheld it from me.""**"
"11968","Anas ibn Mālik, raḍiya Allāhu ʿanhu, reports that the Messenger of Allah ﷺ said he asked his Lord for three qualities and was granted two but refused one: that no enemy from outside them be empowered over his community, granted; that they not be destroyed by famine, granted; but that they not be divided into factions, refused. > **""I asked my Lord Almighty for three qualities; He granted me two and withheld one. I asked Him not to empower an enemy from outside them over my community, and He granted it to me. I asked Him not to destroy my community by famine, and He granted it to me. I asked Him not to divide them into factions, and He refused me.""**"
"11969","ʿAlī, raḍiya Allāhu ʿanhu, reports that the Prophet ﷺ said he asked his Lord for three qualities and was granted two but refused one, addressing his Lord concerning hunger, an outside enemy of polytheists, and mutual violence. > **""I asked my Lord Almighty for three qualities; He granted me two and withheld one. I said: O Lord, do not destroy my community by hunger. He said: That is yours. I said: O Lord, do not empower over them an enemy from outside them, meaning the people of polytheism, who would uproot them. He said: That is yours. I said: O Lord, do not set their violence among themselves. And He withheld that from me.""**"
"11970","Jabr ibn ʿAtīk, raḍiya Allāhu ʿanhu, reports that the Messenger of Allah ﷺ asked for three things in the mosque of Banū Muʿāwiyah and was granted two but refused one: that his community not perish by hunger and that no enemy be given dominance over them, both granted; but that their violence not be set among themselves, refused."
"11971","Ibn ʿAbbās, raḍiya Allāhu ʿanhumā, reports that Muḥammad ﷺ asked his Lord not to divide his followers into factions tasting one another's violence, but He refused."
"11972","Nāfiʿ ibn Khālid al-Khuzāʿī reports from his father that the Messenger of Allah ﷺ normally prayed a light yet complete prayer before the people, but one day he prolonged his sitting until some of them signalled to others to be silent, thinking revelation was descending upon him. When he finished, they asked about the length, and he explained that it was a prayer of hope and fear in which he had asked Allah for three things and been granted two but refused one. The narrator adds that when asked whether his father had heard this directly from the Messenger of Allah ﷺ, he affirmed it, swearing he had heard him say he heard it from the Messenger ﷺ as many times as his ten fingers. > **""No, but it was a prayer of hope and fear. I asked Allah therein for three things; He granted me two and withheld one. I asked Him not to punish you with a punishment with which He punished those before you, and I asked Him not to empower an enemy over you all who would exterminate you, and He granted me both. I asked Him not to divide you into factions and make some of you taste the violence of others, and He withheld it from me.""**"
"11973","ʿAbd Allāh ibn Masʿūd, raḍiya Allāhu ʿanhu, reports that the Messenger of Allah ﷺ commanded restraint when his Companions are mentioned, with the fuller narration already cited at length in the Book of Divine Decree. > **""When my Companions are mentioned, withhold.""**"
"11974","Ibn ʿUmar, raḍiya Allāhu ʿanhumā, reports that the Messenger of Allah ﷺ said whoever honours him by honouring his Companions will reach his Pond, while whoever fails to honour him in them will see him on the Day of Resurrection only from afar. > **""Whoever honours me in my Companions will come to my Pond, and whoever does not honour me in my Companions will see me on the Day of Resurrection only from afar.""**"
"11975","Ṭāriq ibn Shihāb reports that sharp words passed between Khālid ibn al-Walīd and Saʿd ibn Abī Waqqāṣ, raḍiya Allāhu ʿanhumā, and that when Khālid was mentioned critically before Saʿd, Saʿd said to stop, for whatever lay between them had never touched their religion."
"11976","ʿUrwah ibn al-Zubayr reports that ʿAlī ibn Abī Ṭālib met al-Zubayr in the market and they exchanged reproaches concerning the affair of ʿUthmān, raḍiya Allāhu ʿanhum, after which ʿAbd Allāh ibn al-Zubayr spoke harshly to ʿAlī. ʿAlī appealed to al-Zubayr about what was being said to him, whereupon al-Zubayr struck his own son until the youth fell."
"11977","Abū Rāshid reports that men of Baṣrah came to ʿUbayd ibn ʿUmayr asking about ʿAlī and ʿUthmān, raḍiya Allāhu ʿanhumā. When they confirmed that this alone had brought them, he recited that they were a nation which had passed away, with for it what it earned and for the questioners what they earn, and that they would not be asked about what those before them had done."
"11978","Ṭāriq ibn Ashyam, raḍiya Allāhu ʿanhu, reports that he heard the Prophet ﷺ say that being slain suffices for his Companions. > **""Killing suffices for my Companions.""**"
"11979","Saʿīd ibn Zayd, raḍiya Allāhu ʿanhu, reports that the Messenger of Allah ﷺ foretold trials after him involving this and that, and that when the hearers said that living to see them would mean ruin, he replied that being slain suffices for his Companions. > **""There will be trials after me, involving this and involving that. Killing suffices for my Companions.""**"
"11980","In another narration of the same report the added words state that people will plunge into those trials with extreme swiftness. > **""People will plunge into them with the swiftest plunge.""**"
"11981","Al-Zubayr ibn al-ʿAwwām, raḍiya Allāhu ʿanhu, reports concerning the verse commanding fear of a trial that will not strike only the wrongdoers among them that during the lifetimes of the Messenger ﷺ, Abū Bakr, ʿUmar, and ʿUthmān they used to discuss it without supposing themselves to be its people, until it finally descended among them."
"11982","ʿAbd al-Raḥmān ibn ʿAwf, raḍiya Allāhu ʿanhu, reports that the Messenger of Allah ﷺ said the Hour would not come until two great factions fought one another while professing a single cause. > **""The Hour will not come until two great factions fight, while their claim is one.""**"
"11983","Umm Ḥabībah, raḍiya Allāhu ʿanhā, reports from the Prophet ﷺ that he saw what his community would face after him, including mutual bloodshed already decreed by Allah as with earlier nations, and that he therefore asked to be granted intercession for them on the Day of Resurrection, which was granted. > **""I saw what my community will face after me and their mutual bloodshed, and that was already decreed by Allah Almighty as it was decreed for the nations before them, so I asked Him to grant me intercession on the Day of Resurrection for them, and He did.""**"
"11984","ʿAbd Allāh ibn Yazīd al-Khaṭmī, raḍiya Allāhu ʿanhu, reports that the Messenger of Allah ﷺ said the punishment of his community lies in its worldly life. > **""The punishment of my community is in its worldly life.""**"
"11985","Abū Hurayrah, raḍiya Allāhu ʿanhu, reports that the Messenger of Allah ﷺ said his community is a mercy shown nation from whom punishment has been lifted, except that their punishment comes from their own hands against themselves. > **""My community is a mercy shown nation; punishment has been lifted from them except their punishment of themselves by their own hands.""**"
"11986","Maʿqil ibn Yasār, raḍiya Allāhu ʿanhu, is reported to have visited the ailing ʿUbayd Allāh ibn Ziyād and reminded him that the appointed time of such people is the Hour, and that the Hour is more calamitous and more bitter."
"11987","Abū Burdah, raḍiya Allāhu ʿanhu, reports that after leaving ʿUbayd Allāh ibn Ziyād inflicting a severe punishment, he sat with a Companion of the Prophet ﷺ who quoted the Messenger of Allah ﷺ as saying that the punishment of this community is by the sword. > **""The punishment of this community is by the sword.""**"
"11988","Abū Burdah reports that when the heads of the Khawārij were brought and he remarked that they were for the Fire, ʿAbd Allāh ibn Yazīd, raḍiya Allāhu ʿanhu, asked how he knew, then quoted the Messenger of Allah ﷺ as saying that Allah had placed the punishment of this community in its worldly life. > **""Allah has placed the punishment of this community in its worldly life.""**"
"11989","Jābir, raḍiya Allāhu ʿanhu, reports that when the Messenger of Allah ﷺ mentioned a coming trial, Abū Bakr asked whether he would live to see it and was told no, ʿUmar asked whether he would live to see it and was told no, and when ʿUthmān asked whether he would live to see it, he was told that through him the people would be tested. > **""No. No. Through you they will be tested.""**"
"11990","ʿUthmān, raḍiya Allāhu ʿanhu, reports that the Messenger of Allah ﷺ told him he would be tested after him and commanded him never to fight back. > **""You will be tested after me, so never fight.""**"
"11991","ʿAbd Allāh ibn Ḥawālah, raḍiya Allāhu ʿanhu, reports that he came to the Messenger of Allah ﷺ while he sat in the shade of a dumah tree dictating to a scribe, that the Prophet ﷺ repeatedly asked whether something should be written for him while he deferred the choice to Allah and His Messenger, that he noticed ʿUmar's name in the document and knew it could only concern something good, and that when he finally agreed the Prophet ﷺ questioned him about trials emerging from the earth's edges and a later trial beside which the earlier one would seem small, then commanded him to follow a man passing by with covered face, whom he seized and turned toward the Prophet ﷺ and who proved to be ʿUthmān ibn ʿAffān. > **""Shall I not have you written down, O Ibn Ḥawālah? O Ibn Ḥawālah, what will you do during trials emerging from the edges of the earth as though they were the horns of cattle? What will you do during another emerging after it, as though the other in it were the leap of a hare? Follow this man. Yes.""**"
"11992","In another narration of Ibn Ḥawālah's report, while travelling with the Prophet ﷺ and camping under a dumah tree with only the Prophet ﷺ and his scribe present, he saw Abū Bakr and ʿUmar named at the beginning of the document. When he asked what he should do during the coming trials, he was commanded to adhere to Shām, though he later could not recall exactly what was said about the final trial, which he would have valued above all else to remember. > **""Adhere to Shām.""**"
"11993","Shaqīq reports that ʿAbd al-Raḥmān ibn ʿAwf met al-Walīd ibn ʿUqbah, who asked why he appeared to have shunned ʿUthmān, Commander of the Faithful. ʿAbd al-Raḥmān told him to convey that he had not fled on the Day of ʿAynayn, meaning Uḥud, had not stayed back from Badr, and had not abandoned the practice of ʿUmar. When this was conveyed, ʿUthmān replied that fleeing at Uḥud was a sin Allah had already pardoned, citing the verse that Satan caused those who turned back when the two hosts met to slip through some of what they had earned but Allah had pardoned them; that his absence from Badr was because he was nursing Ruqayyah, daughter of the Messenger ﷺ, until she died, while the Messenger ﷺ had assigned him a share so that he counted as present; and that neither he nor his critic could truly bear the practice of ʿUmar."
"11994","Saʿīd ibn al-Musayyab reports that ʿUthmān, raḍiya Allāhu ʿanhu, had an announcer who went before him to prayer. One day after prayer the announcer withdrew to a corner, while ʿUthmān wrapped his cloak under his head, reclined, and placed his whip before him. ʿAlī then arrived in waist wrapper and cloak with a staff in hand. Seeing him from afar, the announcer announced ʿAlī's arrival, so ʿUthmān sat up and covered himself. ʿAlī stood at his head and said he had bought the estate of a certain family although the endowment of the Messenger of Allah ﷺ had a right in its water, adding that none but ʿUthmān would have bought it. ʿUthmān rose, sharp words passed between them which the narrator says he will never report until meeting Allah, then ʿAbbās intervened: ʿUthmān raised the whip against ʿAlī and ʿAlī raised the staff against ʿUthmān, while ʿAbbās kept calming them, addressing ʿAlī with the title Commander of the Faithful and ʿUthmān with the reminder that ʿAlī was his cousin, until both fell silent. The next morning both were seen holding one another's hands in conversation."
"11995","Abū ʿAwn al-Anṣārī reports that ʿUthmān ibn ʿAffān said to Ibn Masʿūd, raḍiya Allāhu ʿanhumā, asking whether he would desist from what had reached him about him, and that after Ibn Masʿūd offered some excuse, ʿUthmān said he too had memorised and heard, but not as Ibn Masʿūd had heard, and quoted the Prophet's ﷺ words foretelling a slain commander and an erupting aggressor. ʿUthmān explained that he himself was that slain one, not ʿUmar, for ʿUmar was killed by a single man whereas a crowd would gather against ʿUthmān. > **""A commander will be killed and an aggressor will erupt.""**"
"11996","Sālim ibn Abī al-Jaʿd reports that ʿUthmān, raḍiya Allāhu ʿanhu, summoned several Companions of the Prophet ﷺ, including ʿAmmār ibn Yāsir, and adjured them by Allah whether they knew that the Messenger of Allah ﷺ used to prefer Quraysh over all other people and Banū Hāshim over all Quraysh. When they remained silent, ʿUthmān said that if Paradise's keys were in his hand he would give them to Banū Umayyah until every last one entered. He then sent for Ṭalḥah and al-Zubayr and told them about ʿAmmār: how he once walked with the Messenger of Allah ﷺ hand in hand through the open ground until they came upon ʿAmmār's father, mother, and ʿAmmār himself being tortured; how ʿAmmār's father asked whether it would always be so; and how the Prophet ﷺ answered him. > **""Be patient. O Allah, forgive the family of Yāsir, and it has been done.""**"
"11997","Ibrāhīm ibn ʿAbd al-Raḥmān ibn ʿAwf reports that ʿUthmān, raḍiya Allāhu ʿanhu, declared that if they found in the Book of Allah Almighty that his foot should be placed in fetters, they might do so."
"11998","Aslam, the freedman of ʿUmar, reports that he witnessed ʿUthmān, raḍiya Allāhu ʿanhu, on the day he was besieged at the funeral ground, where the crowd was so dense that a thrown stone would only fall upon someone's head. ʿUthmān looked out from the skylight beside the station of Jibrīl and called out asking whether Ṭalḥah was present, repeating the call three times amid silence until Ṭalḥah ibn ʿUbayd Allāh stood. ʿUthmān expressed surprise that Ṭalḥah could hear his final calls among a crowd yet not answer, then adjured him to recall the day when only the two of them were with the Messenger of Allah ﷺ at a certain place and the Prophet ﷺ addressed Ṭalḥah concerning him. Ṭalḥah affirmed it before Allah, and ʿUthmān withdrew. > **""O Ṭalḥah, there is no prophet except that with him is a companion from his community in Paradise, and this ʿUthmān ibn ʿAffān is my companion in Paradise.""**"
"11999","ʿUbādah ibn Azhar Abū Rawwāʿ reports that he heard ʿUthmān, raḍiya Allāhu ʿanhu, preaching that by Allah they had accompanied the Messenger of Allah ﷺ in travel and residence, that he visited their sick, followed their funerals, raided alongside them, and consoled them in little and much, yet now some people informed ʿUthmān about the Prophet ﷺ although perhaps none of them had ever even seen him. The narration adds that Aʿyan, son of the wife of al-Farazdaq, called out calling him Naʿthal and accusing him of altering, that ʿUthmān asked who he was and was told Aʿyan, that ʿUthmān retorted against him, that the people rushed at Aʿyan, and that a man of Banū Layth held them back until he brought Aʿyan into his house."
"12000","Abū Saʿīd, the freedman of Abū Usayd, reports that when ʿUthmān, raḍiya Allāhu ʿanhu, learned that a delegation of the people of Egypt was approaching, he met them in one of his villages outside Madīnah, disliking that they enter upon him directly. They asked for the Muṣḥaf, so he had it brought and read until reaching the verse commanding the Prophet ﷺ to ask what they thought of declaring part of Allah's provision lawful or unlawful and whether Allah had permitted them or they fabricated against Allah. They challenged him whether the preserve was by Allah's permission or a fabrication against Allah. He told them to proceed, explained the verse's occasion, and said ʿUmar had reserved the preserve for charity camels and that upon succeeding him he had done exactly the same without addition. They questioned him on further matters, some of which he answered in that manner and some of which he acknowledged without excuse, saying he sought Allah's forgiveness. They then demanded that Medinans take no stipends, since the wealth belonged to those who fought for it and to the elders among the Companions of Muḥammad ﷺ. He agreed, they agreed, undertakings were taken and written that they would neither split the staff nor leave the community, and they returned with him to Madīnah. There he praised Allah and declared he had never seen a better delegation, then told cultivators to return to their crops and herdsmen to their milking, for no funds remained with him except for those who fought and for those elders. The people grew angry and called it an Umayyad trick, while the delegation left satisfied. On the road a rider kept approaching, leaving, returning, and reviling them until they seized him. Claiming to be the Commander of the Faithful's messenger to his governor in Egypt, he was searched and found carrying a letter in ʿUthmān's name bearing his seal, ordering their crucifixion, beheading, or amputation. They returned declaring the covenant broken and his blood lawful, came to Madīnah, asked ʿAlī to rise with them against the enemy of Allah, but ʿAlī swore he would not and swore ʿUthmān had never written to them. They looked at one another in confusion while ʿAlī withdrew to a village outside Madīnah. They confronted ʿUthmān, who offered them two alternatives: produce two witnesses, or accept his oath by Allah that he neither wrote, dictated, nor knew of it, since letters are forged in men's names and seals imitated. They besieged him. Looking down one day he greeted them and barely anyone answered even inwardly. He adjured them whether they knew he had bought the well of Rūmah with his own wealth for sweet water yet shared his rope as any Muslim's, and they said yes. He asked why they then barred him from its water until he broke fast on seawater. He adjured them whether they knew he had enlarged the mosque with his own wealth, and they said yes. He asked whether anyone before him had ever been barred from praying in it. He then mentioned further things the Messenger of Allah ﷺ had said to him, including apparently his handwriting of the detailed chapters, until the report spread and men said to hold back from the Commander of the Faithful."
"12001","Al-Mughīrah ibn Shuʿbah, raḍiya Allāhu ʿanhu, reports that he entered upon ʿUthmān, raḍiya Allāhu ʿanhu, while he was besieged and told him that as leader of the people in the plight he saw, he offered him three options to choose from: to go out and fight them with the numbers and strength at his side while upon truth against their falsehood; to open a different door from the one they watched, mount his camels, and join Makkah, where they would never violate him; or to join Shām with its people and Muʿāwiyah among them. ʿUthmān replied that going out to fight would make him the first after the Messenger of Allah ﷺ to succeed him in his community through bloodshed; that going to Makkah was impossible for the reason he had heard from the Prophet ﷺ; and that he would never abandon his migration abode and neighbourhood of the Messenger of Allah ﷺ for Shām. > **""A man of Quraysh will deviate in Makkah, and upon him will be half the punishment of the world.""**"
"12002","Al-Nuʿmān ibn Bashīr, raḍiya Allāhu ʿanhu, reports that when a man of theirs named Khārijah ibn Zayd died and was shrouded, he stood to pray, heard a stir, finished, and found the body moving and speaking. The voice described the strongest and most just as Allah's servant ʿUmar, Commander of the Faithful, strong in body and strong in Allah's cause, and ʿUthmān ibn ʿAffān as the chaste, abstinent Commander who pardoned many sins; said two nights had passed and four remained while the people were divided without order; urged the people to turn to their leader, hear, and obey, announcing the arrival of the Messenger of Allah ﷺ and Ibn Rawāḥah; asked what had become of Zayd ibn Khārijah, his own father; confessed to having seized the well of Arīs unjustly; then the voice quieted."
"12003","ʿAbd Allāh ibn Rāfiʿ reports from his mother that al-Ṣaʿbah bint al-Ḥaḍramī urged her son Ṭalḥah ibn ʿUbayd Allāh, raḍiya Allāhu ʿanhu, to intervene since ʿUthmān's siege had grown severe. Ṭalḥah, then washing one side of his head, did not answer until his mother thrust her hands through her garment, bared her breasts, and adjured him by bearing and nursing him. He then rose, twisted his washed hair into a knot, went out with his mother and the narrator's mother until he reached ʿAlī, who sat beside his house, and asked him to relieve the pressure upon ʿUthmān. ʿAlī tapped three times with a cup in his hand, raised his head, and swore by Allah that he liked nothing for ʿUthmān which ʿUthmān himself disliked."
"12004","Muḥammad ibn Sīrīn reports that Muḥammad ibn Abī Ḥudhayfah ibn ʿUtbah ibn Rabīʿah and Kaʿb boarded a ship at sea, and Muḥammad asked Kaʿb whether he found their ship's sailing described in the Torah. Kaʿb replied that he did not, but that he found therein a most wretched Qurayshī youth who would plunge into strife like a leaping donkey, warning Muḥammad to beware lest he be that man. Ibn Sīrīn adds that people later claimed he indeed proved to be that man."
"12005","Fāṭimah bint ʿAlī and ʿAbd Allāh ibn Jaʿfar, raḍiya Allāhu ʿanhum, report that ʿAlī ibn Abī Ṭālib entered upon ʿAmmār ibn Yāsir while ʿAmmār held al-Ḥasan ibn ʿAlī by the collar. ʿAlī asked what was between him and his nephew, and ʿAmmār said al-Ḥasan claimed ʿUthmān had not disbelieved. ʿAlī asked whether ʿAmmār would affirm what ʿUthmān denied or deny what ʿUthmān affirmed, and ʿAmmār said no, so ʿAlī told him to release his nephew. After al-Ḥasan left, ʿAlī asked ʿAmmār whether he knew that ʿUthmān had affirmed Allah and denied al-Lāt and al-ʿUzzā, and ʿAmmār said yes indeed."
"12006","Waththāb, who had been overtaken by ʿUthmān's emancipation and used to stand before him, reports that ʿUthmān sent him to summon al-Ashtar. Cushions were laid for al-Ashtar and for the Commander of the Faithful. ʿUthmān asked what the people wanted from him, and al-Ashtar said three unavoidable alternatives: that ʿUthmān leave their rule to them and let them choose whom they wished; that he submit himself to retaliation; or that if he refused, they would kill him. ʿUthmān asked whether any alternative was unavoidable, and al-Ashtar confirmed it. ʿUthmān replied that he would never strip off a garment with which he had been clothed, adding that by Allah to be brought forward and beheaded was dearer to him than deposing himself over the community of Muḥammad ﷺ and setting them upon one another. As for retaliation, he swore he knew his two predecessors used to inflict punishment but his own body could not bear retaliation. As for being killed, he swore that if they killed him they would never love one another after him nor ever fight an enemy united. Al-Ashtar then rose and left. After waiting and hoping the people might relent, a wolf like man peered through a door and withdrew, then Muḥammad ibn Abī Bakr arrived with thirteen men until they reached ʿUthmān. He seized ʿUthmān's beard, shook and struck it until the narrator heard his molars clash, taunting him over what Muʿāwiyah, Ibn ʿĀmir, and his letters had availed him. ʿUthmān twice told him to release his beard, calling him nephew. The narrator then saw ʿUthmān call a specific man who rose with a broad arrowhead and stabbed him in the head. Asked what happened next, he said they assisted one another against him until, by Allah, they killed him."
"12007","Nāʾilah bint al-Furāfiṣah, wife of ʿUthmān, raḍiya Allāhu ʿanhumā, reports that the Commander of the Faithful ʿUthmān dozed and woke saying the people would surely kill him. When she replied that it would never be so, God willing, since his subjects were only remonstrating with him, he said he had seen the Prophet ﷺ with Abū Bakr and ʿUmar in a dream, telling him of his fast breaking with them that night. > **""You will break fast with us tonight.""**"
"12008","Kathīr ibn al-Ṣalt reports that ʿUthmān, raḍiya Allāhu ʿanhu, slept on the Friday in which he was killed, and that upon waking he said that were it not for people claiming ʿUthmān was expressing wishes, he would relate a narration. When they urged him to speak, he said he had seen the Messenger of Allah ﷺ in that sleep, telling him of his attending Friday prayer with them. > **""You will attend Friday prayer with us.""**"
"12009","Ibn ʿUmar, raḍiya Allāhu ʿanhumā, reports that ʿUthmān, raḍiya Allāhu ʿanhu, awoke telling the people he had seen the Prophet ﷺ in a dream commanding him to break fast with them, so he began that day fasting and was killed that very day. > **""O ʿUthmān, break fast with us.""**"
"12010","Muslim Abū Saʿīd, freedman of ʿUthmān ibn ʿAffān, reports that ʿUthmān, raḍiya Allāhu ʿanhu, freed twenty owned slaves, called for trousers which he had never worn in ignorance or Islam, fastened them upon himself, and said he had seen the Messenger of Allah ﷺ with Abū Bakr and ʿUmar the previous night, commanding him to be patient. He then called for a Muṣḥaf, spread it before him, and was killed with it still before him. > **""Be patient, for you will break fast with us tomorrow.""**"
"12011","ʿAbd Allāh ibn Muḥammad ibn ʿAqīl reports that ʿUthmān, raḍiya Allāhu ʿanhu, was killed in the year thirty five, and that the strife lasted five years, including the four months of al-Ḥasan."
"12012","Abū al-ʿĀliyah reports that during the ten days of Aḍḥā they remained at the door of ʿUthmān, raḍiya Allāhu ʿanhu."
"12013","Abū Maʿshar reports that ʿUthmān, raḍiya Allāhu ʿanhu, was killed on a Friday, the eighteenth of Dhū al-Ḥijjah in the year thirty five, and that his caliphate lasted twelve years minus twelve days."
"12014","Abū ʿUthmān al-Nahdī reports that ʿUthmān, raḍiya Allāhu ʿanhu, was killed in the middle of the days of Tashrīq."
"12015","ʿAbd Allāh ibn Farrūkh reports that he witnessed ʿUthmān, raḍiya Allāhu ʿanhu, buried in his blood stained clothes without being washed."
"12016","Qatādah reports that al-Zubayr, raḍiya Allāhu ʿanhu, prayed over ʿUthmān and buried him in accordance with ʿUthmān's bequest to him."
"12017","Abū Thawr al-Ḥuddānī of the Murād tribe reports that he came upon Ḥudhayfah and Abū Masʿūd in the Kūfah mosque during the days of al-Jaraʿah, when the people had done to Saʿīd ibn al-ʿĀṣ what they did. Abū Masʿūd was teaching the people and swearing that he did not see the affair receding until blood was shed, while Ḥudhayfah swore it would indeed recede without even a cupping glass of blood, adding that what he knew about it that day he had already known while Muḥammad ﷺ was alive."
"12018","In another narration Abū Thawr al-Ḥuddānī reports that he came upon Ḥudhayfah and Abū Masʿūd in the mosque, with Abū Masʿūd swearing that he had not thought the affair would recede without a cupping glass of blood being shed. Ḥudhayfah replied that he knew it would recede even though such blood would be shed: a man would wake as a believer and sleep as a disbeliever, or sleep as a believer and wake as a disbeliever, his heart inverted and his baser part uppermost, fighting in the trial today while Allah would slay him tomorrow. Abū Masʿūd then said he had spoken the truth, for thus had the Messenger of Allah ﷺ told them about the trial."
"12019","Al-Ḥasan al-Baṣrī reports that he heard Jundub narrating from the Messenger of Allah ﷺ, who asked about the state of people whose leaders entered Paradise while their followers entered the Fire, and who explained, when asked how that could be even if the followers acted like their leaders, that the leaders would enter Paradise for what had preceded from them while the followers would enter the Fire for what they later innovated. > **""How will you be with people whose leaders enter Paradise while their followers enter the Fire? Their leaders will enter Paradise for what preceded from them, while their followers will enter the Fire for what they innovated, even if they acted like their deeds.""**"
"12020","Ḥudhayfah ibn al-Yamān, raḍiya Allāhu ʿanhu, reports from the Messenger of Allah ﷺ that his Companions would commit a lapse which Allah would forgive them on account of their companionship, but that later people emulating them in it would be cast face down by Allah into the Fire. > **""My Companions will commit a lapse which Allah will forgive them on account of their companionship, while later people emulating them will be cast by Allah face down into the Fire.""**"
"12021","Ḥudhayfah, raḍiya Allāhu ʿanhu, reports that he heard the Messenger of Allah ﷺ say that a commander of trial would surely enter Paradise while those with him entered the Fire. > **""A commander of trial will surely enter Paradise, while those with him will surely enter the Fire.""**"
"12022","Abū Bakrah, raḍiya Allāhu ʿanhu, reports that when asked what had prevented him from fighting on the Day of the Camel, he said he had heard the Messenger of Allah ﷺ describe a perishing, unprosperous people emerging under a woman's leadership, while their leader would be in Paradise. > **""A perishing people will emerge who will not prosper, led by a woman, while their leader is in Paradise.""**"
"12023","ʿAlī ibn Abī Ṭālib, raḍiya Allāhu ʿanhu, reports that the Messenger of Allah ﷺ foretold disagreement and dispute over rule after him, and counselled that if one could remain the peaceful one, he should do so. > **""After me there will be disagreement and rule; if you can be the peaceful one, then do so.""**"
"12024","Abū Rāfiʿ, raḍiya Allāhu ʿanhu, reports that the Messenger of Allah ﷺ told ʿAlī ibn Abī Ṭālib of a coming matter between him and ʿĀʾishah, and that when ʿAlī asked whether he himself was meant and whether he would then be the most wretched, he was told yes to the first and no to the second, then commanded to return her to her place of safety when it happened. > **""There will be a matter between you and ʿĀʾishah. Yes. No, but when that happens, return her to her safe abode.""**"
"12025","Qays ibn Abī Ḥāzim reports that when ʿĀʾishah, raḍiya Allāhu ʿanhā, alighted at al-Ḥawʾab and heard the barking of dogs, she said she thought she must return, since she had heard the Messenger of Allah ﷺ address his wives concerning one of them being barked at by the dogs of al-Ḥawʾab. Al-Zubayr told her to proceed instead, hoping Allah might reconcile people through her. > **""Would that I knew which of you will be barked at by the dogs of al-Ḥawʾab.""**"
"12026","Ibn ʿAbbās, raḍiya Allāhu ʿanhumā, reports that the Messenger of Allah ﷺ addressed his wives expressing his concern about one of them riding a much marked camel, emerging to the barking of the dogs of Ḥawʾab, with many slain on her right and left, then escaping after nearly perishing. > **""Would that I knew which of you is the rider of the much marked camel; she will emerge to the barking of the dogs of Ḥawʾab, many will be slain on her right and left, then she will escape after nearly perishing.""**"
"12027","Abū Saʿīd al-Khudrī, raḍiya Allāhu ʿanhu, reports that while they were with several Emigrants and Helpers at the Prophet's ﷺ house, the Prophet ﷺ offered to tell them the best of them. When they agreed, he described their best as those who fulfil trusts and are wholesome, and said Allah loves the discreet and pious. Then ʿAlī ibn Abī Ṭālib passed by, and he declared that truth was with him. > **""Shall I not inform you of the best of you? Your best are those who fulfil trusts and are wholesome; indeed, Allah loves the discreet, the pious. Truth is with him; truth is with him.""**"
"12028","Abū Jarw al-Māzinī reports that he witnessed ʿAlī and al-Zubayr, raḍiya Allāhu ʿanhumā, when they halted facing one another, and that ʿAlī adjured al-Zubayr by Allah whether he had heard the Messenger of Allah ﷺ tell him he would fight ʿAlī while unjust. Al-Zubayr said yes, but that he had remembered it only in that very stand, and then he withdrew. > **""Will you fight him while you are unjust? Yes.""**"
"12029","ʿAlī, raḍiya Allāhu ʿanhu, is reported to have ascended the pulpit one Friday and preached, whereupon al-Ashʿath stood and said the little red lady had prevailed against them through ʿAlī. ʿAlī asked who would excuse him from such useless folk, when one of them lagged turning upon his bedding while others hastened to Allah's remembrance, declaring that expelling them would make him among the wrongdoers. He then swore by Allah that he had heard the Prophet ﷺ foretell that they would surely fight them for religion again just as they had fought them for it at first. > **""They will surely fight you for religion again just as you fought them for it at first.""**"
"12030","ʿAbbād ibn ʿAbd Allāh al-Asadī reports from ʿAlī ibn Abī Ṭālib, raḍiya Allāhu ʿanhu, that one Friday Zayd ibn Ṣūḥān was with him while he preached from a pulpit of bricks with freedmen around him. A man stood and spoke words the narrator could not recall, angering ʿAlī until his face reddened. While thus, al-Ashʿath ibn Qays came stepping over the people and said that the little red lady had prevailed over ʿAlī's cause. Zayd ibn Ṣūḥān struck the narrator's thigh and exclaimed that the Arabs would now reveal what they had concealed. ʿAlī then asked who would excuse him from such useless folk, when one of them turned upon his bed while others hastened to Allah's remembrance, asking what was commanded of him and whether expelling them would make him among the wrongdoers. He then swore by Him Who split the seed and created the soul that he had heard the Messenger of Allah ﷺ foretell it. > **""They will surely fight you for religion again just as you fought them for it at first.""**"
"12031","Muḥammad ibn Ibrāhīm al-Taymī reports that an unnamed pilgrim entered Madīnah and the people came greeting him, until Saʿd, raḍiya Allāhu ʿanhu, entered and greeted. The man remarked that Saʿd had not aided their truth against others' falsehood. Saʿd remained silent awhile, and when asked why he did not speak, he said that when trial and darkness erupted he had told his camel to kneel and stayed back until it cleared. A man objected that he had read Allah's Book from beginning to end without finding any such kneeling command. Saʿd grew angry and said that since the objection was raised, he had heard the Messenger of Allah ﷺ speak concerning ʿAlī in the house of Umm Salamah. They sent to Umm Salamah, raḍiya Allāhu ʿanhā, and she confirmed the Messenger of Allah ﷺ had said it in her house. The man then told Saʿd he had never been more blameworthy in his eyes than now, and when Saʿd asked why, he said that had he heard this from the Prophet ﷺ he would never have ceased serving ʿAlī until death. > **""ʿAlī is with the truth, or the truth is with ʿAlī, wherever he is.""**"
"12032","Zayd ibn Wahb reports that while they were seated around Hudhayfah, may Allah be pleased with him, he asked how they would fare when the people of their Prophet's ﷺ household emerged split into two factions striking one another's faces with the sword, and when they asked him, O Abu ʿAbdillah, could that really happen, one of his companions asked what they should do if they lived to see that time, whereupon he told them to look for the faction that calls to the cause of ʿAli, may Allah be pleased with him, and to join it, for it would be upon guidance."
"12033","Zahdam al-Jarmi reports that during an evening gathering with Ibn ʿAbbas, may Allah be pleased with them both, he told them he would relate something neither secret nor public: that when the affair of that man, meaning ʿUthman, may Allah be pleased with him, reached what it reached, he had advised ʿAli to withdraw, for even if he hid in a burrow he would be sought out and dragged forth, but ʿAli disobeyed him, and he swore by Allah that Muʿawiyah would surely rule over them, since Allah, Blessed and Exalted, says that whoever is killed wrongfully, his heir has been given authority, so let him not transgress in killing, for he is aided, and he warned that Quraysh would surely compel them to follow the custom of Persia and Rome, and that Jews, Christians, and Magians would be trusted over them, so whoever among them on that day held fast to what he recognized as right would be saved, while whoever abandoned it, and they would abandon it, would become like one of the perished nations of old."
"12034","Ibn ʿAbbas, may Allah be pleased with them both, reports that when the companions of ʿAli, may Allah be pleased with him, marched toward Basra and learned that the people of Basra had gathered behind Talhah and al-Zubayr, it weighed heavily on them and misgivings entered their hearts, so ʿAli swore by Him besides whom there is no god that his men would surely prevail over the people of Basra, that Talhah and al-Zubayr would surely be killed, and that there would come out to them from Kufa six thousand five hundred and fifty men, or, as the transmitter al-Ahlaj doubted, five thousand five hundred and fifty men, and Ibn ʿAbbas says this unsettled him, so he resolved to watch the people of Kufa as they arrived and count them, to see whether the matter was something ʿAli had heard or merely a stratagem of war, and when he asked a man of the army, the man stated, without delay, exactly the number ʿAli had stated, and Ibn ʿAbbas added that this was among the matters the Messenger of Allah ﷺ had foretold to ʿAli."
"12035","Qays ibn ʿAdi reports that he heard ʿAmr ibn Thabit on the day of Basra swearing by Allah that the assembled host would surely be routed and would surely turn their backs, whereupon a man of Nakhʿ said, I seek refuge with Allah from any taint, O Abu Yaqzan, against your speaking of what you have no knowledge of, and ʿAmr replied that he would be worse than a camel dragged by its halter between Najd and Tihamah if he spoke of what he had no knowledge of."
"12036","Yazid ibn Muʿawiyah al-Bakkaʾi reports that he was sitting with ʿAbdullah ibn Masʿud and Hudhayfah, may Allah be pleased with them both, when a woman and a man passed by them riding a camel with their faces turned away from each other, and one of the two Companions said to his fellow that this was what they used to talk about, but the other replied that along with that there would be the flashing of swords."
"12037","ʿUmayr ibn Saʿid reports that they were sitting with Ibn Masʿud, may Allah be pleased with him, while Abu Musa was with him, when the governor seized a man, beat him, and mounted him upon a camel, and the people began crying out about the camel, until one man asked, O Abu ʿAbd al-Rahman, is this the camel we used to hear about, and Ibn Masʿud replied by asking where then was the flashing of swords."
"12038","Saʿid ibn Kawz reports that he was with his master on the Day of the Camel when a horseman approached calling out, O Mother of the Believers, and ʿAʾishah, may Allah be pleased with her, told them to ask who he was, and when asked he said he was ʿAmmar ibn Yasir, so she told them to ask what he wanted, and he adjured her by Allah who had sent down the Book upon the Messenger of Allah ﷺ in her house, asking whether she knew that the Messenger of Allah ﷺ had appointed ʿAli as guardian over his family and concerning his family, to which she answered, O Allah, yes, and when he asked what then was her course, she said she sought retribution for the blood of ʿUthman, Commander of the Believers, after which he spoke further and withdrew, and then four horsemen came, one of whom called out, and ʿAʾishah declared by the Lord of the Kaʿbah that it was the son of Abu Talib and told them to ask what he wanted, and when asked he said he was ʿAli ibn Abi Talib, and he put to her the same adjuration about the Book sent down in her house and whether the Messenger of Allah ﷺ had appointed him guardian over his family and concerning his family, to which she again answered, O Allah, yes, and when he asked what then was her course, she again said she sought the blood of ʿUthman, Commander of the Believers, so he told her to show him the killers of ʿUthman, then he turned away and battle was joined, and the narrator adds that he saw Hilal ibn Wakiʿ, chief of Banu Tamim, with a dark servant boy like a serpent fighting before ʿAʾishah while chanting verses about striking them, the flight of ʿAwn and Abu Himat, and the people straying from the path, until, when the narrator glanced again, both Hilal and his boy lay crushed dead."
"12039","Abu Bakrah, may Allah be pleased with him, reports that on the Day of the Camel, when ʿAli, may Allah be pleased with him, saw heads falling severed, he took the hand of al-Husayn, placed it upon his own belly, and asked what good could remain after this."
"12040","Muhammad ibn Qays reports that when the Day of the Camel was mentioned before ʿAʾishah, may Allah be pleased with her, and the people asked whether they meant the Day of the Camel, she said she wished she had remained seated as her fellow wives had remained seated, and that she would have loved to have borne the Messenger of Allah ﷺ more than ten children, each like ʿAbd al-Rahman ibn al-Harith ibn Hisham and like ʿAbdullah ibn al-Zubayr."
"12041","ʿAmir al-Shaʿbi reports that when ʿAli, may Allah be pleased with him, set out for Siffin he left Abu Masʿud in charge of Kufa, and that certain Kufans who had kept hidden from ʿAli emerged after his departure and came to Abu Masʿud saying that Allah had destroyed his enemies and granted the believers victory, to which Abu Masʿud replied that by Allah he did not count the triumph of one of the two parties over the other as victory or well-being, and when they asked what then, he said there should be a truce between the people, and when ʿAli returned and this was reported to him, he told Abu Masʿud to withdraw from his office, and when Abu Masʿud asked why, ʿAli said they had found him lacking understanding, to which Abu Masʿud answered that what understanding remained to him told him the other course was worse."
"12042","ʿAli, may Allah be pleased with him, reports that the Messenger of Allah ﷺ made a covenant with him concerning fighting the oath-breakers, the unjust partisans, and the renegades. > **""Fight the oath-breakers, the unjust, and the renegades.""**"
"12043","In another narration ʿAli, may Allah be pleased with him, reports that he was commanded to fight the oath-breakers, with the rest of the report as before. > **""I was commanded to fight the oath-breakers.""**"
"12044","ʿAbdullah ibn Masʿud, may Allah be pleased with him, reports that ʿAli, may Allah be pleased with him, was commanded to fight the oath-breakers, the unjust partisans, and the renegades. > **""Ali was commanded to fight the oath-breakers, the unjust, and the renegades.""**"
"12045","Abu Saʿid ʿUqaysa reports that he heard ʿAmmar, may Allah be pleased with him, while they were heading for Siffin, saying that the Messenger of Allah ﷺ had commanded fighting the oath-breakers, the unjust partisans, and the renegades. > **""I was commanded to fight the oath-breakers, the unjust, and the renegades.""**"
"12046","Qays ibn Abi Hazim reports that ʿAli, may Allah be pleased with him, said to march forth against the remnant of the confederates and to hasten with him to what Allah and His Messenger had decreed, for his party said Allah and His Messenger had spoken the truth while their opponents said Allah and His Messenger had lied."
"12047","ʿAbdullah ibn ʿAmr ibn al-ʿAs, may Allah be pleased with them both, reports that the Messenger of Allah ﷺ once visited the mother of ʿAbdullah ibn ʿAmr, a woman who treated the Messenger of Allah ﷺ with kindness, and after exchanging greetings she told him her son had cut himself off from the world by forbidding himself sleep, fasting without break, refusing meat, and withholding his family's rights, so the Prophet ﷺ asked where he was, was told he had gone out but would soon return, and told her to detain him upon his return, and when ʿAbdullah came and the Messenger of Allah ﷺ soon returned, he questioned him about these reports, to which ʿAbdullah answered that he sought thereby safety from the day of the greatest terror, food better than meat in Paradise, and wives better than his wife in Paradise, whereupon the Prophet ﷺ reminded him that in the Messenger of Allah there is an excellent example, for he fasts and breaks fast, sleeps and prays by night, eats meat, and renders his family their rights, and that Allah, his body, and his family each have rights over him, and when ʿAbdullah proposed various schemes of fasting, the Prophet ﷺ declined them until ʿAbdullah proposed fasting one day and breaking fast one day, which the Prophet ﷺ approved as the fast of his brother Dawud, and he further warned him of a time when he would remain among worthless people whose covenants would be corrupted, intertwining his fingers to depict their confusion, and instructed him then to hold to what he recognized, leave what he disapproved, attend to his private affair, and leave the people and their public matters, then took his hand, walked with him, placed it in his father's hand, and told him to obey his father, and the narration concludes that on the Day of Siffin his father ordered him to go out and fight, and when ʿAbdullah reminded him of the Prophet's ﷺ covenant, his father adjured him that the last of that covenant was precisely the placing of his hand in his father's with the command to obey him, so ʿAbdullah went out girt with his sword, and after the battle lifted, ʿAmr ibn al-ʿAs and ʿAbdullah ibn ʿAmr each recited verses describing how war had blazed, how the Iraqis had come like spring clouds, how ranks had surged like sea waves, how the day had turned, and how the call to pledge ʿAli had been refused. > **""O Abdullah ibn Amr, you have in the Messenger of Allah an excellent example; obey your father; hold to what you recognize and leave what you disapprove.""**"
"12048","Abu ʿAbd al-Rahman al-Sulami reports that they were present with ʿAli at Siffin, having assigned two men to guard his horse, yet whenever the guards grew heedless ʿAli would spur his horse into the enemy camp and return with his sword dyed in blood, saying, O my companions, excuse me, excuse me, and that during truces men of each camp entered the other, while ʿAmmar ibn Yasir served as a banner for the Companions of Muhammad ﷺ, so that wherever ʿAmmar walked in the valleys of Siffin the Companions followed him, until they reached Hashim ibn ʿUtbah ibn Abi Waqqas, who had planted the standard, and ʿAmmar reproached him for hanging back as a one-eyed coward, upon which Hashim seized the standard reciting verses about the one-eyed man seeking a place for his family, wearied of life, bound either to blunt the enemy or be blunted, and ʿAmmar told him to advance, for Paradise lies beneath the flashing swords and the wide-eyed maidens stand adorned with Muhammad and his party in the highest company, and neither returned until both were slain, and the narrator adds that during a truce he saw four men riding, Muʿawiyah, Abu al-Aʿwar al-Sulami, ʿAmr ibn al-ʿAs and his son, so he spurred his horse between them, placing two on his right and two on his left, and listened as ʿAbdullah ibn ʿAmr said to his father that they had killed that man concerning whom the Messenger of Allah ﷺ had said what he said, and when his father asked which man, he said ʿAmmar ibn Yasir, reminding him of the day of the building of the mosque when they carried one brick at a time while ʿAmmar carried two at a time, and the Prophet ﷺ had declared that the rebellious party would kill him and that he was of the people of Paradise, whereupon Muʿawiyah told him to be silent and accused him of blundering, claiming that they had not killed him but that those who brought him and cast him between their spears had killed him, until it was proclaimed in Muʿawiyah's camp that only those who brought ʿAmmar had killed him. > **""The rebellious party will surely kill him, and he is of the people of Paradise.""**"
"12049","Muhammad ibn ʿAmr ibn Hazm reports that when ʿAmmar ibn Yasir, may Allah be pleased with him, was killed, ʿAmr ibn Hazm went to ʿAmr ibn al-ʿAs and said ʿAmmar had been killed although the Messenger of Allah ﷺ had declared that the rebellious party would kill him, so ʿAmr ibn al-ʿAs rose in alarm repeating remembrance of Allah until he entered upon Muʿawiyah, and when Muʿawiyah asked what troubled him and ʿAmr told him ʿAmmar had been killed, Muʿawiyah asked what of it, and when ʿAmr cited the Prophet's ﷺ declaration, Muʿawiyah accused him of blundering and argued that they had not killed him, but that ʿAli and his companions had killed him by bringing him and casting him between their spears, or, as doubtfully worded, between their swords. > **""The rebellious party will kill him.""**"
"12050","Muhammad ibn ʿUmarah ibn Khuzaymah ibn Thabit reports that his grandfather kept his weapon sheathed until ʿAmmar was killed at Siffin, whereupon he unsheathed his sword, fought until he was killed, and declared that he had heard the Messenger of Allah ﷺ say that the rebellious party would kill ʿAmmar. > **""The rebellious party will kill him.""**"
"12051","It is reported about ʿAmr ibn al-ʿAs, may Allah be pleased with him, that he distributed gifts to some people while giving ʿAmmar ibn Yasir, may Allah be pleased with him, preference, and when questioned about it he said he had heard the Messenger of Allah ﷺ declare that the rebellious party would kill him. > **""The rebellious party will kill him.""**"
"12052","Zayd ibn Wahb reports that ʿAmmar had become embroiled with Quraysh and they with him, so they came upon him in the morning and beat him, whereupon ʿUthman, may Allah be pleased with him, came out with a staff, ascended the minbar, praised Allah, and protested against Quraysh for falling upon a man in the morning and beating him, declaring that he had heard the Messenger of Allah ﷺ say to ʿAmmar that the rebellious party would kill him. > **""The rebellious party will kill you.""**"
"12053","Anas, may Allah be pleased with him, reports that while the Messenger of Allah ﷺ was building the mosque, ʿAmmar ibn Yasir, may Allah be pleased with him, was carrying two stones at a time, whereupon the Prophet ﷺ lamented over Ibn Sumayyah and declared that the rebellious party would kill him. > **""Alas for Ibn Sumayyah; the rebellious party will kill him.""**"
"12054","Ibn ʿUmar, may Allah be pleased with them both, said he grieved over nothing so much as that he had not fought the rebellious party alongside ʿAli, may Allah be pleased with him."
"12055","ʿAmmar ibn Yasir, may Allah be pleased with him, reports that the Messenger of Allah ﷺ struck him on his side and declared it a believing side, that the rebellious party would kill him, and that his last provision would be a draught of milk diluted with water. > **""A believing side; the rebellious party will kill you, and your last provision will be a draught of milk.""**"
"12056","ʿAbdullah ibn Salamah reports that he saw ʿAmmar on the Day of Siffin as a tall, elderly, dark-skinned man holding a spear in a trembling hand, swearing by Him in whose hand his soul is that he had fought under this banner with the Messenger of Allah ﷺ three times and that this was the fourth, and that even if the enemy struck them back to the heights of Hajar he would still know that his own party was upon the truth and that they were upon error."
"12057","ʿAbdullah ibn Salamah reports that when it was said to ʿAmmar, may Allah be pleased with him, that Abu Musa had withdrawn, he swore by Allah that Abu Musa's troops would surely be deserted, his efforts would flee, and his covenant would be broken, and that he saw a people who would strike such a blow as to make the partisans of falsehood doubt, yet even if they fought back to the heights of Hajar he would still know that his companion was upon the truth and that they were upon falsehood."
"12058","Sayyar Abu al-Hakam reports that Banu ʿAbs asked Hudhayfah, may Allah be pleased with him, for his counsel after the killing of ʿUthman, Commander of the Believers, and he commanded them to cling to ʿAmmar, and when they objected that ʿAmmar never separated from ʿAli, he replied that envy destroys the body and that only ʿAmmar's closeness to ʿAli estranged them from him, swearing by Allah that ʿAli was superior to ʿAmmar by as much as is between dust and clouds, while ʿAmmar was surely among the excellent, and he knew well that if they clung to ʿAmmar they would be with ʿAli."
"12059","ʿAbdullah, meaning Ibn Masʿud, may Allah be pleased with him, reports from the Prophet ﷺ that when people differ, Ibn Sumayyah, that is ʿAmmar, will be with the truth. > **""When people differ, Ibn Sumayyah is with the truth.""**"
"12060","Ibn ʿUmar, may Allah be pleased with them both, reports that the Messenger of Allah ﷺ said he had earnestly commended ʿAmmar to them, for he would call them to Paradise while they called him to the Fire. > **""I commend him to you concerning Ammar; he calls them to Paradise and they call him to the Fire.""**"
"12061","Abu al-Bakhtari reports that on the Day of Siffin ʿAmmar, may Allah be pleased with him, asked for a drink of milk, explaining that the Messenger of Allah ﷺ had told him his last drink in this world would be a drink of milk, so milk was brought, he drank it, then advanced and was killed, with the added detail that he looked toward Muʿawiyah's banner and said he had fought its bearer alongside the Messenger of Allah ﷺ. > **""The last drink you shall drink in this world will be a drink of milk.""**"
"12062","Kulthum ibn Jabr reports that while they were at Wasit al-Qasab with ʿAbd al-Aʿla ibn ʿAbdillah ibn ʿAmir, a man called Abu al-Ghadiyah asked for water, was brought a silver-plated vessel, refused to drink from it, and cited the Prophet ﷺ as declaring that they must not return after him into disbelief or error striking one another's necks, and Abu al-Ghadiyah added that when a man reviled someone, he had sworn that if Allah empowered him over him in battle he would act, and that on the Day of Siffin he found the man in armor, noticed a gap where the armor flowed, thrust through it and killed him, only to discover he was ʿAmmar ibn Yasir, whereupon the narrator marvelled at a hand that scrupled to drink from a silvered vessel yet had killed ʿAmmar ibn Yasir. > **""Do not return after me into disbelief or error, striking one another's necks.""**"
"12063","Hanzalah ibn Khuwaylid al-ʿAnbari reports that while he was with Muʿawiyah, may Allah be pleased with him, two men came disputing over the head of ʿAmmar, each claiming to have killed him, so ʿAbdullah ibn ʿAmr told one of them to yield it gladly to his fellow, for he had heard the Messenger of Allah ﷺ declare that the rebellious party would kill him, and when Muʿawiyah asked why he was then with them, ʿAbdullah explained that his father had complained of him to the Messenger of Allah ﷺ, who had commanded him to obey his father as long as he lived and not disobey him, so he was with them but would not fight. > **""The rebellious party will kill him; obey your father as long as he lives and do not disobey him.""**"
"12064","Abu Ghadiyah reports that after ʿAmmar was killed and ʿAmr ibn al-ʿAs was informed, ʿAmr declared that he had heard the Messenger of Allah ﷺ say that his killer and despoiler would be in the Fire, and when it was objected that he himself was fighting him, he replied that the Prophet ﷺ had specified only the killer and the despoiler, with a parallel version stating that two men came to ʿAmr disputing over the blood and spoils of ʿAmmar and were told to leave it, for the killer of ʿAmmar and his despoiler would be in the Fire. > **""The killer of Ammar and his despoiler will be in the Fire.""**"
"12065","Qays ibn ʿAbbad reports that they were with ʿAli, may Allah be pleased with him, who whenever he witnessed a battle, ascended a hill, or descended into a valley would exclaim glory be to Allah, Allah and His Messenger have spoken the truth, so he and a man of Banu Yashkur went to the Commander of the Believers to ask about this saying and whether the Messenger of Allah ﷺ had given him any particular covenant concerning it, and after ʿAli turned away and they pressed him, he swore by Allah that the Messenger of Allah ﷺ had given him no covenant apart from what he gave to the people at large, but that the people had fallen upon ʿUthman and killed him, with others in worse state and deed therein than himself, and that he had then seen himself as the most entitled of them to authority and had seized it, and Allah alone knows whether they were right or wrong."
"12066","ʿUmayr ibn Zawdi reports that ʿAli, may Allah be pleased with him, addressed the people but they interrupted his sermon, so he declared that he was only weakened on the day ʿUthman was killed, and he struck for them the parable of three bulls, black, red, and white, gathered with a lion in a thicket, who held together against the lions whenever one was sought, until the lions told the black and red bulls that the white one disgraced them in their thicket and asked to be left to devour him, since their colour matched his, so the lion pounced and soon killed him, then told the black bull that the red one disgraced them and asked to be left to devour him, since their colours matched, so he pounced and killed him, then told the black bull that now he would devour him, whereupon the black bull asked leave to cry out three times that he was only devoured on the day the white bull was devoured, repeating it thrice, and ʿAli concluded that likewise he was only weakened on the day ʿUthman was killed."
"12067","Al-Tabarani records, through ʿUbaydullah ibn Abi Rafiʿ, the names of those concerned: Usayd ibn Malik Abu ʿAmrah, also called Yasir ibn ʿAmr ibn Mihsan, or Thaʿlabah ibn ʿAmr ibn Mihsan, or ʿAmr ibn Mihsan of Banu Mazin ibn al-Najjar, of whom it is said that Abu ʿAmrah gave ʿAli one hundred thousand dirhams to aid him on the Day of the Camel, and those killed on the Day of Siffin including Jabalah ibn ʿAmr, al-Hajjaj ibn ʿAmr ibn Ghaziyah, who used to cry out in battle, O company of Ansar, do you wish to say to your Lord when you meet Him that you obeyed your chiefs and great ones and they led you astray, along with Hanzalah ibn al-Nuʿman, Khalid ibn Abi Khalid, Khalid ibn Abi Dujanah, Khuwaylid ibn ʿAmr, a veteran of Badr of Banu Salamah, Rabiʿah ibn Qays ibn ʿAdwan, and Rabiʿah ibn ʿAbbad al-Duʾali."
"12068","Muhammad ibn ʿUmarah ibn Khuzaymah ibn Thabit reports that Khuzaymah ibn Thabit, may Allah be pleased with him, fought on the Day of Siffin until he was killed."
"12069","Suwayd ibn Ghafalah reports that he heard Abu Musa al-Ashʿari, may Allah be pleased with him, quoting the Messenger of Allah ﷺ as saying that there would be in this community two misguided arbiters, and misguided is whoever follows them, whereupon the narrator warned Abu Musa to take care not to be one of them. > **""There will be in this community two misguided arbiters; misguided is whoever follows them.""**"
"12070","Abu Maryam reports that he heard ʿAmmar ibn Yasir, may Allah be pleased with him, addressing Abu Musa, asking whether he had not heard the Messenger of Allah ﷺ declare that whoever deliberately lies about him should take his seat in the Fire, then announcing he would question him, and if he spoke truthfully the Companions who confirmed him would not blame him, then adjuring him by Allah whether the Messenger of Allah ﷺ had not singled him out personally by declaring that there would be a trial in his community in which, O Abu Musa, his sleeping would be better than his sitting, his sitting better than his standing, and his standing better than his walking, emphasizing that the Messenger of Allah ﷺ had specified him and not people at large, whereupon Abu Musa departed without answering. > **""There will be a trial in my community; O Abu Musa, in it the one asleep is better than the one seated, the seated better than the standing, and the standing better than the walking.""**"
"12071","In a version of al-Tabarani, ʿAmmar ibn Yasir, may Allah be pleased with him, reports that the Messenger of Allah ﷺ spoke to a certain man, though the chain contains ʿAli ibn Abi Fatimah, that is ʿAli ibn al-Hazawwar, who is abandoned."
"12072","Muhammad ibn al-Dahhak al-Harami reports that ʿAli, may Allah be pleased with him, stood upon the minbar of Kufa when the two arbiters disagreed and said he had forbidden them this arbitration but they had disobeyed him, whereupon a dark-complexioned youth stood and accused him of commanding rather than forbidding it, ruining them, then disowning himself and loading his sin upon them when its outcome displeased him, so ʿAli rebuked him for meddling in such speech, invoked ugliness upon him, reminded him that he had been obscure during unity but had sprouted like a goat's horn in tribulation, then turned to the people praising the station taken by Saʿd ibn Malik and ʿAbdullah ibn ʿUmar, swearing by Allah that if it was a sin it was small and forgiven, and if it was good it was great and gratefully rewarded."
"12073","ʿAbdullah ibn Salam, may Allah be pleased with him, reports that when the people rose over the affair of ʿUthman he addressed them not to kill that elder but to seek his satisfaction, for no community kills its prophet without its affairs remaining disordered until seventy thousand of them shed their blood, and no community kills its caliph without its affairs remaining disordered until forty thousand shed their blood, but they disregarded his words and assaulted ʿUthman, and that he later sat waiting for ʿAli upon the road and asked where he intended to go, and when ʿAli said the land of Iraq, he told him not to go to Iraq but to keep to the minbar of the Messenger of Allah ﷺ, whereupon some of ʿAli's companions rushed at him, but ʿAli told them to leave him, for he was of them, of the household, and that after ʿAli was killed ʿAbdullah told Ibn Maʿqil that this was the head of the fortieth year, that at its close there would be a truce, and that no community kills its prophet but seventy thousand are slain for him, and no community kills its caliph but forty thousand are slain for him."
"12074","Jabir, may Allah be pleased with him, reports that the Messenger of Allah ﷺ said of his son, meaning al-Hasan, may Allah be pleased with him, that he is a chief and that Allah, Mighty and Majestic, will surely reconcile through him between two parties of Muslims. > **""This son of mine is a chief, and Allah will surely reconcile through him between two parties of Muslims.""**"
"12075","Abu Mijlaz reports that ʿAmr and al-Mughirah ibn Shuʿbah said to Muʿawiyah that al-Hasan ibn ʿAli was a man of weak speech with a fixed discourse and view, and that since they knew his speech they could preempt it and leave him speechless, but Muʿawiyah told them not to do so, and when they insisted, ʿAmr ascended the minbar, mentioned ʿAli and disparaged him, then al-Mughirah ibn Shuʿbah ascended, praised Allah, and likewise disparaged ʿAli, after which al-Hasan ibn ʿAli was told to ascend, and he refused to ascend or speak until they pledged that if he spoke truth they would confirm him and if he spoke falsehood they would refute him, and when they pledged, he ascended the minbar, praised Allah, and adjured ʿAmr and al-Mughirah by Allah whether they knew that the Messenger of Allah ﷺ had said, Allah curse the driver and the rider, one of whom was a certain man, to which both answered, O Allah, yes, then adjured Muʿawiyah and al-Mughirah by Allah whether they knew that the Messenger of Allah ﷺ had cursed ʿAmr with a curse for every rhyme he uttered, to which both answered, O Allah, yes, then adjured ʿAmr and Muʿawiyah ibn Abi Sufyan by Allah whether they knew that the Messenger of Allah ﷺ had cursed the folk of this man, to which both answered yes, whereupon al-Hasan praised Allah against whom they had offended, since they had reviled one who was clear of such curses, and continued his discourse. > **""Allah curse the driver and the rider; Allah cursed Amr for every rhyme he uttered and cursed the folk of this man.""**"
"12076","ʿIsa ibn Yazid reports that al-Ashʿath ibn Qays sought permission to enter upon Muʿawiyah at Kufa, was kept waiting awhile, and upon entering found Ibn ʿAbbas and al-Hasan ibn ʿAli with him, so he asked, O Commander of the Believers, did you veil me on account of these two, knowing that their master came to them and filled them with lies, meaning ʿAli, whereupon Ibn ʿAbbas retorted that with ʿAli stood the steeds of al-Ashʿath's grandfather and a thrust at his father, and when al-Ashʿath protested at these words, Muʿawiyah replied that he himself had begun it."
"12077","Shaddad ibn Aws, may Allah be pleased with him, reports that he entered upon Muʿawiyah while he sat with ʿAmr ibn al-ʿAs seated on his bedding, so Shaddad sat between them and asked whether they knew why he sat between them, explaining that he had heard the Messenger of Allah ﷺ declare that when they are seen together they should be separated, for by Allah they come together only upon treachery, and that he had therefore wished to separate them. > **""When you see these two together, separate them, for by Allah they come together only upon treachery.""**"
"12078","Muhammad ibn Sirin reports that when Muʿawiyah resolved to appoint Yazid as successor he wrote to his governor of Madinah to send a deputation of whomsoever he wished, so ʿAmr ibn Hazm al-Ansari came as a deputy and sought permission, and Muʿawiyah's chamberlain announced him, but Muʿawiyah asked what need they had of him and, told that he sought his favour, ordered the chamberlain to have him write whatever he wished and be granted it without audience, yet ʿAmr protested that having come to the Commander of the Believers' door he would be veiled from him, insisting on meeting and speaking with him, so Muʿawiyah appointed a morning after dawn prayer on such-and-such a day, and when he had prayed dawn he ordered a couch placed in his portico, cleared the people away so that none remained but a chair set for ʿAmr, admitted him, received his greeting, and asked his need, whereupon ʿAmr praised Allah and declared that Muʿawiyah's son had become of central lineage in Quraysh, needing neither kingship nor any good thing, and reminded him that he had heard the Messenger of Allah ﷺ declare that Allah never entrusts a servant with subjects but will question him over them how he dealt with them, adjuring Muʿawiyah by Allah concerning the community of Muhammad ﷺ in whom he would appoint over them, at which Muʿawiyah was seized with trembling and heavy breathing on a cold morning, wiping sweat from his face thrice before recovering, praising Allah, and answering that ʿAmr was a sincere adviser who had spoken his view to its utmost, but that none remained except his own son and their sons, and his son was worthier than theirs, then asked again his need, and ʿAmr said he had none, while his brother remarked they had travelled from Madinah only for these words, and Muʿawiyah ordered gifts for them, with a like share for ʿAmr. > **""Allah never entrusts a servant with subjects but He will question him over them how he dealt with them.""**"
"12079","Al-Haytham ibn ʿAdi reports that Sulayman ibn Surad died in the year sixty-five, while Muhammad ibn ʿAli, meaning Ibn al-Madini Fustuqah, adds that it reached him that Sulayman ibn Surad al-Khuzaʿi and al-Musayyab ibn Najabah al-Fazari set out with four thousand men, encamped at al-Nukhaylah seeking vengeance for the blood of al-Husayn ibn ʿAli under the command of Sulayman ibn Surad at the opening of Rabiʿ al-Akhir in the year sixty-five, then marched against ʿUbaydullah ibn Ziyad, met his vanguard, fought, and both Sulayman ibn Surad and al-Musayyab were killed at the opening of Rabiʿ al-Akhir."
"12080","Muhammad ibn Saʿid, meaning Ibn Rummanah, reports that when death approached Muʿawiyah he told Yazid ibn Muʿawiyah that he had smoothed the lands and peoples for him and feared for him only the people of the Hijaz, so if any suspicion arose from them he should send Muslim ibn ʿUqbah al-Murri against them, for he had tried him repeatedly and found none like him in obedience and counsel, and that when the defiance of Ibn al-Zubayr and his call to himself reached Yazid, he summoned Muslim ibn ʿUqbah al-Murri, who suffered from paralysis, reminded him of the Commander of the Believers' covenant in his illness to send him if any suspicion arose from the Hijaz, and was answered that he was as the Commander of the Believers supposed, so Yazid should appoint him and mobilize the armies, and Muslim then came to Madinah, halted there three days, summoned them to pledge Yazid as owned bondsmen in obedience to Allah and in disobedience, and they complied except one Qurashi man born of a slave mother, who refused except in obedience to Allah, so Muslim refused to accept it from him and killed him, whereupon the man's mother swore that if Allah empowered her over Muslim, alive or dead, she would burn him with fire, and when Muslim left Madinah his illness worsened and he died, so the Qurashi's mother went with her slaves to Muslim's grave, ordered it dug from the head side, and when they reached him they found a serpent coiled about his neck seizing the tip of his nose and sucking it, and though the people shrank back and told her Allah had sufficed her against his evil, she insisted on fulfilling her vow to Allah, ordered digging from the feet, and they found the serpent trailing its tail about his legs, so she withdrew, prayed two cycles, supplicated that if her anger at Muslim ibn ʿUqbah was for Allah's sake He should let her at him, then took a stick to the serpent's tail until it slipped from behind his head and left the grave, after which she ordered him taken out and burned with fire."
"12081","Abu Harun al-ʿAbdi reports that he saw Abu Saʿid al-Khudri, may Allah be pleased with him, with his beard plucked out, and when he asked whether he toyed with his beard, Abu Saʿid answered no, but that this was what he had suffered from the oppressors of Syria, who entered upon him in the days of al-Harrah, seized whatever furnishings were in the house, and when a second party found nothing and grieved to leave empty-handed, they ordered the elder laid down, laid him down, and each man tore a lock from his beard."
"12082","Iyad ibn al-Walid reports that ʿAbdullah ibn al-Zubayr wrote to Ibn ʿAbbas concerning allegiance but Ibn ʿAbbas refused to pledge him, so Yazid ibn Muʿawiyah supposed the refusal was on his own account and wrote to Ibn ʿAbbas that the heretic Ibn al-Zubayr had invited him to his allegiance to make him a helper in falsehood and a partner in sin, but that Ibn ʿAbbas had refused out of recognition of the rights of Yazid's household, for which Allah would reward him as He rewards those who join kinship and fulfil covenants, and that whatever else he forgot he would never forget Ibn ʿAbbas's kindness, kinship, handsome gift, obedience, honour, and kinship to the Messenger of Allah ﷺ, urging him to dissuade his people and visitors from distant lands whom Ibn al-Zubayr had bewitched with his tongue and embellished speech, since they obeyed Ibn ʿAbbas more readily than that heretical, divisive renegade, and Ibn ʿAbbas replied that his letter had reached him with its claim that Ibn al-Zubayr's invitation had been refused out of recognition of Yazid's right, but even were it so he sought no favour thereby and Allah knew his intention, and as for urging the people toward Yazid and deterring them from Ibn al-Zubayr, never would there be joy therein, but dust in Yazid's mouth and ruin upon him, a lonely outcast deluded by his soul, lost and accursed, and as for hastening favour and kinship, Yazid should withhold his favour since Ibn ʿAbbas would withhold his affection and support, for whatever Yazid gave from what was in his hands was little while he withheld the broad and ample, and how could he forget Yazid's killing of Husayn and the youths of Banu ʿAbd al-Muttalib, lamps of darkness and stars of battalions, left by his horses at his command fallen in one plain, shrouded in blood, stripped in the open, neither shrouded nor pillowed, swept by winds, preyed upon by wolves and limping hyenas, until Allah brought a people innocent of their blood who shrouded and buried them, and by them and by Ibn ʿAbbas himself Allah had favoured Yazid so that he sat in his present seat, and whatever else he forgot he would never forget Yazid's empowering over them the claimant son of a claimant, born of an adulterous profligate woman, distant in kinship, base in father and mother, through whose claimed paternity Yazid's father earned disgrace, sin, abasement, and shame in this world and the next, since the Messenger of Allah ﷺ had declared that the child belongs to the bed and the adulterer gets the stone, whereas Yazid's father claimed the child for other than the bed, and had killed the prophetic practice in ignorance while reviving misleading innovations deliberately, nor would he forget Yazid's driving of Husayn from the sanctuary of the Messenger of Allah ﷺ to the sanctuary of Allah, sending men against him and instigating them, pursuing him until he was driven from Makkah to Kufa with horses and troops roaring at him like lions, out of enmity toward Allah, His Messenger, and his household, then writing to Ibn Marjanah to confront him with cavalry, men, spears, and swords, then ordering his prosecution without delay until he and the youths of Banu ʿAbd al-Muttalib of the household, whom Allah had purified, were killed, not like Yazid's coarse forefathers, and Husayn had been the mightiest of the valley's people of old and of recent times, who defiled neither sanctuary by settling nor by fighting therein, but refused to be the one to violate Allah's sanctuary, the sanctuary of His Messenger ﷺ, and the inviolable House, while Husayn had sought truce and asked to return, but they exploited his few helpers and the extermination of his household as though slaying a household of Turks or Kabul, and how could Ibn ʿAbbas befriend Yazid or grant him support while Yazid had killed his father's sons with his sword dripping from his own blood while demanding his vengeance, for Allah willing his blood would not go unavenged, and if they were preceded therein, prophets and the families of prophets had been killed before and their blood sought, with Allah as the tryst and sufficient as avenger for the oppressed, and most wondrous of all was Yazid's carrying of the daughters of ʿAbd al-Muttalib and their little children to Syria to display them as conquered and humiliated, though by them and by Ibn ʿAbbas Allah had favoured Yazid and his parents from captivity, and Yazid might pass mornings and evenings safe from Ibn ʿAbbas's hand but his tongue, pen, censure, and resolve would wound him grievously, and disputation should not delude him, for Allah would grant him only brief respite after killing the progeny of the Messenger of Allah ﷺ before seizing him painfully and expelling him from the world sinful and blameworthy, so let him live as he wished, for his deeds had already destroyed him with Allah, and when Yazid read the letter he remarked that Ibn ʿAbbas had long been bent upon evil. > **""The child belongs to the bed, and the adulterer gets the stone.""**"
"12083","ʿUrwah ibn al-Zubayr reports that when Muʿawiyah died, ʿAbdullah ibn al-Zubayr withheld obedience from Yazid ibn Muʿawiyah and openly reviled him, and when this reached Yazid he swore Ibn al-Zubayr would only be brought to him in fetters or he would send against him, so it was suggested to Ibn al-Zubayr that silver fetters be made for him to wear beneath his robe to fulfil the oath, since reconciliation befitted him, but he prayed Allah would not fulfil Yazid's oath and recited that he would never yield for other than truth until stone softened to the grinder's tooth, declaring by Allah that a sword-stroke in honour was dearer to him than a whip-stroke in disgrace, then he called the people to himself and openly opposed Yazid ibn Muʿawiyah, so Yazid dispatched Muslim ibn ʿUqbah al-Murri with the Syrian army, ordering him to fight the people of Madinah and then march to Makkah, and Muslim entered Madinah while the remnants of the Companions of the Messenger of Allah ﷺ fled from him, wrought havoc and excessive killing, then departed, and dying on the way appointed Husayn ibn Numayr al-Kindi, warning him as son of a donkey's packsaddle to beware Qurashi deceits and to deal with them only by arms and then by reaping, and Husayn marched to Makkah, fought Ibn al-Zubayr for days while Ibn al-Zubayr pitched a tent in the mosque where women watered and nursed the wounded, fed the hungry, and sheltered the injured, until Husayn complained that a lion kept emerging from that tent as from its den and asked who would suffice him, and a Syrian volunteered, and when night fell placed a torch at his spear-tip, spurred his horse, stabbed the tent so it blazed, while the Kaʿbah that day was draped in carpets with striped cloth above, so the wind carried the flames to the Kaʿbah until it burned, consuming that day the ram's horns with which Ishaq had been ransomed, and when news of Yazid ibn Muʿawiyah's death reached Husayn he fled, and after Yazid died Marwan ibn al-Hakam called the people to himself and was answered by Hims, Jordan, and Palestine, so Ibn al-Zubayr sent al-Dahhak ibn Qays al-Fihri with one hundred thousand men, and they met at Marj Rahit while Marwan stood with five thousand of Banu Umayyah, their clients and Syrian followers, and Marwan told his client Kidah to charge whichever flank he wished, and when Kidah balked at their numbers Marwan told him they were pressed men and hirelings, slaves of gold and silver, whom bright spear-thrusts would make to destroy themselves, so he charged, routed them, killed al-Dahhak ibn Qays, and shattered the army, concerning which Zufar recited verses on the carnage of Rahit left for Marwan, on war's persistence, on pastures regrowing while rancours remain, and on Bahdal's house living while Ibn al-Zubayr is slain, swearing by Allah's House they would not kill him before a bright fateful day and flashing swords like sunlight, and then Marwan died, ʿAbd al-Malik called the people to himself, the Syrians answered, and he asked from the minbar who would deal with Ibn al-Zubayr, and al-Hajjaj thrice volunteered, saying he had dreamt he stripped Ibn al-Zubayr's robe and wore it, so ʿAbd al-Malik appointed him over the army to Makkah, and al-Hajjaj fought Ibn al-Zubayr there, while Ibn al-Zubayr urged the Makkans to hold the two mountains, for they would remain well and honoured so long as the enemy did not dominate them, but soon al-Hajjaj and his men dominated Abu Qubays, mounted the catapult upon it, and pelted Ibn al-Zubayr and his men in the mosque, and on the morning Ibn al-Zubayr was killed he entered upon his mother Asmaʾ daughter of Abu Bakr, then a hundred years old with neither tooth lost nor sight impaired, and when she asked how his party fared and he told her, he laughed and said in death there is rest, but she replied, O my son, do you wish it for me, for she would not die until she attained one of his two ends, either his rule to cool her eyes or his martyrdom to commend him to Allah, and as he bade her farewell she warned him never to surrender any of his religion for fear of killing, and he left her, entered the mosque, set two door-leaves by the Black Stone to shield himself from the catapult, and when a comer suggested opening the Kaʿbah's door for him to ascend, he answered that he guarded his brother against everything but his own appointed term, and asked whether the Kaʿbah had sanctity this place lacked, for by Allah if they found them clinging to the Kaʿbah's curtains they would kill them, and when asked about treating for peace he asked whether this was an hour of peace, for by Allah if found therein they would all be slaughtered, reciting that he would neither buy life with disgrace nor climb a ladder from fear of death, but would meet fates like an unswerving arrow, then he exhorted the Zubayrids to sheath each his sword as he guards his face and not let it break so he must fend with his hand like a woman, swearing he had never met an assault but in the foremost rank nor felt a wound but the touch of medicine, and while thus engaged men entered through the Banu Jumah gate including a dark man, and told they were Himsis he charged them with two swords, struck the dark man first severing his leg, and when the man cursed him as son of an adulteress he answered, silence, son of Ham, is Asmaʾ an adulteress, then drove them from the mosque, and likewise charged Jordanians entering through the Banu Sahm gate reciting that he had never known a torrent-like raid whose dust clears not till night, driving them out, and charged Makhzum-gate entrants declaring one adversary alone would suffice him, while his helpers atop the mosque pelted the enemy with bricks, until a brick struck his crown, split his head, and he stood reciting that their wounds bleed not on their heels but drip upon their feet, then fell, his two clients crouched over him declaring the slave guards his master and is guarded, and then his head was severed and carried away."
"12084","Ibn Sirin reports that Ibn al-Zubayr said everything Kaʿb had foretold them had befallen him as foretold, except his saying that a youth of Thaqif would kill him, though that one's head, meaning al-Mukhtar's, lay before him, while Ibn Sirin remarked that Ibn al-Zubayr did not perceive that Abu Muhammad, meaning al-Hajjaj, had been kept in store for him."
"12085","Ishaq ibn Abi Ishaq reports that he was present at the killing of Ibn al-Zubayr on the day he was killed in the Sacred Mosque, when troops kept entering through the mosque gates and every party that entered through any gate was charged single-handedly by Ibn al-Zubayr until he expelled them, and while thus engaged a parapet from the mosque's parapets fell upon his head and felled him, while he recited verses in which Asmaʾ says weep for me not, for nothing remains but my lineage and my religion and a sharp sword my right hand wielded."
"12086","Abu Nawfal ibn Abi ʿAqrab al-ʿAranji reports that al-Hajjaj crucified Ibn al-Zubayr upon the pass of Madinah to display him to Quraysh, and after the crowds dispersed they passed by without stopping until ʿAbdullah ibn ʿUmar passed, stopped, greeted him thrice as Abu Khubayb, thrice reminded him that he had forbidden him this course, and bore witness that he had been fasting, praying by night, and joining kinship, and when al-Hajjaj learned of ʿAbdullah ibn ʿUmar's stand he had the body taken down and cast among Jewish graves, then summoned Asmaʾ daughter of Abu Bakr, though her sight had gone, and when she refused he threatened to send one to drag her by her forelocks, to which she swore by Allah she would not come until he sent one to drag her thus, and when his messenger reported this he demanded his sandals, rose in fury to her, and asked how she saw Allah had dealt with His enemy, so she answered that he had ruined Ibn al-Zubayr's world while Ibn al-Zubayr had ruined his hereafter, and as for taunting him as son of the woman of two sashes, indeed she had owned two sashes, one with which she covered the Messenger of Allah's ﷺ food from ants and another indispensable to women, and she had heard the Messenger of Allah ﷺ declare that among Thaqif there would be a destroyer and a liar, and as for the liar they had seen him, while as for the destroyer, he was al-Hajjaj, whereupon he departed. > **""Among Thaqif there will be a destroyer and a liar.""**"
"12087","Abu al-Muhayyah, meaning al-Mukhtar, reports from his father that he arrived in Makkah three days after Ibn al-Zubayr had been crucified, or killed, and that his mother Asmaʾ daughter of Abu Bakr addressed al-Hajjaj asking whether it was not time for that rider to descend, and when al-Hajjaj asked whether she meant the hypocrite, she swore by Allah he was no hypocrite and had indeed been fasting and praying by night, so he told her to be silent as a doting old woman, but she denied dotage ever since hearing the Messenger of Allah ﷺ declare that there would emerge from Thaqif a liar and a destroyer, explaining that as for the liar they had seen him, meaning al-Mukhtar, while as for the destroyer, it was al-Hajjaj, with Abu Bakr ibn Abi Shaybah adding in his version that al-Hajjaj retorted in his report that he was the destroyer of hypocrites. > **""There will emerge from Thaqif a liar and a destroyer.""**"
"12088","Al-Qasim ibn Muhammad reports that Asmaʾ daughter of Abu Bakr came with her slave girls, though her sight had gone, asking where al-Hajjaj was, and when told he was not there, she instructed them to order him to release those bones, for she had heard the Messenger of Allah ﷺ forbid mutilation, and to inform him when he came that she had heard the Messenger of Allah ﷺ declare that there would emerge among Thaqif a liar and a destroyer. > **""There will emerge among Thaqif a liar and a destroyer.""**"
"12089","ʿAqil ibn Khalid reports that his father was with al-Hajjaj when Ibn al-Zubayr was killed, and al-Hajjaj sent him to Asmaʾ daughter of Abu Bakr telling her through him to separate her own wealth from the wealth of ʿAbdullah ibn al-Zubayr, whereupon she answered, do it, O son of Asmaʾ, for she had heard the Messenger of Allah ﷺ declare that there would emerge from this clan of Thaqif two men, one a liar and the other a destroyer, and as for the liar they had known him, nor did she deem the other but the destroyer, and when her answer was conveyed al-Hajjaj did not dislike it. > **""There will emerge from this clan of Thaqif two men, one a liar and the other a destroyer.""**"
"12090","Abu Maʿshar reports that when Muʿawiyah ibn Yazid died, all the Syrians pledged Ibn al-Zubayr except the people of Jordan, and when the chiefs of Banu Umayyah and Syrian notables, among them Rawh ibn Zinbaʿ al-Judhami, saw this, they asked one another whether kingship that had lain among them, the Syrians, should pass to the Hijazis, declaring they would not accept it."
"12091","ʿAbd al-Rahman ibn ʿAwf, may Allah be pleased with him, reports that the Messenger of Allah ﷺ said the adornment of the world would be lifted in the year one hundred and twenty-five. > **""The adornment of the world will be lifted in the year one hundred and twenty-five.""**"
"12092","Al-Mustawrid ibn Shaddad, may Allah be pleased with him, reports that he heard the Messenger of Allah ﷺ say that every community has a term, that his community's term is one hundred years, and that when one hundred years pass over his community, what Allah, Mighty and Majestic, promised it will come to it. > **""Every community has a term, and my community's term is one hundred; when one hundred years pass over my community, what Allah promised it will come to it.""**"
"12093","In another version with al-Tabarani, al-Mustawrid, may Allah be pleased with him, reports that he heard the Messenger of Allah ﷺ say that every community has a term and his community's term is one hundred years, and when one hundred years pass over his community what Allah, Mighty and Majestic, promised it will come to it, with Ibn Lahiʿah explaining that he meant the abundance of tribulations. > **""Every community has a term, and my community's term is one hundred years.""**"
"12094","Thawban, may Allah be pleased with him, reports that the Messenger of Allah ﷺ said that all they are promised lies within one hundred years. > **""All that you are promised lies within one hundred years.""**"
"12095","Anas ibn Malik, may Allah be pleased with him, reports that a man renowned for harming the enemy and striving was mentioned to the Messenger of Allah ﷺ, who said he did not know him, and when his description was repeated the Prophet ﷺ still said he did not know him, until the man appeared and they identified him, whereupon the Prophet ﷺ declared he had never known him, that this was the first horn he had seen in his community, and that there was in him a dark touch of Satan, and when the man drew near and greeted, the Prophet ﷺ returned his greeting and adjured him by Allah whether, as he approached them, his soul had whispered that none among the people was better than himself, to which the man answered, O Allah, yes, so he entered the mosque and prayed, and the Messenger of Allah ﷺ told Abu Bakr to rise and kill him, but Abu Bakr entered, found him standing in prayer, deemed prayer inviolable, and returned to seek further leave, asking whether he should still kill him, and the Prophet ﷺ told him he was not his slayer and sent ʿUmar instead, but ʿUmar entered, found him prostrate, waited long, deemed prostration inviolable, and likewise returned to seek leave, and the Prophet ﷺ told him he too was not his slayer and commanded ʿAli, saying he was his slayer if he found him, but ʿAli entered to find he had already left the mosque and returned empty-handed, whereupon the Messenger of Allah ﷺ declared that had he been killed no two men of his community would have disputed until the Dajjal emerged, and then the Messenger of Allah ﷺ discoursed on the nations, declaring that the community of Musa split into seventy-one sects, seventy in the Fire and one in Paradise, that the community of ʿIsa split into seventy-two sects, seventy-one in the Fire and one in Paradise, and that his own community would exceed both sects together by one, with seventy-two in the Fire and one in Paradise, and when asked who they were he answered, the united congregations, while Yaʿqub ibn Zayd adds that ʿAli ibn Abi Talib, whenever he narrated this report from the Messenger of Allah ﷺ, would recite concerning the people of Musa that among the people of Musa there is a community guiding by truth and judging thereby, concerning the community of ʿIsa that had the People of the Book believed and feared Allah He would have expiated their sins and admitted them to blissful Gardens, and concerning their own community that among those He created there is a community guiding by truth and judging thereby. > **""Had he been killed, no two men of my community would have disputed until the Dajjal emerges; my community will divide into seventy-three sects, seventy-two in the Fire and one in Paradise: the united congregation.""**"
"12096","Abu Umamah, may Allah be pleased with him, reports that he heard the Messenger of Allah ﷺ say that the Children of Israʾil split into seventy-one sects, the Christians into seventy-two sects, and his community would exceed them by one sect, all of them in the Fire except the great mass. > **""The Children of Israil split into seventy-one sects, the Christians into seventy-two, and my community will exceed them by one; all are in the Fire except the great mass.""**"
"12097","Saʿd, meaning Ibn Abi Waqqas, may Allah be pleased with him, reports that the Messenger of Allah ﷺ said the Children of Israʾil split into seventy-one creeds, and nights and days would not pass before his community split into the like thereof. > **""The Children of Israil split into seventy-one creeds, and my community will split into the like thereof.""**"
"12098","Ibn ʿUmar, may Allah be pleased with them both, reports that he heard the Messenger of Allah ﷺ say that in his community there would be more than seventy callers, each calling to the Fire, whose fathers, mothers, and tribes he could name if he wished. > **""In my community there will be more than seventy callers, each calling to the Fire.""**"
"12099","Abu al-Dardaʾ, Abu Umamah, Wathilah ibn al-Asqaʿ, and Anas ibn Malik, may Allah be pleased with them, report that the Messenger of Allah ﷺ once came out to them while they disputed over some religious matter, grew angrier than ever before, rebuked them, and warned them gently as the community of Muhammad, for those before them perished through such disputation, commanding them again and again to abandon quarrelling for its scant good, since the believer does not quarrel, the quarreller's loss only grows, mere persistence in quarrelling suffices as sin, he would not intercede on the Day of Resurrection for the quarreller, he guarantees three houses in Paradise, in its meadows, its midst, and its heights, for whoever abandons quarrelling though truthful, his Lord first forbade him quarrelling after idol-worship along with wine-drinking, Satan has despaired of being worshipped but contents himself with incitement among them, which is quarrelling, and the Children of Israʾil split into seventy-one sects and the Christians into seventy-two, all upon error except the great mass, and when asked who the great mass were he answered those upon what he and his Companions were upon, who do not quarrel over Allah's religion and do not declare any monotheist an unbeliever for a forgiven sin, then declared that Islam began strange and would return strange, and when asked who the strangers were he answered those who set things right when people corrupt them, who do not quarrel over Allah's religion, and who do not declare any monotheist an unbeliever for a sin. > **""Abandon quarrelling, for the believer does not quarrel; whoever abandons it though truthful, I guarantee him three houses in Paradise; Islam began strange and will return strange.""**"
"12100","ʿAmr ibn ʿAwf reports that while they sat around the Messenger of Allah ﷺ in a Madinan mosque, Jibril, peace be upon him, came with revelation, the Prophet ﷺ wrapped himself in his cloak, tarried long until it was lifted from him, then uncovered his cloak dripping with heavy sweat while clutching something, and asked which of them knew what emerges from the palm tree, to which they answered, with their fathers and mothers as ransom, that as owners of palms they knew all its yield, so he opened his hand to reveal date-stones, asked what they were and the stones of what year, to which they answered stones of that year, and he confirmed them, then declared that Jibril, peace be upon him, had come to inspect their religion, and that they would surely follow the ways of those before them sole to sole, adopting their practices span for span, cubit for cubit, and fathom for fathom, until were they to enter a lizard's hole they would enter it too, adding that the Children of Israʾil split over Musa, peace be upon him, into seventy sects, all astray save one, Islam and its congregation, then split over ʿIsa, peace be upon him, into seventy-one sects, all astray save one, Islam and its congregation, and that they would become seventy-two sects, all in the Fire save one, Islam and its congregation. > **""You will surely follow the ways of those before you sole to sole, until were they to enter a lizard's hole you would enter it; all sects are in the Fire save one: Islam and its congregation.""**"
"12101","Ibn Masʿud, may Allah be pleased with him, reports that the Messenger of Allah ﷺ entered and called him thrice, to each of which he answered at your service, O Messenger of Allah, then asked whether he knew which people are best, and when he deferred to Allah and His Messenger, explained that the best people are the best in deeds when they understand their religion, then called him again, asked whether he knew which people are most knowledgeable, and when he again deferred, explained that the most knowledgeable are the keenest-sighted upon truth when people differ, even if deficient in deeds and even if crawling upon his buttocks, and that those before him split into seventy-two sects, of which three were saved and the rest perished: one party that confronted kings and fought them over their religion and the religion of ʿIsa son of Maryam until they were seized, killed, and sawn with saws, another party lacking strength to confront kings or to dwell among them calling them to Allah and the religion of ʿIsa son of Maryam, so they roamed the lands and embraced monastic life, namely those of whom Allah, Mighty and Majestic, said they innovated monasticism which was not prescribed for them save seeking Allah's pleasure, and the Prophet ﷺ added that whoever believed in him, confirmed him, and followed him had observed it duly, while whoever did not follow him were the perishing. > **""The best people are the best in deeds when they understand their religion; the most knowledgeable are the keenest-sighted upon truth when people differ.""**"
"12102","In another version the Prophet ﷺ declared that one party remained among kings and tyrants calling to the religion of ʿIsa, so they were seized, killed with saws, and burned with fires, yet endured until they met Allah, with the remainder as before. > **""A party remained among kings and tyrants calling to the religion of Isa, so they were killed with saws and burned with fires, yet endured until they met Allah.""**"
"12103","Sahl ibn Saʿd al-Ansari, may Allah be pleased with him, reports from the Prophet ﷺ that by Him in whose hand his soul is they would surely ride the ways of those before them measure for measure, with the added wording that were they to enter a lizard's hole they would follow them, and when asked whether he meant the Jews and Christians he answered who else but the Jews and Christians. > **""By Him in whose hand my soul is, you will surely ride the ways of those before you measure for measure, even were they to enter a lizard's hole.""**"
"12104","Shaddad ibn Aws, may Allah be pleased with him, reports from the report of the Messenger of Allah ﷺ that the worst of this community would surely adopt the ways of the former Scripture folk who passed before them, arrow-feather to arrow-feather. > **""The worst of this community will surely adopt the ways of the former Scripture folk who passed before them, feather to feather.""**"
"12105","Ibn ʿAbbas, may Allah be pleased with them both, reports that the Messenger of Allah ﷺ said they would surely ride the ways of those before them span for span, cubit for cubit, and fathom for fathom, until were any of them to enter a lizard's hole they would enter it, and were any of them to consort with his own mother they would do likewise. > **""You will surely ride the ways of those before you span for span, cubit for cubit, and fathom for fathom, until were any of them to enter a lizard's hole you would enter it.""**"
"12106","ʿAbdullah, meaning Ibn Masʿud, may Allah be pleased with him, reports that the Messenger of Allah ﷺ said they are the most resembling of nations to the Children of Israʾil and would surely ride their path feather to feather, until there would be nothing among them but its like would be among this community, even to a woman passed by the folk while one of them rises to her, consorts with her, then returns to his companions laughing to them while they laugh to him. > **""You are the most resembling of nations to the Children of Israil; you will surely ride their path feather to feather.""**"
"12107","Al-Mustawrid ibn Shaddād, raḍiya Allāhu ʿanhu, reports that the Messenger of Allah ﷺ spoke concerning this Ummah and its relation to the practices of earlier peoples. > **""This Ummah will not abandon anything from the ways of the earlier peoples until it adopts it.""**"
"12108","ʿAbd al-Raḥmān al-Ḥaḍramī reports that a man who had heard the Prophet ﷺ informed him of the Prophet's ﷺ statement about a group in his Ummah. > **""Indeed, among my Ummah there are a people who will be given reward like the reward of its first ones; they reject evil.""**"
"12109","Abū Umāmah, raḍiya Allāhu ʿanhu, reports that the Messenger of Allah ﷺ described the advance and retreat of this religion, the state of scholars and sinners, open sin, and the reward of enjoining good in corrupt times. > **""Indeed, this religion has an advance and a retreat. Among its advance is that a whole tribe gains understanding until none remains in it except one or two sinners, both humiliated; if they speak they are overpowered and oppressed. And among its retreat is that a whole tribe becomes neglectful so none remains in it except one or two learned men, both humiliated; if they speak they are overpowered and oppressed. And the last of this Ummah will curse its first. Upon them falls the curse, until they drink wine openly, until a woman passes by a group and one of them rises to her and lifts her skirt as one lifts the tail of a ewe, and one who speaks on that day will say: Had you concealed her behind a wall. On that day he among them will be like Abū Bakr and ʿUmar among you. Whoever enjoins good and forbids evil on that day will have the reward of fifty of those who saw me and believed in me and obeyed me and pledged to me.""**"
"12110","ʿAbd Allāh ibn ʿAmr, raḍiya Allāhu ʿanhumā, reports that he heard the Messenger of Allah ﷺ speak about his Ummah fearing to confront an oppressor. > **""When you see my Ummah fearing the oppressor, that they say to him: You are an oppressor, then they have been left without support.""**"
"12111","Abū Mūsā, raḍiya Allāhu ʿanhu, reports that the Messenger of Allah ﷺ swore by the One in Whose Hand is the soul of Muḥammad ﷺ and described good and evil as two creations set forth for people. > **""By the One in Whose Hand is the soul of Muḥammad, indeed good and evil are two natures set forth for people on the Day of Resurrection. As for good, it will give glad tidings to its people and promise them good. And as for evil, it will say: To me, to me, and they will be unable to do anything but cling to it.""**"
"12112","Ibn ʿUmar, raḍiya Allāhu ʿanhumā, reports that the Prophet ﷺ described the people of good and the people of evil in this world and the next. > **""The people of good in this world are the people of good in the Hereafter, and the people of evil in this world are the people of evil in the Hereafter.""**"
"12113","Qabīṣah ibn Burmah al-Asadī, raḍiya Allāhu ʿanhu, reports that he was sitting with the Prophet ﷺ and heard him describe the people of good and evil in both abodes. > **""The people of good in this world are the people of good in the Hereafter, and the people of evil in this world are the people of evil in the Hereafter.""**"
"12114","Abū Hurayrah, raḍiya Allāhu ʿanhu, reports that the Messenger of Allah ﷺ described the people of good and evil in this world and the next. > **""The people of good in this world are the people of good in the Hereafter, and the people of evil in this world are the people of evil in the Hereafter.""**"
"12115","Abū Mūsā al-Ashʿarī, raḍiya Allāhu ʿanhu, reports that the Messenger of Allah ﷺ described the people of good and evil in both abodes. > **""The people of good in this world are the people of good in the Hereafter, and the people of evil in this world are the people of evil in the Hereafter.""**"
"12116","Ibn ʿAbbās, raḍiya Allāhu ʿanhumā, reports that the Messenger of Allah ﷺ described the people of good and evil in this world and the next. > **""The people of good in this world are the people of good in the Hereafter, and the people of evil in this world are the people of evil in the Hereafter.""**"
"12117","Salmān, raḍiya Allāhu ʿanhu, reports that the Messenger of Allah ﷺ described the people of good and evil in this world and the next. > **""Indeed, the people of good in this world are the people of good in the Hereafter, and indeed the people of evil in this world are the people of evil in the Hereafter.""**"
"12118","Abū Umāmah, raḍiya Allāhu ʿanhu, reports that the Messenger of Allah ﷺ described the people of good and their early entry into Paradise. > **""Indeed, the people of good in this world are the people of good in the Hereafter, and indeed the first of the people of Paradise to enter Paradise are the people of good.""**"
"12119","Durrah bint Abī Lahab, raḍiya Allāhu ʿanhā, reports that a man stood before the Prophet ﷺ while he was on the pulpit and asked who the best of people is, and the Prophet ﷺ answered with qualities of recitation, piety, enjoining good, forbidding evil, and joining kinship. In the addition of al-Ṭabarānī, she said she was with ʿĀʾishah when a man was brought to the Prophet ﷺ as though calling to him upon the pulpit with the same question, then the man was seized and said he had no fault but that another had commanded him, and the rest is similar. > **""The best of people are the most reciting, the most God-fearing, the most enjoining of good, the most forbidding of evil, and the most joining of kinship.""**"
"12120","Anas ibn Mālik, raḍiya Allāhu ʿanhu, reports that the Messenger of Allah ﷺ described the believer in relation to his brother. > **""The believer is the mirror of the believer.""**"
"12121","ʿĀʾishah, raḍiya Allāhu ʿanhā, reports that the Messenger of Allah ﷺ commanded helping one's brother in both states and explained how. > **""Help your brother whether oppressor or oppressed; if he is an oppressor then restrain him, and if he is oppressed then secure his right for him.""**"
"12122","ʿAbd al-Raḥmān ibn ʿAwf, raḍiya Allāhu ʿanhu, reports that the Messenger of Allah ﷺ recalled witnessing a pact of Banū Hāshim, Zuhrah, and Taym, and his willingness to answer a like call for justice. > **""I witnessed a pact of Banū Hāshim, Zuhrah, and Taym, and it would not please me to break it even for red camels. If I were called to it today I would answer, upon enjoining good and forbidding evil and taking for the oppressed from the oppressor.""**"
"12123","ʿAbd Allāh ibn Masʿūd, raḍiya Allāhu ʿanhu, reports as a raised narration from the Prophet ﷺ concerning the curse upon the world and its exceptions. > **""The world is cursed, and cursed is what is in it, except enjoining good and forbidding evil and remembrance of Allah.""**"
"12124","Sahl ibn Saʿd, raḍiya Allāhu ʿanhu, reports that he along with Abū Dharr, Abū Saʿīd al-Khudrī, Muḥammad ibn Maslamah, and another man pledged to the Messenger of Allah ﷺ that the blame of no blamer would affect them concerning Allah."
"12125","Yazīd ibn Abī Ḥabīb reports that Muḥammad ibn Yazīd ibn Abī Ziyād narrated that Qays ibn Kharashah and Kaʿb traveled together until they reached Ṣiffīn, where Kaʿb paused a while and swore that Muslim blood would be shed in that place as in no other spot on earth. Qays became angry and asked how he knew this hidden matter reserved to Allah. Kaʿb replied that no span of earth exists except that what will occur upon it and emerge from it until the Day of Resurrection is written in the Torah Allah sent to Mūsā. When Muḥammad ibn Yazīd asked who Qays ibn Kharashah was, he was told he was a man of Qays from his own land whom he did not know. He then told that Qays came to the Prophet ﷺ offering pledge upon what came from Allah and upon speaking truth, that the Prophet ﷺ warned him of later rulers with whom he might be unable to speak truth, that Qays swore to fulfill whatever he pledged, and that the Prophet ﷺ assured him of safety. Thereafter Qays used to criticize Ziyād and his son ʿUbayd Allāh ibn Ziyād, who summoned him and accused him of fabricating against Allah and His Messenger, to which Qays replied that the fabricator is one who abandons action upon the Book of Allah and the practice of His Messenger ﷺ. > **""O Qays, perhaps if life is prolonged for you, rulers will come after me with whom you will be unable to speak truth. Then nothing will harm you.""**"
"12126","Abū Dharr, raḍiya Allāhu ʿanhu, reports that his close friend ﷺ enjoined him to let no blame of a blamer affect him concerning Allah, to look to one below him and not above him, to love the poor and draw near them, to speak truth even if bitter, to join kinship even if it turns away, to ask people for nothing, and to say often: Lā ḥawla wa lā quwwata illā billāh al-ʿAlī al-ʿAẓīm, for it is among the treasures of Paradise. > **""My close friend ﷺ enjoined me to let no blame of a blamer affect me concerning Allah, to look to one below me and not above me, to love the poor and draw near them, to speak truth even if bitter, to join kinship even if it turns away, to ask people for nothing, and to say often: Lā ḥawla wa lā quwwata illā billāh al-ʿAlī al-ʿAẓīm, for it is among the treasures of Paradise.""**"
"12127","Abū Hurayrah, raḍiya Allāhu ʿanhu, reports that the Messenger of Allah ﷺ addressed him about entering upon rulers and commanding piety without fear. > **""O Abā Hurayrah, do not enter upon a ruler; if you are compelled to that, do not exceed my practice, and do not fear his sword and his whip in commanding them to fear Allah and obey Him.""**"
"12128","Abū Saʿīd al-Khudrī, raḍiya Allāhu ʿanhu, reports from the Messenger of Allah ﷺ that fear of people must not prevent speaking truth or reminding of greatness, for it neither hastens death nor withholds provision. > **""Let not fear of people prevent one of you from speaking truth when he sees it and reminding of greatness, for speaking truth or reminding of greatness neither brings near a term nor keeps away provision.""**"
"12129","ʿĀʾishah, raḍiya Allāhu ʿanhā, reports from the Prophet ﷺ that Mūsā, peace be upon him, asked his Lord about His most honored creation, and his Lord described three qualities concerning zeal, attachment to the righteous, and anger over violated sanctities. > **""My most honored creation to Me is he who hastens to My desire as an eagle hastens to its abode, and he who attaches himself to My righteous servants as a child is attached to people, and he who becomes angry when My sanctities are violated like a leopard angry for itself, for when the leopard is angry it cares not whether people are few or many.""**"
"12130","Ibn ʿAbbās, raḍiya Allāhu ʿanhumā, reports that the Messenger of Allah ﷺ named the master of martyrs and one who stands to an unjust ruler with command and forbiddance and is killed. > **""The master of martyrs is Ḥamzah ibn ʿAbd al-Muṭṭalib, and a man who stands to an unjust ruler, enjoins him and forbids him, and he kills him.""**"
"12131","Abū Jaʿfar al-Khaṭmī reports that his grandfather ʿUmayr ibn Ḥabīb ibn Ḥamāshah, raḍiya Allāhu ʿanhu, who had reached the Prophet ﷺ near his maturity, advised his son with counsel against sitting with fools, its harm, forbearance and regret, contentment with little and much, and preparation with patience and trust in reward from Allah the Exalted when enjoining good and forbidding evil, for whoever trusts in reward from Allah, Mighty and Majestic, will not be harmed by affliction. His words were: O my son, beware sitting with fools, for their company is disease; whoever is forbearing toward a fool rejoices, whoever answers him regrets, whoever is not content with little brought by a fool will be content with much; when one of you intends to enjoin good or forbid evil, let him prepare himself for patience over harm and trust in reward from Allah the Exalted, for whoever trusts in reward from Allah, Mighty and Majestic, the touch of harm will not harm him."
"12132","ʿĀʾishah, raḍiya Allāhu ʿanhā, reports that the Messenger of Allah ﷺ entered upon her with signs of distress on his face, performed ablution, went out without speaking to anyone, and she drew near the chambers and heard him address people about enjoining good and forbidding evil before invocation and appeal for help go unanswered. > **""O people, indeed Allah says: Enjoin good and forbid evil before you call upon Me and I do not answer you, and you ask Me and I do not give you, and you seek My help and I do not help you.""**"
"12133","Ibn ʿUmar, raḍiya Allāhu ʿanhumā, reports that the Messenger of Allah ﷺ addressed people about enjoining good and forbidding evil before supplication and seeking forgiveness go unanswered, and about the fate of Jewish scholars and Christian monks who abandoned it. > **""O people, enjoin good and forbid evil before you call upon Allah and He does not answer you, and before you seek His forgiveness and He does not forgive you. Enjoining good does not bring near a term. When the rabbis of the Jews and the monks of the Christians abandoned enjoining good and forbidding evil, Allah cursed them upon the tongues of their prophets and tribulation encompassed them.""**"
"12134","Abū Hurayrah, raḍiya Allāhu ʿanhu, reports that the Messenger of Allah ﷺ warned that people must enjoin good and forbid evil or else their worst will be set over them and the supplication of their best will go unanswered. > **""You shall enjoin good and you shall forbid evil, or Allah will set your worst over you, then your best will supplicate and they will not be answered.""**"
"12135","Ibn ʿAbbās, raḍiya Allāhu ʿanhumā, reports that the Messenger of Allah ﷺ conveyed that his Lord, Mighty and Majestic, swore by His might and majesty to take recompense from the oppressor sooner and later, and from one who sees an oppressed person and can help him yet does not. > **""Your Lord, Mighty and Majestic, says: By My might and majesty, I shall take vengeance from the oppressor in his present and his future, and I shall take vengeance from one who sees an oppressed person and is able to help him yet does not do so.""**"
"12136","Sahl ibn Ḥunayf, raḍiya Allāhu ʿanhu, reports from the Prophet ﷺ about one in whose presence a believer is humiliated and who, though able, does not help him, and his humiliation before creation on the Day of Resurrection. > **""Whoever has a believer humiliated in his presence and does not help him while able to help him, Allah, Mighty and Majestic, will humiliate him before all creation on the Day of Resurrection.""**"
"12137","ʿAdī ibn ʿAdī al-Kindī reports from Mujāhid, from a freedman, from his grandfather, that he heard the Messenger of Allah ﷺ state that Allah does not punish the general for the deed of the elite until they see evil among them, are able to reject it, and do not, whereupon Allah punishes elite and general alike. > **""Indeed Allah, Mighty and Majestic, does not punish the general for the deed of the elite until they see evil among them, while able to reject it, and they do not reject it; when they do that, Allah punishes the elite and the general.""**"
"12138","Jābir and Abū Ayyūb al-Anṣārī, raḍiya Allāhu ʿanhumā, report that the Messenger of Allah ﷺ described the recompense for forsaking a Muslim where his honor is diminished and his sanctity violated, and for supporting him in such a place. > **""There is no man who forsakes a Muslim in a place where his honor is diminished and his sanctity violated except that Allah will forsake him in a place where he desires His support. And there is no man who supports a Muslim in a place where his honor is diminished and his sanctity violated except that Allah will support him in a place where he desires His support.""**"
"12139","ʿImrān ibn Ḥuṣayn, raḍiya Allāhu ʿanhu, reports that the Messenger of Allah ﷺ described the reward for one who supports his brother in absence while able to do so, in this world and the next. > **""Whoever supports his brother in absence while able to support him, Allah will support him in this world and the Hereafter.""**"
"12140","Ibn ʿUmar, raḍiya Allāhu ʿanhumā, reports that he heard the Messenger of Allah ﷺ tell of a man placed in his grave, visited by two angels who struck him until his grave filled with fire, then answered his question about the cause with prayer without purification and passing a wronged man without helping him. > **""A man was placed in his grave, and two angels came to him and said to him: We shall strike you a blow. He said to them: For what will you strike me? They struck him a blow with which his grave filled with fire, then they left him until he awoke and terror left him, and he said to them: For what did you strike me? They said: You prayed a prayer while not in a state of purity, and you passed by a wronged man and did not help him.""**"
"12141","Abū Hurayrah, raḍiya Allāhu ʿanhu, reports that the Messenger of Allah ﷺ distinguished between hidden sin which harms only its doer and open sin which, left unchanged, harms the general. > **""When sin is hidden it harms none but its doer, and when it appears and is not changed it harms the general.""**"
"12142","ʿAbd Allāh ibn Masʿūd, raḍiya Allāhu ʿanhu, reports that he heard the Messenger of Allah ﷺ warn about a man who practices disobedience among a people more numerous and mightier than he, while they are negligent toward him, and Allah's punishment upon them. > **""There is no man among a people who practices disobedience to Allah among them, while they are more numerous than he and mightier, then they are negligent toward him, except that Allah punishes them.""**"
"12143","Al-ʿUrs ibn ʿAmīrah, raḍiya Allāhu ʿanhu, reports that the Messenger of Allah ﷺ stated that Allah does not punish the general for the deed of the elite until the elite act and the general, though able to change it, does not, whereupon Allah permits the ruin of general and elite. > **""Indeed Allah does not punish the general for the deed of the elite until the elite do a deed which the general are able to change and do not change; then Allah permits the destruction of the general and the elite.""**"
"12144","Ibn ʿAbbās, raḍiya Allāhu ʿanhumā, reports that it was asked whether a town with righteous people would perish, the Prophet ﷺ affirmed, and when asked why he attributed it to their witnessing disobedience and silence. > **""Yes. It was asked: Why, O Messenger of Allah? He said: Because of their witnessing disobedience to Allah and their silence toward it.""**"
"12145","Umm Salamah, raḍiya Allāhu ʿanhā, reports that she heard the Messenger of Allah ﷺ warn that when sins appear in his Ummah Allah will encompass them with punishment from Him, and she asked about the righteous among them and was told they would be afflicted then move to forgiveness and pleasure. > **""When sins appear in my Ummah, Allah will encompass them with punishment from Him. I said: O Messenger of Allah, are there not righteous among them? He said: Yes. I said: What will be done with them? He said: What afflicts people will afflict them, then they will pass to forgiveness from Allah and pleasure.""**"
"12146","ʿĀʾishah, raḍiya Allāhu ʿanhā, conveying from the Prophet ﷺ, reports that when evil appears in a land Allah sends His might upon its people, and when asked about the obedient among them she was told they would pass to Allah's mercy. > **""When evil appears in a land, Allah, Mighty and Majestic, sends His might upon the people of the land. She said: And among them are those obedient to Allah? He said: Yes, then they will pass to the mercy of Allah.""**"
"12147","Ibn ʿUmar, raḍiya Allāhu ʿanhumā, reports that the Messenger of Allah ﷺ stated that when Allah sends punishment upon a people it afflicts all among them, then Allah will raise them according to their deeds. > **""When Allah, Blessed and Exalted, sends punishment upon a people, the punishment afflicts whoever is among them, then Allah, Blessed and Exalted, will raise them according to their deeds.""**"
"12148","Anas ibn Mālik, raḍiya Allāhu ʿanhu, reports that a swallowing of the earth toward the east was mentioned in the time of the Messenger of Allah ﷺ, a man asked whether a land with Muslims would be swallowed, and the Prophet ﷺ linked it to the prevalence of corruption. > **""Yes, when most of its people are corrupt.""**"
"12149","Umm Ḥabībah, raḍiya Allāhu ʿanhā, reports that the Messenger of Allah ﷺ entered upon her saying words of return to Allah and warning of approaching evil and the opening of the barrier of Yaʾjūj and Maʾjūj, and when she asked about destruction with righteous present he linked it to abundance of corruption. > **""Indeed we belong to Allah and indeed to Him we return. Woe to the Arabs from an evil that has drawn near; opened from the barrier of Yaʾjūj and Maʾjūj is like this, and he made a circle of ninety. I said: O Messenger of Allah, shall we perish while righteous are among us? He said: Yes, when corruption abounds.""**"
"12150","Buraydah, raḍiya Allāhu ʿanhu, reports that the Messenger of Allah ﷺ described three consequences for breach of covenant, open lewdness, and withholding zakāh concerning killing, death, and rain. > **""No people break a covenant except that killing spreads among them, no lewdness ever appears among a people except that Allah sets death over them, and no people ever withhold zakāh except that Allah withholds rain from them.""**"
"12151","Ibn ʿUmar, raḍiya Allāhu ʿanhumā, reports as a raised narration from the Prophet ﷺ about a seal suspended at the leg of the Throne, sent when kinship is severed, sins are practiced, and boldness is shown toward Allah, sealing the heart beyond understanding. > **""The seal is suspended at the leg of the Throne; when kinship complains and sins are practiced and boldness is shown toward Allah, Allah sends the seal and seals his heart, so he understands nothing thereafter.""**"
"12152","Abū Umāmah, raḍiya Allāhu ʿanhu, reports from the Prophet ﷺ that when sins are practiced among a people of whom the doer is one, and they do not prevent it or change evil, the protection of Allah is removed from them. > **""Whoever practices sins among a people of whom he is one, and they do not prevent him from that until they change evil, the protection of Allah is removed from them.""**"
"12153","Abū Mūsā, raḍiya Allāhu ʿanhu, reports from the Prophet ﷺ that among Banū Isrāʾīl before them, one who forbade a sinner would the next day sit, eat, and drink with him as if he had not seen him in sin, so Allah joined their hearts and cursed them upon the tongues of Dāwūd and ʿĪsā son of Maryam, and he warned his Ummah to enjoin good, forbid evil, restrain the wrongdoer, and bend him to truth. > **""Among those before you of Banū Isrāʾīl, when a doer practiced sin among them, one who forbade would forbid him as reproof, then the next day he would sit with him and eat with him and drink with him as if he had not seen him in sin yesterday. When Allah the Exalted saw that from them, He joined the hearts of some to others upon the tongue of Dāwūd and ʿĪsā son of Maryam, for they disobeyed and transgressed. By the One in Whose Hand is my soul, you shall enjoin good and you shall forbid evil and you shall seize the hand of the wrongdoer and bend him to truth, or Allah will join the hearts of some of you to others and curse you as He cursed them.""**"
"12154","ʿAbd Allāh ibn ʿAmr, raḍiya Allāhu ʿanhu, reports that he heard the Messenger of Allah ﷺ speak about his Ummah fearing to name the oppressor as oppressor. > **""When you see my Ummah fearing the oppressor, that they say to him: You are an oppressor, then they have been left without support.""**"
"12155","Jābir, raḍiya Allāhu ʿanhu, reports that the Messenger of Allah ﷺ spoke about his Ummah fearing to name the oppressor as oppressor. > **""When you see my Ummah fearing the oppressor, that they say to him: You are an oppressor, then they have been left without support.""**"
"12156","Jābir, raḍiya Allāhu ʿanhu, reports that the Messenger of Allah ﷺ told of Allah's revelation to an angel to overturn a certain city, the angel's mention of a servant who had never disobeyed for a blink, and Allah's command to overturn it upon him and them because his face never changed for His sake. > **""Allah revealed to an angel among the angels: Overturn such and such a city upon its people. He said: In it is Your servant so-and-so who never disobeyed You for a blink? He said: Overturn it upon him and upon them, for his face never changed for a moment.""**"
"12157","Abū Hurayrah, raḍiya Allāhu ʿanhu, reports that the Messenger of Allah ﷺ foretold rulers after him who act with knowledge and as commanded, and rulers who act without knowledge and do what is not commanded, and distinguished one who rejects, one who withholds, and one who approves and follows. > **""There will be successors after me who act upon what they know and do what they are commanded, and there will be successors after me who act upon what they do not know and do what they are not commanded. Whoever rejects them is absolved, and whoever withholds his hand is safe, but whoever approves and follows.""**"
"12158","Ibn ʿUmar, raḍiya Allāhu ʿanhumā, reports that the Messenger of Allah ﷺ foretold rulers who act with knowledge and as commanded, then rulers after them who act without knowledge and do what is not commanded, and the safety of one who rejects. > **""There will be rulers over you who act upon what they know and do what they are commanded, and there will be after them rulers who act upon what they do not know and do what they are not commanded. Whoever rejects is safe, but whoever approves and follows.""**"
"12159","Muʿādh ibn Jabal, raḍiya Allāhu ʿanhu, reports that the Messenger of Allah ﷺ stated that they remain under protection from their Lord while two intoxications have not appeared, while they enjoin good, forbid evil, and strive, and described speakers by Book and practice in times of worldliness as like the foremost Emigrants and Helpers. > **""You remain under protection from your Lord while two intoxications have not appeared among you: the intoxication of ignorance and the intoxication of love of life, while you enjoin good and forbid evil and strive in the path of Allah. When love of the world appears among you, you will not enjoin good nor forbid evil nor strive in the path of Allah; those who speak by the Book and the practice on that day will be like the foremost predecessors among the Emigrants and the Helpers.""**"
"12160","Abū Umāmah, raḍiya Allāhu ʿanhu, reports that the Messenger of Allah ﷺ described the advance and retreat of religion, the humiliation of the learned in corrupt times, open wine-drinking and shamelessness, and the reward of fifty of the Prophet's ﷺ companions for one who enjoins good then. > **""Indeed, this religion has an advance and a retreat. Among its advance is that a whole tribe gains understanding until none remains in it except one or two sinners, both humiliated; if they speak they are overpowered and oppressed. And among its retreat is that a whole tribe becomes neglectful so none remains in it except one or two learned men, both humiliated; if they speak they are overpowered and oppressed. And the last of this Ummah will curse its first. Upon them falls the curse, until they drink wine openly, until a woman passes by a group and one of them rises to her and lifts her skirt as one lifts the tail of a ewe, and one who speaks on that day will say: Had you concealed her behind this wall. On that day he among them will be like Abū Bakr and ʿUmar among you. Whoever enjoins good and forbids evil on that day will have the reward of fifty of those who saw me and believed in me and obeyed me and pledged to me.""**"
"12161","ʿAbd al-Raḥmān ibn al-Ḥaḍramī reports that a man who had heard the Prophet ﷺ informed him of the Prophet's ﷺ statement about a people in his Ummah who reject evil and receive like the reward of its first. > **""Indeed, among my Ummah are a people who will be given like the reward of its first; they reject evil.""**"
"12162","ʿAbd Allāh ibn Masʿūd, raḍiya Allāhu ʿanhu, states that among the greatest sins is for a man to say to his brother, Fear Allah, and for the other to reply, Mind yourself, do you command me."
"12163","ʿAbd Allāh, raḍiya Allāhu ʿanhu, states that it suffices a man for sin that when it is said to him, Fear Allah, he becomes angry."
"12164","Samurah, raḍiya Allāhu ʿanhu, reports that the Messenger of Allah ﷺ described the most excellent striving as speaking truth before authority, with a narrator's doubt whether he said before an unjust ruler. > **""The best striving is to speak truth before a ruler, or he said: Before an unjust ruler.""**"
"12165","ʿAbd Allāh ibn ʿAbbās, raḍiya Allāhu ʿanhumā, reports that the Messenger of Allah ﷺ named the master of martyrs on the Day of Resurrection and one who stands to an unjust ruler with command and forbiddance and is killed. > **""The master of martyrs on the Day of Resurrection is Ḥamzah ibn ʿAbd al-Muṭṭalib, and a man who stands to an unjust ruler, enjoins him and forbids him, and he kills him.""**"
"12166","Abū ʿUbaydah ibn al-Jarrāḥ, raḍiya Allāhu ʿanhu, reports that he asked the Messenger of Allah ﷺ about the most honored martyr and the severest punishment, and the Prophet ﷺ answered with one killed for enjoining good, one who kills a prophet or a counselor, recited the verse of Āl ʿImrān about those who kill prophets and those who enjoin justice, and told of forty-three prophets killed in one hour and one hundred twelve worshippers of Banū Isrāʾīl killed for enjoining good. > **""A man who stands to an unjust ruler, enjoins him to good and forbids him evil, and he kills him. It was asked: Which people suffer severest punishment? He said: A man who kills a prophet or kills a man who enjoined him to good and forbade him evil. Then he recited: And they kill the prophets without right and kill those who enjoin justice among people, so give them tidings of painful punishment. Then he said: O Abā ʿUbaydah, Banū Isrāʾīl killed forty-three prophets in one hour, and one hundred and twelve men among the worshippers of Banū Isrāʾīl rose, enjoined good and forbade evil, and all were killed.""**"
"12167","Al-Muʿallā ibn Ziyād reports that when Yazīd ibn al-Muhallab routed the people of Basra, he feared to sit in the circle of al-Ḥasan ibn Abī al-Ḥasan lest he be singled out and recognized, so he visited al-Ḥasan at his house. He asked him about a verse of the Book of Allah concerning those hastening in sin, aggression, and devouring unlawful gain, and al-Ḥasan replied that the people had brought forth the sword and the sword had come between speech. When asked whether he knew any merit for one who speaks in such time, al-Ḥasan answered that he did not. Al-Muʿallā then cited two narrations through Abū Saʿīd al-Khudrī, raḍiya Allāhu ʿanhu, from the Messenger of Allah ﷺ. Al-Ḥasan then cited another narration from the Messenger of Allah ﷺ. When asked about Yazīd al-Ḍabbī and his speech concerning prayer, al-Ḥasan said he would not leave prison until he regretted. Al-Muʿallā then left the gathering of al-Ḥasan and went to Yazīd, telling him that he and al-Ḥasan had discussed and set forth his affair, and conveyed al-Ḥasan's remark about regret. Yazīd swore by Allah that he had not regretted his statement and that he had stood a stand risking himself. Yazīd then went to al-Ḥasan complaining that they were overcome in all things including prayer, and al-Ḥasan replied that he had done nothing but exposed himself to them, repeating the same on a second visit. Then Yazīd stood on Friday in the mosque while al-Ḥakam ibn Ayyūb was sermonizing and called out for prayer. Men surrounded him, seized his beard and collar, and struck his belly with sword hilts and sandals. They took him toward the enclosure, and he thought they would kill him before reaching it. When brought before al-Ḥakam, who was silent, al-Ḥakam asked whether he was mad and denied neglect of prayer. Yazīd asked whether any speech is superior to the Book of Allah and whether reciting a copy from morning to night would suffice for prayer, to which al-Ḥakam deemed him mad. Anas ibn Mālik, raḍiya Allāhu ʿanhu, sat silent beneath the pulpit, and when Yazīd adjured him by Allah concerning his service to the Messenger of Allah ﷺ and whether his word was right or wrong, good or evil, Anas answered not a word. Al-Ḥakam addressed Anas, who responded in deference. With prayer time elapsed except a remnant of sun, al-Ḥakam ordered imprisonment. Yazīd swore to al-Muʿallā that what he met from his companions, some calling him ostentatious and some mad, was harder than his statement. Al-Ḥakam wrote to al-Ḥajjāj that a man of Banū Ḍabbah had risen on Friday calling for prayer during the sermon and that upright witnesses deemed him mad, and al-Ḥajjāj replied to release him if upright witnesses affirmed madness, otherwise to sever hands and feet, blind eyes, and crucify. Witnesses affirmed madness before al-Ḥakam, so he released him. Al-Muʿallā further reports from Yazīd al-Ḍabbī that after a brother's death and burial, he sat apart with a group remembering Allah and the return, companions fled at sight of horses and spears, al-Ḥakam questioned his conduct and flight, Yazīd professed innocence and trust, and ʿAbd al-Malik ibn al-Muhallab identified him as the Friday speaker. Al-Ḥakam grew angry, ordered seizure, inflicted four hundred lashes until Yazīd lost awareness, then sent him to Wāsiṭ, where he remained in the prison of al-Ḥajjāj until al-Ḥajjāj died. > **""Let not fear of people prevent one of you from speaking truth when he sees it or reminding of greatness, for it neither brings near a term nor keeps away provision. It is not for the believer to humiliate himself. It was asked: What is his humiliating himself? He said: He exposes himself to trial which he cannot bear.""**"
"12168","ʿAlī ibn Zayd reports that he was in the palace with al-Ḥajjāj while he interrogated people over Ibn al-Ashʿath. Anas ibn Mālik, raḍiya Allāhu ʿanhu, came forward until he drew near, and al-Ḥajjāj addressed him with abuse, calling him wicked and a wanderer in trials, at times with ʿAlī ibn Abī Ṭālib, at times with Ibn al-Zubayr, at times with Ibn al-Ashʿath, swearing by the One in Whose Hand is his soul to uproot him like gum is uprooted and strip him like a lizard is stripped. Anas asked whom the commander, may Allah keep him right, meant, and al-Ḥajjāj replied that he meant him and invoked deafness upon his hearing. Anas recited return to Allah and left him, then said that but for remembering his children and fearing for them, he would have addressed him in that standing with words after which al-Ḥajjāj could never answer him."
"12169","Ibn ʿUmar, raḍiya Allāhu ʿanhumā, reports that he heard al-Ḥajjāj deliver a sermon with objectionable speech, intended to correct him, then recalled the statement of the Messenger of Allah ﷺ that the believer should not humiliate himself by exposing himself to unbearable trial. > **""It is not fitting for the believer to humiliate himself. He said: I said: O Messenger of Allah, how does he humiliate himself? He said: He exposes himself to trial which he cannot bear.""**"
"12170","ʿAlī, raḍiya Allāhu ʿanhu, reports that the Messenger of Allah ﷺ stated that the Muslim should not humiliate himself, and when asked how, explained it as undertaking unbearable trial. > **""It is not for the Muslim to humiliate himself. They said: O Messenger of Allah, how does he humiliate himself? He said: He exposes himself to trial which he cannot bear.""**"
"12171","ʿUbādah ibn al-Ṣāmit, raḍiya Allāhu ʿanhu, reports that he heard the Messenger of Allah ﷺ warn of trials in which the believer cannot change evil by hand or tongue, and when ʿAlī ibn Abī Ṭālib, raḍiya Allāhu ʿanhu, asked whether that diminishes faith, the Prophet ﷺ likened it to a drop from a waterskin, because they hate it in their hearts. > **""There will be trials in which the believer cannot change by hand nor by tongue. ʿAlī ibn Abī Ṭālib said: O Messenger of Allah, will that diminish anything from their faith? He said: No, except as a drop diminishes from a waterskin. He said: Why is that? He said: They hate it in their hearts.""**"
"12172","ʿUbādah ibn al-Ṣāmit, raḍiya Allāhu ʿanhu, reports that the Messenger of Allah ﷺ asked how he would fare among worthless people in disagreement, interlacing his fingers, and when ʿUbādah replied that Allah and His Messenger know best, commanded holding to the known and leaving the rejected. > **""How will you fare when among worthless people who differ until they become like this, and he interlaced his fingers? He said: Allah and His Messenger know best. He said: Take what you know and leave what you reject.""**"
"12173","ʿAbd Allāh ibn Masʿūd, raḍiya Allāhu ʿanhu, reports that the Messenger of Allah ﷺ stated that it suffices a man who sees evil he cannot change that Allah knows he rejects it. > **""It suffices a man who sees evil which he cannot change, that Allah knows he rejects it.""**"
"12174","Abū Umāmah, raḍiya Allāhu ʿanhu, reports from the Prophet ﷺ that when people see a matter they cannot change, they should be patient until Allah Himself changes it. > **""When you see a matter which you cannot change, be patient until Allah Himself changes it.""**"
"12175","Ṭāriq ibn Shihāb reports that ʿItrīs ibn ʿUrqūb al-Shaybānī came to ʿAbd Allāh and said that ruined is one who does not enjoin good and forbid evil, but ʿAbd Allāh replied that ruined is one whose heart does not know good nor reject evil."
"12176","ʿAbd Allāh ibn Masʿūd, raḍiya Allāhu ʿanhu, states that people are three, and no good in others besides them: a man who sees a band fighting in the path of Allah and strives with himself and wealth, a man who strives with his tongue and enjoins good and forbids evil, and a man who knows truth in his heart."
"12177","ʿAbd Allāh ibn Masʿūd, raḍiya Allāhu ʿanhu, states that when one sees a sinner and cannot change his conduct, one should meet him with a stern face."
"12178","ʿAbd Allāh ibn Busr, raḍiya Allāhu ʿanhu, reports that he long ago heard a narration that when one is among twenty men or fewer or more, examines their faces, and finds none feared for the sake of Allah, Mighty and Majestic, then affairs have become weak."
"12179","Al-Walīd ibn ʿUqbah, raḍiya Allāhu ʿanhu, reports that the Messenger of Allah ﷺ described people of Paradise looking toward people of Fire and asking how they entered Fire when Paradise was reached through learning from them, and their answer that they spoke without acting. > **""Some people of Paradise will look toward some people of Fire and say: How did you enter Fire, when by Allah we entered Paradise only through what we learned from you? They will say: We used to speak and not act.""**"
"12180","Anas ibn Mālik, raḍiya Allāhu ʿanhu, reports that the Messenger of Allah ﷺ told of the night of his Ascension, when he came upon men whose lips were clipped with shears of fire, asked Jibrīl who they were, and was told they were the preachers of his Ummah who enjoined righteousness yet forgot themselves while reciting the Book. > **""I came on the night I was taken by night upon men whose lips were clipped with shears of fire. I said: Who are these, O Jibrīl? He said: These are the preachers of your Ummah who enjoined people to righteousness and forgot themselves while reciting the Book; do they not understand.""**"
"12181","In another version of the Ascension narration concerning preachers who enjoined good yet neglected themselves, the wording mentions tongues clipped with shears of fire, with a narrator's doubt whether he said shears of iron. > **""Their tongues are clipped with shears of fire, or he said: Of iron.""**"
"12182","In another wording of the preceding report, the Prophet ﷺ describes coming to the lowest heaven on the night he was taken by night journey and seeing men there whose tongues and lips were being cut off, after which the narrator related a similar account to the previous one. > **""I came to the lowest heaven on the night I was taken by night journey, and I saw therein men whose tongues and lips were being cut off.""**"
"12183","Ibn ʿUmar, may Allah be pleased with him, reports that the Messenger of Allah ﷺ warned about a man who invites others to a saying or deed while failing to act upon it himself, explaining how long divine displeasure remains upon him and what removes it. > **""Whoever calls people to a saying or a deed while he himself does not act upon it will remain under the displeasure of Allah until he desists or acts upon what he said or invited to.""**"
"12184","ʿAmir ibn Shahr, may Allah be pleased with him, reports that he heard the Messenger of Allah ﷺ command adherence to the sound speech of Quraysh while abandoning their wrongful conduct. > **""Take the speech of Quraysh and leave their deeds.""**"
"12185","Anas ibn Malik, may Allah be pleased with him, reports that the Companions asked the Messenger of Allah ﷺ whether they should refrain from enjoining good until they practiced it fully and refrain from forbidding evil until they abandoned it entirely, and the Prophet ﷺ answered them. > **""Rather, enjoin good even if you do not act upon it, and forbid evil even if you do not avoid all of it.""**"
"12186","ʿAishah, may Allah be pleased with her, reports that the Messenger of Allah ﷺ described the condition under which the people of the declaration of divine unity remain upon good, and what is said to them when they cease to care about deficiencies in religion caused by pursuit of worldly welfare. > **""The people of La ilaha illa Allah will remain upon good so long as they care about what is diminished from the affair of their religion in the pursuit of the welfare of their worldly life, but when they cease to care about what is diminished from the affair of their religion for the welfare of their worldly life, it will be thrown back at them and it will be said to them: You are not truthful.""**"
"12187","Abu Hurayrah, may Allah be pleased with him, reports that the Messenger of Allah ﷺ explained how long the declaration of divine unity repels harm from its speaker, and what is said when its speakers no longer care about harm to their religion so long as their worldly life is secure. > **""La ilaha illa Allah will continue to repel harm from its speaker so long as its speakers care about what befalls them in their worldly life while their religion remains sound for them, but when its speakers cease to care about what befalls them in their religion for the safety of their worldly life, and they say: La ilaha illa Allah, it will be said to them: You have lied.""**"
"12188","Anas ibn Malik, may Allah be pleased with him, reports that the Messenger of Allah ﷺ stated that the declaration of divine unity shields the servant from divine anger until worldly transactions are preferred over religion, and what Allah says when it is then uttered. > **""La ilaha illa Allah shields the servant from the anger of Allah so long as they do not prefer their worldly bargain over their religion, but when they do that and then say: La ilaha illa Allah, Allah says: You have lied.""**"
"12189","Saʿd ibn Abi Waqqas, may Allah be pleased with him, reports that he heard the Messenger of Allah ﷺ describe how faith began in strangeness and will return to strangeness as it began, give glad tidings to the strangers when people become corrupt, swear by Him in Whose Hand is the soul of Abu al-Qasim, and liken the retreat of faith between the two mosques to a serpent retreating to its hole. > **""Faith began strange, and it will return strange as it began, so glad tidings that day belong to the strangers when people become corrupt. By Him in Whose Hand is the soul of Abu al-Qasim, faith will surely retreat to between these two mosques just as the serpent retreats to its hole.""**"
"12190","ʿAbd al-Rahman ibn Sannah, may Allah be pleased with him, reports that he heard the Prophet ﷺ declare that Islam began strange and will return strange as it began with glad tidings for the strangers, and when asked who the strangers are he described them as those who remain upright when people become corrupt, then swore by Him in Whose Hand is his soul that faith will withdraw toward Madinah like a torrent flows and that Islam will return to between the two mosques like a serpent returns to its hole. > **""Islam began strange, then it will return strange as it began, so glad tidings belong to the strangers. They are those who remain upright when people become corrupt. By Him in Whose Hand is my soul, faith will surely withdraw to Madinah just as the torrent flows, and by Him in Whose Hand is my soul, Islam will surely return to between these two mosques just as the serpent returns to its hole.""**"
"12191","ʿAbd Allah ibn ʿAmr, may Allah be pleased with him, reports that while the Companions were with the Messenger of Allah ﷺ one day, the Prophet ﷺ gave glad tidings to the strangers, and when asked who the strangers are he described them as righteous people among evil people whose disobedient opponents outnumber their obedient followers. > **""Glad tidings belong to the strangers. They are righteous people among evil people, those who disobey them being more numerous than those who obey them.""**"
"12192","Ibn ʿUmar, may Allah be pleased with him, reports from the Prophet ﷺ that Islam began strange and will return strange as it began, with glad tidings for the strangers. > **""Islam began strange, and it will return strange as it began, so glad tidings belong to the strangers.""**"
"12193","Sahl ibn Saʿd al-Saʿidi, may Allah be pleased with him, reports that the Messenger of Allah ﷺ declared that Islam began strange and will return strange as it began with glad tidings for the strangers, and when asked who they are he identified them as those who remain upright when people become corrupt. > **""Islam began strange, and it will return strange as it began, so glad tidings belong to the strangers. They are those who remain upright when people become corrupt.""**"
"12194","Jabir ibn ʿAbd Allah, may Allah be pleased with him, reports that the Messenger of Allah ﷺ declared that Islam began strange and will again become strange with glad tidings for the strangers, and when asked who the strangers are he identified them as those who remain upright when people become corrupt. > **""Islam began strange, and it will return strange, so glad tidings belong to the strangers. They are those who remain upright when people become corrupt.""**"
"12195","Ibn ʿAbbas, may Allah be pleased with him, reports that the Messenger of Allah ﷺ declared that Islam began strange and will return strange with glad tidings for the strangers, after which the narrator related the full hadith which will come later. > **""Islam began strange, and it will return strange, so glad tidings belong to the strangers.""**"
"12196","Abu Saʿid al-Khudri, may Allah be pleased with him, reports that the Messenger of Allah ﷺ declared that Islam began strange and will return strange as it began, with glad tidings for the strangers. > **""Islam began strange, and it will return strange as it began, so glad tidings belong to the strangers.""**"
"12197","Salman, may Allah be pleased with him, reports that the Messenger of Allah ﷺ declared that Islam began strange and will return strange. > **""Islam began strange and it will return strange.""**"
"12198","Abu Musa al-Ashʿari, may Allah be pleased with him, reports that the Messenger of Allah ﷺ declared that the Hour will not come until the earth is drenched with blood and Islam becomes strange, after which the narrator related the remainder of the hadith. > **""The Hour will not come until the earth is drenched with blood and Islam becomes strange.""**"
"12199","ʿAlqamah ibn ʿAbd Allah al-Muzani reports from an unnamed man who was present in a gathering in Madinah with ʿUmar ibn al-Khattab, may Allah be pleased with him, that ʿUmar asked him how he heard the Messenger of Allah ﷺ describe Islam, and when the Prophetic description through the stages of a young camel to full maturity was given, ʿUmar remarked that after full maturity nothing remains except decline. > **""Islam began as a jadhaʿ, then became a thani, then a rabaʿi, then a sadis, then a bazil.""**"
"12200","Ibn ʿUmar, may Allah be pleased with him, reports from the Prophet ﷺ that the Prophet ﷺ addressed ʿAbd Allah ibn ʿUmar about the time when he would remain among worthless remnants of people whose covenants and trusts would be entangled and who would differ until they became like interlaced fingers, which the Prophet ﷺ illustrated by interlacing his fingers, and when asked what to do the Prophet ﷺ instructed him to hold to what he recognizes, abandon what he rejects, attend to his private affairs, and leave the common masses. > **""How will you be, O ʿAbd Allah ibn ʿUmar, when you remain among worthless remnants of people whose covenants and trusts have become entangled and they have differed and become like this? Take what you recognize, leave what you reject, attend to your private affairs, and leave the common masses.""**"
"12201","Sahl ibn Saʿd al-Saʿidi, may Allah be pleased with him, reports that the Messenger of Allah ﷺ came out to them one day while they were in the gathering of ʿAmr ibn al-ʿAs and his two sons, and asked how they would fare if they were left until the time of worthless remnants whose covenants and vows would be entangled and interlocked like interlaced fingers, which he illustrated by interlacing his fingers, and when they replied that Allah and His Messenger know best he instructed them to hold to what they recognize, abandon what they reject, let each attend to his private self, and leave the affair of the common masses. > **""What will you think if you are left until the time of worthless remnants of people whose covenants and vows have become entangled and interlocked and they become like this? Take what you recognize, leave what you reject, let each of you attend to his private self, and leave the affair of the common masses.""**"
"12202","In another wording of the preceding report from Sahl, may Allah be pleased with him, the Prophet ﷺ warned against fickleness in the religion of Allah. > **""Beware of shifting colours in the religion of Allah.""**"
"12203","ʿUbadah ibn al-Samit, may Allah be pleased with him, reports that the Messenger of Allah ﷺ asked how he would fare when among worthless remnants who differ until they become like interlaced fingers, which he illustrated by interlacing his fingers, and when ʿUbadah replied that Allah and His Messenger know best the Prophet ﷺ instructed him to take what he recognizes and leave what he rejects. > **""How will you be when you are among worthless remnants of people and they differ until they become like this? Take what you recognize and leave what you reject.""**"
"12204","ʿAbd Allah ibn Masʿud, may Allah be pleased with him, said that one should mix with people and associate with them outwardly according to their desires, but one must not injure or compromise ones religion."
"12205","In another wording from Ibn Masʿud, may Allah be pleased with him, he said to mix with people while keeping oneself distinct from them."
"12206","ʿAbd Allah ibn ʿAmr, may Allah be pleased with him, reports from the Prophet ﷺ the parable of a guest who stayed with a man of the Children of Israil in whose house was a bitch near delivery which resolved by Allah that it would not bark at the guests household, after which its puppies cried out in its belly, and when asked about this Allah revealed to one of their men that this is the likeness of a nation coming after them whose foolish ones will overpower its forbearing ones. > **""A guest stayed with a man of the Children of Israil, and in his house was a bitch near delivery, so the bitch said: By Allah, I will not bark at the guest of my household. Then its puppies cried out in its belly. It was said: What is this? So Allah, Mighty and Majestic, revealed to one of their men: This is the likeness of a nation that will come after you, whose foolish ones will overpower its forbearing ones.""**"
"12207","Abu Bakrah, may Allah be pleased with him, reports that he heard the Messenger of Allah ﷺ declare that a time will come upon people in which they will neither enjoin good nor forbid evil. > **""There will come upon people a time in which they neither enjoin good nor forbid evil.""**"
"12208","ʿAbd Allah, meaning Ibn Masʿud, may Allah be pleased with him, said that the righteous will depart and the people of doubt will remain, those who neither recognize good nor reject evil."
"12209","ʿAbd al-ʿAziz ibn Abi Bakrah reports that Abu Bakrah, may Allah be pleased with him, married a woman of Banu ʿUlathah who then died, that he carried her to the graveyard where her brothers prevented him from leading the funeral prayer, that he told them not to do so for he had more right to lead the prayer than they did, that they acknowledged the Companion of the Messenger of Allah ﷺ spoke truly so he led the prayer over her, that he then entered the grave and they pushed him away harshly so he fell and lost consciousness, that he was carried to his family while twenty of his sons and daughters cried out over him that day, that ʿAbd al-ʿAziz was then among the youngest of them, that when Abu Bakrah recovered he told them not to cry out over him for by Allah no soul departing is dearer to him than the soul of Abu Bakrah, that the people became alarmed and asked why, and that he replied he feared reaching a time in which he could neither enjoin good nor forbid evil, in which there would be no good."
"12210","Abu Hurayrah, may Allah be pleased with him, reports that the Messenger of Allah ﷺ asked the people how they would fare when their women transgress and their youths become corrupt, and when they asked whether that would really happen he affirmed it and worse, then asked how they would fare when they abandoned enjoining good and forbidding evil, and when they asked whether that would happen he affirmed it and worse, then asked how they would fare when they regarded the reprehensible as good and the good as reprehensible. > **""How will you be, O people, when your women transgress and your youths become corrupt? Yes, and worse than that. How will you be when you abandon enjoining good and forbidding evil? Yes, and worse than that. How will you be when you regard the reprehensible as good and the good as reprehensible?""**"
"12211","Abu Umamah al-Bahili, may Allah be pleased with him, reports from the Messenger of Allah ﷺ that the bonds of Islam will be undone one by one, that whenever one bond is undone people will cling to the next, and that the first of them to be undone is judgment while the last is prayer. > **""The bonds of Islam will surely be undone one by one, so whenever one bond is undone people will cling to the one that follows it, and the first of them to be undone is judgment and the last of them is prayer.""**"
"12212","Shaddad Abu ʿAmmar reports from a neighbour of Jabir ibn ʿAbd Allah, may Allah be pleased with him, that the neighbour returned from a journey and Jabir came to greet him, that the neighbour began speaking about the division of people and what they had innovated, that Jabir wept and then said he heard the Messenger of Allah ﷺ declare that people entered the religion of Allah in crowds and will leave it in crowds. > **""People entered the religion of Allah in crowds, and they will leave it in crowds.""**"
"12213","Ibn ʿAbbas, may Allah be pleased with him, reports that the Messenger of Allah ﷺ addressed Banu Hashim about the harsh estrangement that would befall them after him and instructed them to seek help against it through the weakest of people. > **""O Banu Hashim, harsh estrangement will befall you after me, so seek help against it through the weakest of people.""**"
"12214","Abu Hurayrah, may Allah be pleased with him, reports that the Messenger of Allah ﷺ warned of woe to the Arabs from an approaching evil with trials like portions of dark night, in which a man will be a believer in the morning and a disbeliever in the evening, people will sell their religion for a small portion of the world, and the one clinging to his religion will be like one grasping a burning coal, or like grasping a thorn, and in another wording like grasping thorny branches. > **""Woe to the Arabs from an evil that has drawn near, trials like portions of dark night. A man will be a believer in the morning and a disbeliever in the evening. People will sell their religion for a small portion of the world. The one clinging to his religion will be like one grasping a burning coal, or like grasping a thorn.""**"
"12215","ʿUtbah ibn Ghazwan, may Allah be pleased with him, being among the Companions, reports that the Prophet of Allah ﷺ declared that after them would come days of patience in which one who clings then to the like of what they are upon would have the reward of fifty of them, and when asked whether fifty from among the later people or from among the Companions the Prophet ﷺ replied three or four times that they would be from among the Companions. > **""After you there are days of patience. For one who clings therein to the like of what you are upon, his reward will be like the reward of fifty of you. Rather, from among you.""**"
"12216","ʿAbd Allah ibn Masʿud, may Allah be pleased with him, reports that the Messenger of Allah ﷺ declared that after them would come days of patience in which patience would be like grasping a burning coal and the worker therein would have the reward of fifty, and when asked whether fifty from among the later people or from among the Companions the Prophet ﷺ said fifty from among the Companions, and in a similar wording that the one who clings will have the reward of fifty martyrs, whereupon ʿUmar asked whether from among the Companions or the later people and the Prophet ﷺ said from among the Companions. > **""After you there are days of patience. Patience therein is like grasping a burning coal. For the worker therein is the reward of fifty. Fifty of you.""**"
"12217","Hudhayfah, may Allah be pleased with him, said to accustom yourselves to patience, for trials are about to descend upon you, though no trial striking you will be more severe than what befell the Companions alongside the Messenger of Allah ﷺ."
"12218","ʿAbd Allah, meaning Ibn Masʿud, may Allah be pleased with him, reports that he heard the Messenger of Allah ﷺ declare that a time will surely come upon them in which a man will be envied for lightness of burden just as today he is envied for abundance of wealth and children, until one of them will pass by the grave of his brother and roll in the dust as an animal rolls in its pasture and say that he wishes he were in his place, not from longing for Allah nor from righteous deeds he sent forth but because of the trials that befell him. > **""There will surely come upon you a time in which a man will be envied for lightness of burden just as today he is envied for abundance of wealth and children, until one of you will pass by the grave of his brother and roll in the dust as the animal rolls in its pasture and say: Would that I were in your place. It is not from longing for Allah nor from righteous deeds he sent forth, but from the trials that befell him.""**"
"12219","From Ibn Masʿud, may Allah be pleased with him, it is reported that he said a time will surely come upon them when a man will pass by a grave and say that he wishes he were in the place of its occupant, not from love of meeting Allah but from the severity of trials he sees, and when asked what is best then he said a strong horse and strong weapons by which a man may move wherever circumstances shift."
"12220","Abu Dharr, may Allah be pleased with him, reports that the Messenger of Allah ﷺ asked him how he would be when among worthless remnants of people, interlacing his fingers, and when Abu Dharr asked what he commanded him to do the Prophet ﷺ twice commanded patience, outward social conduct according to their manners, and opposition to them in deeds. > **""O Abu Dharr, how will you be when you are among worthless remnants of people? Be patient, be patient. Deal with people according to their manners, but oppose them in deeds.""**"
"12221","Thawban, may Allah be pleased with him, reports from the Messenger of Allah ﷺ that whoever innovates an evil matter, shelters an innovator, ascribes himself to other than his father, or allies himself with other than his patrons bears the curse of Allah, the angels, and all people with neither obligatory nor voluntary deeds accepted from him, and that the Prophet ﷺ then asked how they would fare among people whose covenants and trusts were entangled and who had become worthless remnants, interlacing his fingers, and when asked what to do commanded patience twice, outward conduct according to their manners, and opposition to them in deeds. > **""Whoever innovates a matter, or shelters an innovator, or ascribes himself to other than his father, or allies himself with other than his patrons, upon him is the curse of Allah, the angels, and all people. Neither obligatory nor voluntary deeds are accepted from him. How will you fare among people whose covenants and trusts have become entangled and who have become worthless remnants? Be patient, be patient. Deal with people according to their manners, but oppose them in deeds.""**"
"12222","Abu Hurayrah, may Allah be pleased with him, reports that the Messenger of Allah ﷺ addressed ʿAbd Allah ibn ʿAmr about remaining among worthless remnants, and when asked what that condition would be described it as the time when trusts and covenants become entangled until people become like interlaced fingers, which he illustrated by interlacing his fingers, and when asked what to do instructed him to act upon what he recognizes, abandon what he rejects, attend to his private self, and leave the common masses. > **""How will you be, O ʿAbd Allah ibn ʿAmr, when you are among worthless remnants of people? That is when their trusts and covenants become entangled and they become like this. Act upon what you recognize, abandon what you reject, attend to your private self, and leave the common masses.""**"
"12223","ʿUmar ibn al-Khattab, may Allah be pleased with him, reports that the Prophet ﷺ declared that people will be sifted until they remain as worthless remnants whose covenants are entangled and whose trusts are ruined, and when one of them asked what they should then do the Prophet ﷺ instructed them to act upon what they recognize, abandon what they reject, and say: One, One, help us against whoever wronged us and suffice us against whoever transgresses against us. > **""You will surely be sifted until you remain as worthless remnants of people whose covenants are entangled and whose trusts are ruined. Act upon what you recognize, abandon what you reject, and say: One, One, help us against whoever wronged us and suffice us against whoever transgresses against us.""**"
"12224","Abu Hurayrah, may Allah be pleased with him, reports from the Prophet ﷺ, and ʿAbd Allah ibn Masʿud, may Allah be pleased with him, reports that the Messenger of Allah ﷺ declared that after him there will be selfish preference and matters they will reject, and when asked what he commands whoever encounters that he instructed them to render the duty incumbent upon them and ask Allah for what belongs to them. > **""After me there will be selfish preference and matters you will reject. Render the right incumbent upon you, and ask Allah for what belongs to you.""**"
"12225","Umm Salamah, may Allah be pleased with her, reports that she heard the Messenger of Allah ﷺ declare that a time will surely come upon people in which the truthful will be belied, the liar believed, the trustworthy deemed treacherous, the treacherous trusted, a man will testify though not asked for testimony, a man will swear though not asked for an oath, and the happiest of people with the world will be the despicable son of the despicable who does not believe in Allah and His Messenger. > **""There will surely come upon people a time in which the truthful will be belied, the liar believed, the trustworthy deemed treacherous, the treacherous trusted, a man will testify though not asked for testimony, a man will swear though not asked for an oath, and the happiest of people with the world will be the despicable son of the despicable who does not believe in Allah and His Messenger.""**"
"12226","Anas ibn Malik, may Allah be pleased with him, reports that the Messenger of Allah ﷺ declared that before the Antichrist there will be deceitful years in which the truthful will be belied, the liar believed, the trustworthy deemed treacherous, the treacherous trusted, and the Ruwaybidah will speak, and when asked what the Ruwaybidah are he said the corrupt man speaking about public affairs. > **""Before the Antichrist there are deceitful years. The truthful will be belied therein, the liar believed therein, the trustworthy deemed treacherous therein, the treacherous trusted therein, and the Ruwaybidah will speak therein. The corrupt man speaking about public affairs.""**"
"12227","ʿAmr ibn ʿAwf, may Allah be pleased with him, reports that the Messenger of Allah ﷺ declared that before the Hour there will be deceitful years in which the liar will be believed, the truthful belied, the treacherous trusted, the trustworthy deemed treacherous, and the Ruwaybidah will speak, and when asked what the Ruwaybidah is he said the contemptible man speaking about public affairs. > **""Before the Hour there are deceitful years. The liar will be believed therein, the truthful belied therein, the treacherous trusted therein, the trustworthy deemed treacherous therein, and the Ruwaybidah will speak therein. The contemptible man speaking about public affairs.""**"
"12228","Ibn Isḥaq reports that ʿAbd Allah ibn Dinar narrated to him from Anas, may Allah be pleased with him, from the Prophet ﷺ a similar narration concerning the deceitful years."
"12229","Anas, may Allah be pleased with him, reports that the Messenger of Allah ﷺ declared that among the signs of the Hour are obscenity, foul speech, severing kinship, deeming the trustworthy treacherous, and trusting the treacherous. > **""Among the signs of the Hour are obscenity, foul speech, severing kinship, deeming the trustworthy treacherous, and trusting the treacherous.""**"
"12230","Abu Sabrah, whose name is Salim ibn Sabrah, reports that he met ʿAbd Allah ibn ʿAmr, may Allah be pleased with him, who narrated what he heard from the Messenger of Allah ﷺ and dictated it to him so that he wrote it in his own hand without adding or omitting a letter, namely that Allah does not love obscenity, or hates the obscene and foul-mouthed person, and that the Hour will not come until obscenity, foul speech, severing kinship, and evil neighbourhood appear, and until the treacherous is trusted and the trustworthy deemed treacherous. > **""Allah does not love obscenity, or hates the obscene and foul-mouthed person, and the Hour will not come until obscenity, foul speech, severing kinship, and evil neighbourhood appear, and until the treacherous is trusted and the trustworthy deemed treacherous.""**"
"12231","Hudhayfah, may Allah be pleased with him, reports that the Messenger of Allah ﷺ declared that a time will come upon people in which they will wish for the Antichrist, and when Hudhayfah asked at the cost of his father and mother what the cause would be the Prophet ﷺ said it would be from the successive hardships they encounter. > **""There will come upon people a time in which they will wish for the Antichrist. From the hardship upon hardship they encounter.""**"
"12232","Abu Umamah al-Bahili, may Allah be pleased with him, reports from the Prophet ﷺ that people are like a tree bearing fruit and will soon become like a thorny tree, such that if you contend with them they contend with you, if you leave them they will not leave you, and if you flee from them they seek you, and when asked about the way out the Prophet ﷺ said to lend them ones honour for the day of ones need. > **""People are a tree bearing fruit, and they will soon become a tree bearing thorns. If you contend with them they will contend with you, if you leave them they will not leave you, and if you flee from them they will seek you. Lend them your honour for the day of your need.""**"
"12233","Abu Umamah, may Allah be pleased with him, reports that he heard the Messenger of Allah ﷺ declare that affairs will only increase in severity, wealth will only increase in abundance, people will only increase in miserliness, and the Hour will come only upon the worst of people. > **""Affairs will only increase in severity, wealth will only increase in abundance, people will only increase in miserliness, and the Hour will come only upon the worst of people.""**"
"12234","Ibn Masʿud, may Allah be pleased with him, said that they live in a time when prayer is long, the sermon short, scholars many, and orators few, but a time will come upon people when prayer will be short, the sermon long, orators many, and scholars few, when prayers will be delayed until the late afternoon prayer is performed when the light of the dead has dimmed, so whoever of them encounters that should perform prayer at its proper time and treat his prayer with them as voluntary, and that they live in a time when a man is envied for few dependents and light burden, and he leaves nothing after him among his family dearer to him than their death nor any household more beloved, loving them as people love their families."
"12235","Muhammad ibn Zayd ibn Khulaydah reports that ʿAbd Allah, may Allah be pleased with him, entered upon him while he had arranged goods in his house, so ʿAbd Allah told him to lighten the furnishings of his house, for people will soon be in a state of turmoil."
"12236","Khaythamah reports that ʿAbd Allah, meaning Ibn Masʿud, may Allah be pleased with him, asked his wife whether today is better or yesterday, that she said she did not know, and that he replied he knew that yesterday was better than today, today better than tomorrow, and so it will continue until the Hour comes."
"12237","Anas ibn Malik, may Allah be pleased with him, reports that the Messenger of Allah ﷺ declared that even if the believer were inside the hole of a lizard, one who harms him would be sent to him therein, or a hypocrite who harms him. > **""If the believer were inside the hole of a lizard, one who harms him would surely be sent to him therein, or a hypocrite who harms him.""**"
"12238","Hudhayfah, may Allah be pleased with him, reports that he asked the Prophet ﷺ when enjoining good and forbidding evil, the two masters of the deeds of the righteous, would be abandoned, that the Prophet ﷺ said when there befalls them what befell the Children of Israil, and when asked what befell the Children of Israil he said when their best flatter their wicked, knowledge passes to their worst, and authority passes to their youngest, whereupon trials envelop them which advance and are advanced against them. > **""When there befalls you what befell the Children of Israil. When your best flatter your wicked, knowledge passes to your worst, and authority passes to your youngest, then trials will envelop you which advance and are advanced against you.""**"
"12239","Muʿadh ibn Jabal, may Allah be pleased with him, reports that the Messenger of Allah ﷺ declared that in the last age there will be people who are brothers in public and enemies in secret, and when asked how that would be he said through the desire of some toward others and the fear of some toward others. > **""In the last age there will be people who are brothers in public and enemies in secret. Through the desire of some of them toward others and the fear of some of them toward others.""**"
"12240","Ibn ʿAbbas, may Allah be pleased with him, reports that the Messenger of Allah ﷺ declared that in the last age there will come people whose faces are the faces of men and whose hearts are the hearts of devils, like predatory wolves, with no mercy in their hearts, shedding blood, never refraining from vileness, such that if you follow them they conceal you, if you hide from them they backbite you, if they speak to you they lie to you, if you trust them they betray you, their child insolent, their youth cunning, their elder neither enjoining good nor forbidding evil, honour through them being disgrace, seeking what is in their hands being poverty, the forbearing among them astray, the one enjoining good among them accused, the believer among them weak, the corrupt among them honoured, the practice among them innovation and innovation among them practice, whereupon Allah will set their worst over them and their best will supplicate without being answered. > **""In the last age there will come people whose faces are the faces of men and whose hearts are the hearts of devils, like predatory wolves. There is nothing of mercy in their hearts. They shed blood and do not refrain from vileness. If you follow them they conceal you, if you hide from them they backbite you, if they speak to you they lie to you, and if you trust them they betray you. Their child is insolent, their youth is cunning, and their elder neither enjoins good nor forbids evil. Honour through them is disgrace, seeking what is in their hands is poverty, the forbearing among them is astray, the one enjoining good among them is accused, the believer among them is weak, the corrupt among them is honoured, the practice among them is innovation and innovation among them is practice. Then Allah will set their worst over them, and their best will supplicate without being answered.""**"
"12241","Salman, may Allah be pleased with him, reports that the Messenger of Allah ﷺ declared that when speech becomes prominent, deeds are hoarded, tongues differ, hearts hate one another, and every kinsman severs his kinship, then Allah curses them, deafens them, and blinds their sight. > **""When speech becomes prominent, deeds are hoarded, tongues differ, hearts hate one another, and every kinsman severs his kinship, then Allah curses them, deafens them, and blinds their sight.""**"
"12242","Anas ibn Malik, may Allah be pleased with him, reports that the Messenger of Allah ﷺ foretold a time when people will be like wolves, devouring whoever fails to be a wolf like them. > **""There will come upon people a time when they are wolves, so whoever is not a wolf will be devoured by the wolves.""**"
"12243","Abu Hurayrah, may Allah be pleased with him, reports that he heard the Messenger of Allah ﷺ declare that a time will come upon people in which a man will be given choice between helplessness and sin, so whoever encounters that time should choose helplessness over sin. > **""There will come upon people a time in which a man will be given choice between helplessness and sin, so whoever encounters that time should choose helplessness over sin.""**"
"12244","Abu Hurayrah, may Allah be pleased with him, reports that he heard the Messenger of Allah ﷺ say to Thawban, may Allah be pleased with him, asking how he would be when nations summon one another against them as diners summon one another to a bowl of food from which they take, that Thawban asked at the cost of his father and mother whether it would be from fewness, that the Prophet ﷺ said they would then be many but weakness would be cast into their hearts, and when asked what weakness is he said their love of the world and hatred of fighting. > **""How will you be, O Thawban, when nations summon one another against you as diners summon one another to a bowl of food from which they take? No, you will then be many, but weakness will be cast into your hearts. Your love of the world and your hatred of fighting.""**"
"12245","Muʿawiyah, may Allah be pleased with him, addressed the people of Syria saying that an Ansari, whom Shuʿbah identified as Zayd ibn Arqam, may Allah be pleased with him, narrated to him that the Messenger of Allah ﷺ declared that a party of his nation will remain manifest upon the truth, and Muʿawiyah added that he hoped they, O people of Syria, are them. > **""A party of my nation will remain manifest upon the truth.""**"
"12246","Jabir ibn Samurah, may Allah be pleased with him, reports that he was informed that the Prophet ﷺ declared that this religion will remain established while a band of Muslims fights for it until the Hour comes. > **""This religion will remain established while a band of Muslims fights for it until the Hour comes.""**"
"12247","In another wording from Jabir ibn Samurah, may Allah be pleased with him, through one who narrated to him from the Messenger of Allah ﷺ, the same meaning as the preceding report was related, corresponding to what is established in the sound collection from Jabir ibn Samurah himself."
"12248","Abu Umamah, may Allah be pleased with him, reports that the Messenger of Allah ﷺ declared that a party of his nation will remain manifest upon religion, dominant over its enemy, unharmed by those who oppose them except for hardship that befalls them, until the command of Allah comes to them in that state, and when asked where they are he said at the Sacred House and the environs of the Sacred House. > **""A party of my nation will remain manifest upon religion, dominant over its enemy. Those who oppose them will not harm them except for hardship that befalls them, until the command of Allah comes to them while they are in that state. At the Sacred House and the environs of the Sacred House.""**"
"12249","ʿUmar, may Allah be pleased with him, reports that the Messenger of Allah ﷺ declared that a party of his nation will remain manifest upon the truth until the Hour comes. > **""A party of my nation will remain manifest upon the truth until the Hour comes.""**"
"12250","Jabir, may Allah be pleased with him, reports that the Messenger of Allah ﷺ declared that his nation will remain manifest upon the truth until Jesus son of Mary descends, that their leader will say to advance, that Jesus will say they are more entitled and that some of them are leaders over others as an honour by which this nation was honoured. > **""My nation will remain manifest upon the truth until Jesus son of Mary descends, then their leader will say: Advance. Then he will say: You are more entitled. Some of you are leaders over others, a matter by which this nation was honoured.""**"
"12251","Anas, may Allah be pleased with him, reports that the Messenger of Allah ﷺ declared that men of his nation will reach the time of Jesus son of Mary and witness the battle against the Antichrist. > **""Men of my nation will reach the time of Jesus son of Mary and witness the battle against the Antichrist.""**"
"12252","Abu Hurayrah, may Allah be pleased with him, reports from the Prophet ﷺ that a band of his nation will remain upon this affair, or over this affair, unharmed by the opposition of whoever opposes them until the command of Allah comes to them. > **""A band of my nation will remain upon this affair, unharmed by the opposition of whoever opposes them until the command of Allah comes to them.""**"
"12253","Abu Hurayrah, may Allah be pleased with him, reports from the Messenger of Allah ﷺ that a party of his nation will continue fighting at the gates of Damascus and surroundings and at the gates of the Sacred House and surroundings, unharmed by the abandonment of whoever abandons them, manifest until the Day of Resurrection. > **""A party of my nation will continue fighting at the gates of Damascus and surroundings and at the gates of the Sacred House and surroundings. The abandonment of whoever abandons them will not harm them, manifest until the Day of Resurrection.""**"
"12254","Murrah al-Bahzi, may Allah be pleased with him, reports that he heard the Messenger of Allah ﷺ declare that a party of his nation will remain manifest upon the truth, dominant over whoever opposes them, like a vessel among eaters, until the command of Allah comes while they are in that state, and when asked where they are he said at the environs of the Sacred House, adding that Ramalah is the elevated land comprising east and west. > **""A party of my nation will remain manifest upon the truth, dominant over whoever opposes them, like a vessel among eaters, until the command of Allah comes while they are in that state. At the environs of the Sacred House.""**"
"12255","Jabir ibn ʿAbd Allah, may Allah be pleased with him, reports that the Messenger of Allah ﷺ declared that the throne of Iblis is upon the sea, that he then sends his detachments to tempt people, and that the greatest of them in his estimation is the greatest of them in temptation. > **""The throne of Iblis is upon the sea, then he sends his detachments and they tempt people, and the greatest of them in his estimation is the greatest of them in temptation.""**"
"12256","Jabir, may Allah be pleased with him, reports that the Messenger of Allah ﷺ declared that Allah, Mighty and Majestic, says He avenges Himself upon those He hates through those He hates, then renders all to the Fire. > **""I avenge Myself upon those I hate through those I hate, then I render all to the Fire.""**"
"12257","Jarīr, may Allah be pleased with him, reports that the Messenger of Allah ﷺ spoke about which regions of the earth will hasten most quickly toward ruin. > **""The swiftest parts of the earth to fall into ruin are its left side, then its right side.""**"
"12258","Jubayr ibn Nufayr reports from a man among the Companions of Muḥammad ﷺ, who conveyed from the Prophet ﷺ guidance about the coming opening of Sham and where to settle when choice of dwellings is offered there. > **""Sham will soon be opened for you, so when you are given choice of dwellings therein, you must take a city therein called Damascus, for it is the stronghold of the Muslims in the great battles, and their encampment is from it at a land called al-Ghūṭah.""**"
"12259","Abū al-Dardāʾ, may Allah be pleased with him, reports that the Messenger of Allah ﷺ described a dream in which the pillar of the Book was lifted from beneath his head and he followed it with his gaze. > **""While I was sleeping I saw the pillar of the Book carried away from beneath my head, and I thought it was being taken, so I followed it with my gaze, and it was taken straight to Sham. Indeed, faith, when tribulations occur, will be in Sham.""**"
"12260","Abū Hurayrah, may Allah be pleased with him, reports that Saʿd came to the Prophet ﷺ, and when the Prophet ﷺ saw him he remarked upon goodness in the face of Saʿd, after which mention was made of the killing of Kisrā. > **""May Allah curse Kisrā. Indeed, the first of people to perish are the Arabs, then the people of Persia.""**"
"12261","Abū Hurayrah, may Allah be pleased with him, reports that the Messenger of Allah ﷺ warned the Arabs concerning an evil that has drawn near. > **""Woe to the Arabs from an evil that has drawn near.""**"
"12262","Al-Ḥusayn, meaning the son of ʿAlī, may Allah be pleased with him, conveys, with the narrator knowing it only as from the Prophet ﷺ, a ruling about one who witnesses a matter and dislikes it and one who is absent yet approves of it. > **""Whoever witnesses a matter and dislikes it is like one who was absent from it, and whoever is absent from a matter and approves of it is like one who witnessed it.""**"
"12263","ʿAwn, meaning the son of ʿAbd Allāh ibn ʿUtbah, reports that he said to ʿUmar ibn ʿAbd al-ʿAzīz, may Allah have mercy on him, that Ibn Masʿūd, may Allah be pleased with him, used to say that there will be ambiguous matters, and that whoever is absent from them yet approves of them is like one who witnessed them, while whoever witnesses them yet dislikes them is like one who was absent from them, and this pleased ʿUmar ibn ʿAbd al-ʿAzīz."
"12264","ʿImrān ibn Ḥuṣayn, may Allah be pleased with him, reports a prohibition issued by the Prophet ﷺ concerning commerce at a time of disorder. > **""Do not sell weapons during the tribulation.""**"
"12265","Abū Bakrah, may Allah be pleased with him, reports that the Messenger of Allah ﷺ came upon people passing an unsheathed sword among themselves, censured the act, reminded them of his prior prohibition, and then instructed how one should hand a sword to his brother. > **""May Allah curse whoever does this. Have I not forbidden this? When one of you draws his sword and looks at it, then wishes to hand it to his brother, let him sheathe it first, then hand it to him.""**"
"12266","Bannah al-Juhanī, may Allah be pleased with him, reports that the Prophet of Allah ﷺ passed by people in the mosque, or in a gathering, who were drawing a sword among themselves and passing it unsheathed, whereupon he cursed such conduct, referred to his prior deterrence, and commanded sheathing before giving. > **""May Allah curse whoever does that. If I had not already restrained you from this, then when you draw the sword, a man must sheathe it, then give it in that state.""**"
"12267","Jābir, may Allah be pleased with him, reports that the Messenger of Allah ﷺ passed by people in a gathering who were drawing a sword and passing it among themselves unsheathed, questioned them about his prior restraint, and commanded sheathing before handing to a brother. > **""Did I not restrain you from this? When one of you draws the sword, let him sheathe it, then give it to his brother.""**"
"12268","Abū Mūsā, may Allah be pleased with him, reports that the Messenger of Allah ﷺ commanded those passing through markets while carrying arrows to grasp them in a safe manner. > **""Whoever among you passes through these markets while carrying arrows, let him grasp them by their heads.""**"
"12269","Abū Bakr, may Allah be pleased with him, reports that the Messenger of Allah ﷺ described the continued cursing by angels upon a Muslim who draws a weapon against his brother until he puts it away from him. > **""When a Muslim draws a weapon against his brother, the angels of Allah continue to curse him until he puts it away from him.""**"
"12270","Samurah, may Allah be pleased with him, reports a prohibition the Messenger of Allah ﷺ used to issue regarding conduct between Muslims. > **""A Muslim must not draw a weapon against a Muslim.""**"
"12271","ʿAmr ibn ʿAwf, may Allah be pleased with him, reports that the Messenger of Allah ﷺ disavowed one who draws weapons against the community. > **""Whoever draws weapons against us is not of us.""**"
"12272","Ibn al-Zubayr, may Allah be pleased with him, reports from the Prophet ﷺ a disavowal of one who carries weapons against the community. > **""One who carries weapons against us is not of us.""**"
"12273","Ibn ʿAbbās, may Allah be pleased with him, reports from the Prophet ﷺ a disavowal of one who bears arms against the community. > **""Whoever bears weapons against us is not of us.""**"
"12274","Sahl ibn Saʿd al-Sāʿidī, may Allah be pleased with him, reports that the Messenger of Allah ﷺ forbade brandishing the sword at a brother because Satan may cause the hand to slip and cast the doer into a pit of Hell. > **""Let none of you brandish the sword at his brother, for perhaps Satan will stir his hand so that he falls into a pit from the pits of the Fire.""**"
"12275","ʿAlqamah ibn Abī ʿAlqamah reports in a story from his brother, who said he heard ʿĀʾishah, may Allah be pleased with her, say that she heard the Messenger of Allah ﷺ declare the gravity of pointing a sharp iron implement at a Muslim with intent to kill. > **""Whoever points at anyone among the Muslims with a piece of iron, intending to kill him, his blood has become due.""**"
"12276","Abū Hurayrah, may Allah be pleased with him, reports that the Messenger of Allah ﷺ disavowed one who shoots arrows at the community. > **""Whoever shoots arrows at us is not of us.""**"
"12277","Buraydah, may Allah be pleased with him, reports that the Prophet ﷺ disavowed one who shoots at the community by night. > **""Whoever shoots at us by night is not of us.""**"
"12278","ʿAbd Allāh ibn Jaʿfar, may Allah be pleased with him, reports that the Prophet ﷺ disavowed night shooting against the community and declared void the blood of one who sleeps on a roof without a wall and then falls and dies. > **""Whoever shoots at us by night is not of us, and whoever sleeps on a roof that has no wall and then falls and dies, his blood is void.""**"
"12279","Abū Hurayrah, may Allah be pleased with him, reports that the Prophet ﷺ disavowed one who shoots at the community by night. > **""Whoever shoots at us by night is not of us.""**"
"12280","Jābir ibn ʿAbd Allāh, may Allah be pleased with him, reports that the Messenger of Allah ﷺ described the worst slain as one killed between two ranks when the fight is for kingship. > **""The worst slain between two ranks is one who seeks kingship.""**"
"12281","Tharwān ibn Milḥān reports that while they were sitting in the mosque, ʿAmmār ibn Yāsir, may Allah be pleased with him, passed by them, they asked him to narrate what he heard from the Messenger of Allah ﷺ, and he described a people after the Prophet ﷺ seizing kingship and killing one another for it, and when they doubted any other narrator, he affirmed it will occur. > **""After me there will be a people who seize kingship, and some of them will kill others for it.""**"
"12282","Yaḥyā ibn Ḥibbān reports that he was with ʿAbd Allāh ibn ʿUmar, may Allah be pleased with him, and that ʿAbd Allāh ibn ʿUmar said to him that during the tribulation people will consider killing as nothing."
"12283","Anas ibn Mālik, may Allah be pleased with him, reports that the Messenger of Allah ﷺ promised Paradise for one who avoids four matters connected with blood, wealth, private parts, and drinks. > **""Whoever avoids four things will enter Paradise: blood, wealth, private parts, and drinks.""**"
"12284","ʿUqbah ibn Khālid al-Laythī, may Allah be pleased with him, reports that the Messenger of Allah ﷺ sent an expedition which raided a people, a man from the enemy fled, a man from the expedition followed him with drawn sword, the pursued man twice declared himself Muslim yet was struck and killed, the report reached the Messenger of Allah ﷺ who spoke severely about it, the killer addressed the Prophet ﷺ during a sermon three times claiming the declaration was only to avoid death, the Prophet ﷺ turned away from him twice, then faced him with displeasure visible on his face. > **""Indeed Allah, mighty and exalted, has refused me permission to kill a believer.""**"
"12285","Jundab ibn Sufyān, may Allah be pleased with him, reports that while he was with the Messenger of Allah ﷺ a bearer of glad tidings came from an expedition the Prophet ﷺ had sent, informing of Allah's help and victory, after which the Prophet ﷺ warned of coming tribulations in which a man's faith will shift between morning and evening, advised staying in homes and obscurity, commanded restraint of the hand and choosing to be the slain servant rather than the slaying servant, and described one who consumes his brother's wealth, sheds his blood, disobeys his Lord, and disbelieves in his Creator as deserving Hell. > **""There will be after me tribulations like portions of the dark night, colliding like the colliding of camels and mighty bulls; a man will be Muslim in the morning and disbeliever in the evening, and Muslim in the evening and disbeliever in the morning. Enter your houses and obscure your mention. Let him withhold his hand and be the slain servant of Allah, and not be the slaying servant of Allah, for a man will be within the dome of Islam, then he consumes his brother's wealth, sheds his blood, disobeys his Lord, disbelieves in his Creator, and Hell becomes due for him.""**"
"12286","Abū ʿImrān reports that he told Jundab, may Allah be pleased with him, that he had pledged allegiance to those with Ibn al-Zubayr and that they wished him to go with them to Sham, Jundab told him to hold back, he said they refused, Jundab told him to ransom himself with wealth, he said they insisted on his striking with the sword alongside them, so Jundab narrated from someone that the Messenger of Allah ﷺ described the slain bringing his killer on the Day of Resurrection to question him before the Lord about kingship, and Jundab warned him to avoid it. > **""The slain will come with his killer on the Day of Resurrection and will say: O Lord, ask this one for what he killed me. On what did you kill him? And he will say: I killed him for the kingship of so-and-so.""**"
"12287","ʿUbādah ibn Qurṣ, may Allah be pleased with him, reports that a Muslim charged a disbeliever and stabbed him with a spear, the wounded man turned and declared himself Muslim, yet the attacker killed him, the matter was mentioned to the Messenger of Allah ﷺ who questioned the killing and turned away, then declared refusal of permission concerning one who kills a Muslim. > **""Did you kill him after he said: I am a Muslim? He has refused me permission concerning one who kills a Muslim.""**"
"12288","Al-Ḥasan, may Allah have mercy on him, reports that when the killer died his people buried him but the earth cast him out three times, so they threw him between two mountain slopes, covered him with stones, and wild beasts devoured him, and Ibn Abī al-Zinād reports that it reached him that when the Messenger of Allah ﷺ was informed that the earth had cast him out, he explained the divine intent behind the sign. > **""Indeed the earth accepts one worse than him, but Allah intended to show you the gravity of blood in His sight.""**"
"12289","Abū Hurayrah and Abū Saʿīd, may Allah be pleased with them, report that the Messenger of Allah ﷺ delivered a sermon in which he asked what day it was, they answered that it was a sacred day, and he then likened the inviolability of blood and wealth to the sanctity of that day, month, and city. > **""What day is this? Indeed your blood and your wealth are sacred to you like the sacredness of this day of yours, in this month of yours, in this city of yours.""**"
"12290","ʿAmmār ibn Yāsir, may Allah be pleased with him, reports that the Messenger of Allah ﷺ addressed them, asked in succession what day, month, and city this was, they answered the Day of Sacrifice, Dhū al-Ḥijjah as a sacred month, and a sacred city, and he then likened blood, wealth, and honour to that sanctity and commanded the present to convey to the absent. > **""What day is this? What month is this? What city is this? Indeed your blood, your wealth, and your honour are sacred to you like the sacredness of this day of yours, in this month of yours, in this city of yours. Let the witness convey to the absent.""**"
"12291","Al-Barāʾ and Zayd ibn Arqam, may Allah be pleased with them, report that they heard the Messenger of Allah ﷺ liken the inviolability of blood and wealth to the sanctity of the present day, month, and city. > **""Indeed your blood and your wealth are sacred to you like the sacredness of this day of yours, in this month of yours, in this city of yours.""**"
"12292","Abū Hurayrah, may Allah be pleased with him, reports that the Messenger of Allah ﷺ described the departure of faith from one who kills, commits adultery, drinks wine, or seizes spoils while engaged in the act, likened it to removing a shirt, and described its return upon return to faith. > **""The killer does not kill while he is a believer, nor does the adulterer commit adultery while he is a believer, nor does the drinker drink wine while he is a believer, nor does the plunderer plunder while he is a believer; faith is stripped from him as one removes his shirt, then when he returns to faith, faith returns to him.""**"
"12293","ʿAbd Allāh, meaning Ibn Masʿūd, may Allah be pleased with him, reports that the Prophet ﷺ forbade returning after him to disbelief expressed through striking one another's necks. > **""Do not return after me as disbelievers, some of you striking the necks of others.""**"
"12294","Al-Ṣunābiḥī, may Allah be pleased with him, reports that the Messenger of Allah ﷺ spoke of multiplying the nations through his followers and warned against returning after him to disbelief expressed through mutual killing. > **""I will multiply the nations through you, so do not return after me as disbelievers, some of you striking the necks of others.""**"
"12295","Jābir ibn ʿAbd Allāh, may Allah be pleased with him, reports that the Messenger of Allah ﷺ reminded them of their present adherence to religion, of his boasting through their numbers, and warned against retreating after him. > **""Today you are upon a religion, and I will multiply the nations through you, so do not walk backward after me.""**"
"12296","Anas, may Allah be pleased with him, reports from the Prophet ﷺ a warning addressed to his Companions against being found after him in a state of mutual neck-striking. > **""Let me not find you returning after me as disbelievers, some of you striking the necks of others.""**"
"12297","Usāmah ibn Zayd, may Allah be pleased with him, reports that the Messenger of Allah ﷺ forbade returning after him to killing one another. > **""Do not return after me as disbelievers, some of you striking the necks of others.""**"
"12298","ʿUbādah ibn al-Ṣāmit, may Allah be pleased with him, reports from the Prophet ﷺ that Allah may forgive every sin except death in polytheism and deliberate killing of a believer. > **""Every sin may be such that Allah forgives it, except one who dies as a polytheist or kills a believer deliberately.""**"
"12299","ʿĀmir al-Shaʿbī reports that when Marwān fought al-Ḍaḥḥāk ibn Qays he sent to Ayman ibn Khuraym al-Asadī asking him to fight alongside him, Ayman replied that his father and uncle witnessed Badr and enjoined him never to fight one who testifies that none is worthy of worship but Allah, and that he would fight only if brought an exemption from the Fire, Marwān dismissed and reviled him, so Ayman composed verses declaring he will not fight a praying man for the rule of another Qurashī lord, that the ruler gains his rule while the sin remains upon the fighter, seeking refuge in Allah from ignorance and recklessness, and questioning what benefit remains in fighting a Muslim without cause for as long as he lives."
"12300","Abū Saʿīd, may Allah be pleased with him, reports that when a slain man was found in the time of the Messenger of Allah ﷺ, the Prophet ﷺ ascended to preach, asked three times whether they knew who killed the slain among them, they answered they did not, and he then swore by the One in whose Hand is the soul of Muḥammad ﷺ concerning collective conspiracy to kill a believer and hatred of the People of the House. > **""Do you not know who killed this slain one among you? By the One in whose Hand is the soul of Muḥammad, if the people of the heavens and the people of the earth gathered to kill a believer, Allah would enter them all into Hell, and none hates us, the People of the House, except that Allah casts him into the Fire.""**"
"12301","Ibn ʿAbbās, may Allah be pleased with him, reports that when a slain man was found in the time of the Messenger of Allah ﷺ without his killer being known, the Prophet ﷺ ascended his pulpit, expressed astonishment that a killing occurred among them while he was present yet its doer was unknown, and warned of boundless torment for collective killing of a Muslim. > **""O people, is a slain one killed while I am among you and it is not known who killed him? If the people of heaven and earth gathered to kill a Muslim, Allah would torment them without number and without reckoning.""**"
"12302","Abū Bakrah, may Allah be pleased with him, reports from the Prophet ﷺ a warning that collective agreement to kill a Muslim results in being cast face-first into the Fire. > **""If the people of the heavens and the earth gathered to kill a Muslim, Allah would cast them all upon their faces into the Fire.""**"
"12303","Abū Hurayrah, may Allah be pleased with him, reports from the Prophet ﷺ that collective agreement to kill a believer results in being cast into the Fire. > **""If the people of heaven and earth gathered to kill a believer, Allah would cast them into the Fire.""**"
"12304","ʿAbd Allāh ibn ʿUmar, may Allah be pleased with him, reports that he heard the Messenger of Allah ﷺ declare the distinct ends of one who walks to another and kills him. > **""When a man walks to a man and kills him, the slain is in Paradise and the killer is in the Fire.""**"
"12305","ʿAbd Allāh ibn Masʿūd, may Allah be pleased with him, reports from the Messenger of Allah ﷺ a scene in which the slain comes holding his killer with his jugular veins gushing blood before the Possessor of Might, asking for inquiry, and the killer confesses killing for another man's power, whereupon it is declared that might belongs to Allah. > **""The slain will come holding his killer, with his jugular veins gushing blood, before the Possessor of Might, and will say: O Lord, ask this one for what he killed me. For what did you kill him? He will say: I killed him so that might would belong to so-and-so. It will be said: It belongs to Allah.""**"
"12306","Ibn ʿAbbās, may Allah be pleased with him, reports that a questioner asked him whether there is repentance for a killer, he expressed astonishment and repeated the question twice or thrice, then narrated that he heard their Prophet ﷺ describe the slain coming with his head held in one hand and his killer seized in the other, with veins gushing blood, until brought to the Throne, where the slain complains and Allah condemns the killer to ruin and orders him to the Fire. > **""The slain will come with his head attached in one of his hands, seizing his killer with the other hand, with his jugular veins gushing blood, until he brings him to the Throne. The slain will say to the Lord of the worlds: This one killed me. Allah will say to the killer: May you perish. And he will be taken to the Fire.""**"
"12307","Abū Hurayrah, may Allah be pleased with him, reports from the Prophet ﷺ a thrice-repeated statement distinguishing the killing of a Muslim from all other harmless matters. > **""There is no harm except in killing a Muslim.""**"
"12308","Jundab ibn ʿAbd Allāh, may Allah be pleased with him, reports that the Messenger of Allah ﷺ warned that even a handful of blood shed like slaughtering a chicken will block every gate of Paradise, and enjoined keeping only wholesome food in the belly since the belly is the first part of man to decay. > **""Whoever is able not to place between himself and Paradise even a handful of blood which he sheds, as though slaughtering a chicken, whenever he approaches a gate of Paradise it will stand between him and it, and whoever among you is able not to place in his belly except what is wholesome, for the first part of man to decay is his belly.""**"
"12309","Al-Ḥasan reports from Jundab, may Allah be pleased with him, that he sat with him during the rule of al-Muṣʿab, Jundab said these people have plunged into their blood, allied over worldly life, and built tall buildings, and he swore by Allah that only a short time would pass before a sturdy camel with its ropes and saddle would be dearer to one of them than a great mansion, then he mentioned a similar narration."
"12310","ʿAbd al-Malik ibn Marwān reports that before assuming rule he used to sit with Barīrah in Madīnah, may Allah be pleased with her, she said she saw qualities in him suggesting he would govern this nation and warned him against bloodshed, saying she heard the Messenger of Allah ﷺ describe a man pushed back from the gate of Paradise from even looking at it because of cupped blood shed from a Muslim without right. > **""A man will be pushed back from the gate of Paradise from looking at it because of a cupping-glass of blood which he sheds from a Muslim without right.""**"
"12311","Abū Umāmah, may Allah be pleased with him, reports from the Prophet ﷺ that Allah did not permit in tribulation anything He had forbidden before it, and questioned how one greets his brother and later kills him. > **""Indeed Allah, mighty and exalted, did not permit in tribulation anything He had forbidden before it. What is the matter with one of you that he comes to his brother and greets him, then comes after that and kills him?""**"
"12312","ʿAbd Allāh, meaning Ibn Masʿūd, may Allah be pleased with him, said that a man remains in relief within his religion so long as he does not strike unlawful blood, but when he strikes unlawful blood, modesty is removed from him."
"12313","In another wording, the servants remain in relief under the cover of Allah, mighty and exalted, so long as they maintain worship and do not shed unlawful blood."
"12314","ʿAbd Allāh, meaning Ibn Masʿūd, may Allah be pleased with him, raises the report that the Prophet ﷺ warned against admiring blood-stained forearms and wealth earned unlawfully, describing an undying killer appointed by Allah, rejected spending, unblessed retention, and provision toward Hell. > **""Let not the wide forearms stained with blood please you, for with Allah there is for him a killer who does not die, and let not a man please you who earns wealth unlawfully, for if he spends from it, it will not be accepted from him, if he withholds it, it will not be blessed for him, and if he dies leaving it, it will be his provision to the Fire.""**"
"12315","Ibn ʿAbbās, may Allah be pleased with him, reports that the Messenger of Allah ﷺ warned that one who shares in unlawful blood even with half a word will come on Resurrection with despair of mercy written between his eyes. > **""Whoever shares in unlawful blood with even half a word will come on the Day of Resurrection with written between his eyes: Despairing of the mercy of Allah.""**"
"12316","Ibn Masʿūd, may Allah be pleased with him, said that when people fall into tribulation and tell one to go forth claiming others as precedent, one should reply that there is no precedent for him in evil."
"12317","Ḥumayd ibn Hilāl reports that when tribulation erupted, ʿImrān ibn Ḥuṣayn, may Allah be pleased with him, told Ḥujayr ibn al-Rabīʿ al-ʿAdawī to go to his people and forbid them from the tribulation, Ḥujayr replied that he was held in low regard among them and would not be obeyed, ʿImrān told him to convey the message on his behalf and forbid them, and the narrator heard ʿImrān swear by Allah that being a black Abyssinian servant herding goats on a mountaintop until death would be dearer to him than shooting an arrow at either of two ranks, whether it hit or missed."
"12318","Ibn Sīrīn reports that when Saʿd ibn Abī Waqqās, may Allah be pleased with him, was asked why he did not fight, since he was among the people of consultation and most entitled to the matter, he replied that he would not fight until brought a sword possessing eyes, tongue, and lips that could distinguish believer from disbeliever, affirming that he had struggled and knew struggle."
"12319","ʿAbd Allāh ibn ʿAmr, may Allah be pleased with him, reports that the Messenger of Allah ﷺ spoke of three most wretched people, naming the hamstringer of the she-camel of Thamūd and the son of Adam who killed his brother and bears a share of every blood shed on earth as the first to institute killing, while the third was omitted, apparently the killer of ʿAlī ibn Abī Ṭālib as indicated elsewhere. > **""The most wretched of people are three: the hamstringer of the she-camel of Thamūd, and the son of Adam who killed his brother; no blood is shed on earth except that a share of it reaches him, because he was the first to institute killing.""**"
"12320","Marthad ibn ʿAbd Allāh al-Yazanī reports from a man among the Companions of the Prophet ﷺ that the Messenger of Allah ﷺ was asked about the killer and the one who commands killing, and he described the division of the Fire into seventy parts between them. > **""I have divided the Fire into seventy parts: for the commander sixty-nine, and for the killer one part, and it suffices him.""**"
"12321","Abū Saʿīd al-Khudrī, may Allah be pleased with him, reports that the Messenger of Allah ﷺ described the division of the Fire into seventy portions between the commander and the killer. > **""Indeed Allah has divided the Fire into seventy portions: sixty-nine for the commander and one portion for the killer, and it suffices him.""**"
"12322","Abū al-Dardāʾ, may Allah be pleased with him, reports from the Prophet ﷺ a scene in which killer and slain are brought on Resurrection, the slain asks inquiry, the killer blames one who commanded him, and both are seized and cast into the Fire. > **""The killer and the slain will be brought on the Day of Resurrection, and he will say: O Lord, ask this one for what he killed me. And he will say: O Lord, this one commanded me. Both will be seized by their hands together and cast into the Fire.""**"
"12323","Abū al-Dardāʾ, may Allah be pleased with him, reports that the Prophet ﷺ described the slain sitting on the roadway, seizing the killer as he passes, complaining that his fasting and prayer were cut off, whereupon both killer and commander are tormented. > **""The slain will sit on the roadway, and when the killer passes him he will seize him and say: O Lord, this one cut off my fasting and my prayer. The killer and the one who commanded him will be tormented.""**"
"12324","Kharashah ibn al-Ḥurr, may Allah be pleased with him, who was among the Companions of the Prophet ﷺ, reports from the Prophet ﷺ a warning against witnessing a killing lest the victim be wronged and divine anger afflict the witness. > **""Let none of you witness a slain one, for perhaps he was killed wrongfully, and anger afflicts him.""**"
"12325","Kharashah ibn al-Ḥurr, may Allah be pleased with him, reports that he heard the Messenger of Allah ﷺ describe the coming tribulation in which the sleeper is better than the wakeful and the seated better than the striver, and command breaking the sword upon rock and lying down until it clears. > **""After me there will be a tribulation in which the sleeper is better than the wakeful, and the seated is better than the striver. Whoever encounters it, let him walk with his sword to a rock and strike it until it breaks, then let him lie down for it until it clears as it clears.""**"
"12326","Abū al-Ashʿath al-Ṣanʿānī reports that Yazīd ibn Muʿāwiyah sent him to ʿAbd Allāh ibn Abī Awfā, may Allah be pleased with him, with some Companions of the Messenger of Allah ﷺ, he asked what they commanded people, and Ibn Abī Awfā replied that Abū al-Qāsim ﷺ enjoined him, if he encountered such events, to head to Uḥud, break his sword, and sit in his house, retreating to an inner room if pursued, kneeling and reciting the verse of bearing sin and being among the companions of the Fire as the recompense of wrongdoers, and Ibn Abī Awfā affirmed he had broken his sword and would act accordingly. > **""Sit in your inner room, and if he enters upon you, kneel upon your knees and say: Bear my sin and your sin, so you will be among the companions of the Fire, and that is the recompense of the wrongdoers.""**"
"12327","Muḥammad ibn Maslamah, may Allah be pleased with him, reports that the Messenger of Allah ﷺ commanded, upon seeing people fighting over worldly life, striking the sword against the greatest rock in the lava field until it breaks, then sitting at home until an erring hand or a decreed death arrives, and the narrator affirms he did as commanded. > **""When you see people fighting over worldly life, take your sword to the greatest rock in the lava field and strike it until it breaks, then sit in your house until an erring hand or a decreed death comes to you.""**"
"12328","Saʿīd ibn Zayd al-Ashhalī, may Allah be pleased with him, reports that he gifted a sword from Najrān to the Prophet ﷺ, or a sword from Najrān was gifted to the Prophet ﷺ, and when Muḥammad ibn Maslamah came to him, the Prophet ﷺ gave it to him with instruction to strive therewith in the path of Allah, then to strike it upon stone and remain home like a discarded mat when people differ violently, until an erring hand kills him or a decreed death comes. > **""Strive with this in the path of Allah, then when the necks of people differ, strike it upon stone, enter your house, and remain like a discarded mat until an erring hand kills you or a decreed death comes to you.""**"
"12329","Ibn ʿAbbās, may Allah be pleased with him, reports that the Prophet ﷺ gave Muḥammad ibn Maslamah, may Allah be pleased with him, a sword with instruction to fight polytheists while they are fought, then to blunt it and sit at home when swords differ among Muslims until a decreed death or an erring hand arrives, and the narrator then came to Ibn ʿUmar who narrated a similar report from the Prophet ﷺ. > **""Fight the polytheists while they are fought, then when you see two swords differing among the Muslims, strike with it until it is blunted, sit in your house until a decreed death or an erring hand comes to you.""**"
"12330","Ibn al-Ḥakam ibn ʿAmr al-Ghifārī reports from his grandfather that he was sitting with al-Ḥakam ibn ʿAmr, may Allah be pleased with him, when a messenger of ʿAlī ibn Abī Ṭālib, may Allah be pleased with him, came saying he was most entitled to aid them in the matter, and al-Ḥakam replied that he heard his close friend, the cousin of the messenger's sender ﷺ, instruct taking a wooden sword in such circumstances, which he had done. > **""When it is like this, or similar to this, take a sword of wood.""**"
"12331","Ḥudhayfah, may Allah be pleased with him, raises the report that the Prophet ﷺ warned of tribulations coming like portions of dark night in which faith shifts between morning and evening and religion is sold for paltry worldly goods, and when asked what to do, repeatedly commanded symbolic breaking of limbs until an erring hand or decreed death arrives. > **""Tribulations will come to you like portions of the dark night; a man will be a believer in the morning and a disbeliever in the evening, and a believer in the evening and a disbeliever in the morning; one of you will sell his religion for a paltry portion of worldly life. Break your hand. Break the other. Break your leg. Break the other. Until an erring hand or a decreed death comes to you.""**"
"12332","Ribʿi reported that he heard an unnamed man speaking at the funeral of Hudhayfah, may Allah be pleased with him, who attributed a statement to the occupant of that bier concerning what he had heard from the Messenger of Allah, peace and blessings of Allah be upon him, about withdrawing into one's house at the time of mutual fighting and responding to an intruder with a statement of bearing sin. > **""There is no harm upon me for what I heard from the Messenger of Allah, peace and blessings of Allah be upon him. If you fight one another, I shall surely enter my house, and if one enters upon me, I shall surely say: Ha, bear my sin and your sin.""**"
"12333","Wabisah al-Asadi, may Allah be pleased with him, reported that while he was at home in Kufah at midday he heard a greeting at the door and gave permission to enter, and the visitor proved to be ʿAbdullah ibn Masʿud, may Allah be pleased with him, who said the day had grown long upon him and he recalled one with whom he might converse. They exchanged narrations from the Messenger of Allah, peace and blessings of Allah be upon him, until Ibn Masʿud reported what he had heard about successive degrees of safety during tribulation, when its slain would be in the Fire, identified as the days of Harj when a man would not trust his companion, and gave layered instructions for seclusion ending in the mosque with a gesture and a declaration of lordship until death. > **""There shall be a tribulation in which the sleeper is better than the one reclining, and the one reclining is better than the one sitting, and the one sitting is better than the one standing, and the one standing is better than the one walking, and the one walking is better than the rider, and the rider is better than the one urging his mount. All its slain are in the Fire. That is in the days of Harj, when a man shall not trust his companion. Restrain your hand and your tongue and enter your house, then enter your inner room, then enter your place of prayer, and do thus, holding the wrist with the right hand, and say: My Lord is Allah, until you die upon that.""**"
"12334","Khalid ibn ʿUrfutah, may Allah be pleased with him, reported that the Messenger of Allah, peace and blessings of Allah be upon him, addressed him by name concerning events, tribulations, and disagreement that would occur after him, and commanded him, if able, to take the station of the murdered servant of Allah rather than the murderer. > **""O Khalid, there shall be after me events and tribulations and disagreement, so if you are able to be the slain servant of Allah, not the slayer, then do so.""**"
"12335","A man of ʿAbd al-Qays who had been with the Khawarij then left them reported that they entered a village and ʿAbdullah ibn Khabbab, may Allah be pleased with him, came out alarmed dragging his cloak, and when questioned he said they had frightened him. They asked whether he was the Companion of the Messenger of Allah, peace and blessings of Allah be upon him, and whether he had heard from his father a narration from the Messenger, peace and blessings of Allah be upon him, about tribulation. He affirmed and reported the gradation of the seated over the standing and the walking over the striving, with the command to be the slain servant of Allah and not the slaying servant. When they pressed him whether he had himself heard it from his father from the Messenger, he affirmed. They then brought him to the bank of the river, struck his neck so that his blood flowed as a thong remains undivided and unseparated, and they ripped open his concubine mother of his child. In the fuller account the narrator had accompanied a people dear to him along a riverbank, saw a mosque containing a man who came out startled at the forelocks of horses, their leader asked why he was alarmed, and after the killing the narrator found no people more hateful to him until he found solitude and escaped. > **""There shall be a tribulation in which the seated is better than the standing, and the standing is better than the walking, and the walking is better than the striving. If you reach it, be the slain servant of Allah, and do not be the slaying servant of Allah.""**"
"12336","Jundab ibn Sufyan, may Allah be pleased with him, reported that the Messenger of Allah, peace and blessings of Allah be upon him, foretold tribulations after him like portions of the dark night in which a man would be a believer in the morning and a disbeliever in the evening. When a Muslim asked what to do then, he commanded entering houses and obscuring mention, and when asked about one whose house was entered upon, he commanded restraint of the hand and the station of the slain servant, explaining that a man within the stronghold of Islam might consume his brother's wealth, shed his blood, disobey his Lord, disbelieve in his Creator, and thereby earn the Fire. > **""There shall be after me tribulations like portions of the dark night; a man shall be a believer in the morning and a disbeliever in the evening. Enter your houses and obscure your mention. Withhold your hand, and be the slain servant of Allah and do not be the slaying servant of Allah. For a man shall be within the stronghold of Islam, then he consumes the wealth of his brother, sheds his blood, disobeys his Lord, disbelieves in his Creator, and the Fire becomes due for him.""**"
"12337","Abu Waqid al-Laythi, may Allah be pleased with him, reported that while they sat upon a mat the Messenger of Allah, peace and blessings of Allah be upon him, announced coming tribulation, and when asked what to do he returned his hand to the mat, grasped it, and indicated imitation of that clinging. On another day when many did not hear the announcement, Muʿadh ibn Jabal, may Allah be pleased with him, called attention to it, and when asked what to do the Prophet commanded return to the first state of affairs. > **""There shall be tribulation. Do thus, clinging as this. Return to your first affair.""**"
"12338","Mikhwal al-Bahzi, may Allah be pleased with him, reported that the Messenger of Allah, peace and blessings of Allah be upon him, conversed with them in the evening and foretold a time when the best wealth would be goats pasturing among trees, eating foliage and coming to water, whose owners would eat of their meat, drink of their milk, and wear of their hair or wool, while tribulations would churn among the dwelling-places of the Arabs. He thrice swore by Allah that people would be put to trial. > **""There shall come upon people a time when the best wealth of people shall be goats among trees, eating the trees and coming to water; their owners shall eat of their meat and drink of their milk and wear of their hair. And tribulations shall churn among the dwelling-places of the Arabs. By Allah they shall be tried, by Allah they shall be tried, by Allah they shall be tried.""**"
"12339","Saʿd ibn Abi Waqqas, may Allah be pleased with him, reported that he heard the Messenger of Allah, peace and blessings of Allah be upon him, foretell tribulations after him in which a man would be a believer in the morning and a disbeliever in the evening and conversely. When Saʿd asked, with ransom of father and mother, which man would be most rightly guided, he described one upon a hilltop between the two Sanctuaries who maintains prayer at its times, performs Hajj and ʿUmrah, and remains so until an erring hand or a decreed death reaches him. > **""There shall be after me tribulations; a man shall be a believer in the morning and a disbeliever in the evening, and a believer in the evening and a disbeliever in the morning. A man upon a hilltop between these two Sanctuaries, maintaining prayer at its times, performing Hajj and ʿUmrah, and remaining so until an erring hand or a decreed death comes to him.""**"
"12340","Abu al-Ghadiyah al-Muzani, may Allah be pleased with him, reported that the Messenger of Allah, peace and blessings of Allah be upon him, foretold thick and severe tribulations and identified the best people therein as the Muslims of the deserts who do not stain themselves with the blood or wealth of people. > **""There shall be thick and severe tribulations; the best people therein shall be the Muslims of the deserts, who do not soil themselves with anything of the blood of people nor their wealth.""**"
"12341","ʿAmr ibn al-Hamiq, may Allah be pleased with him, reported that the Messenger of Allah, peace and blessings of Allah be upon him, foretold a tribulation in which the safest or best people would be the western detachment. Ibn al-Hamiq added that it was for this reason he had come to them in Egypt. > **""There shall be a tribulation in which the safest of people therein, or the best of people therein, shall be the western detachment.""**"
"12342","Mikhwal al-Bahzi then al-Sulami, may Allah be pleased with him, reported that traps set for him at Abwa caught a gazelle which escaped, and he went in pursuit and found another man had taken it, so they disputed and proceeded together to the Messenger of Allah, peace and blessings of Allah be upon him, whom they found halted at Abwa beneath a tree shading himself with a leather mat. He judged the gazelle to be divided between them in halves. Mikhwal then asked about finding milk-bearing camels whose udders were tied while in need, and was told to call their owner thrice, and otherwise to untie, drink, then retie while leaving the means of milk for its owner. Asked whether watering stray animals brought reward, he affirmed reward in every living thirsty creature. Then the Prophet began to speak of a time when the best wealth would be goats between the two mosques, eating trees and coming to water, with their owner eating, drinking milk, and wearing wool or hair, while tribulations churned among the Arabs and people would not care, thrice repeated. When asked for counsel, he enjoined prayer, zakat, fasting Ramadan, Hajj of the House and ʿUmrah, kindness to parents, joining kin, honoring the guest, enjoining right, forbidding wrong, and remaining with the truth wherever it turns. > **""Call the owner of the camels thrice; if he comes, well, otherwise untie its udder, then drink, then tie, and leave for the milk its means. Yes, in every moist liver is reward. There shall come upon people a time when the best wealth is goats between the two mosques, eating trees and coming to water; its owner eats of its meat, drinks of its milk, and wears of its wool. Tribulations shall churn among the dwelling-places of the Arabs. By Allah you shall not care. Establish prayer, give zakat, fast Ramadan, perform Hajj of the House and ʿUmrah, be dutiful to your parents, join your kin, honor the guest, enjoin the right, forbid the wrong, and remain with the truth wherever it turns.""**"
"12343","Abu Malik al-Ashʿari, may Allah be pleased with him, reported that the Messenger of Allah, peace and blessings of Allah be upon him, said tribulation would be sent and with it desire and patience, with dark and white outcomes for killing according to which path was followed. > **""Tribulation shall be sent, and with it desire and patience shall be sent. Whoever follows desire, his killing shall be black, and whoever follows patience, his killing shall be white.""**"
"12344","Abu al-Dardaʾ, may Allah be pleased with him, reported from the Prophet, peace and blessings of Allah be upon him, a prohibition of approaching tribulation when it blazes and exposing oneself when it appears, with a command whose wording the compiler suggests may intend patience when it advances rather than striking. > **""Do not approach tribulation when it blazes, and do not expose yourselves to it when it appears, and be patient toward it when it advances.""**"
"12345","Kurz ibn ʿAlqamah al-Khuzaʿi, may Allah be pleased with him, reported that a man asked whether Islam had a limit, and was told that whenever Allah intends good for an Arab or non-Arab household He brings Islam into it, but tribulations would then fall like canopies. When the questioner denied it by Allah, the Prophet swore by Him in whose Hand is his soul that people would return therein as black serpents rising, striking one another's necks. Sufyan explained the black serpent's erect posture as its rising. > **""Yes, whatever Arab or non-Arab household Allah intends good for, He brings Islam into it. Then tribulations fall like canopies. Yes, by Him in whose Hand is my soul, then you shall return therein as black serpents rearing, striking the necks of one another.""**"
"12346","In a variant narration completing the preceding account of tribulations, the most excellent person was described as a believer withdrawn in a mountain ravine, fearing his Lord, blessed and exalted, and sparing people his evil. > **""The most excellent of people is a believer secluded in a ravine among the ravines, fearing his Lord and sparing people his evil.""**"
"12347","Abu Barzah al-Aslami, may Allah be pleased with him, reported, as far as he knew from the Prophet, peace and blessings of Allah be upon him, the Prophet's fear for his community concerning the lusts of deviation in bellies and private parts and misleading tribulations. > **""I fear for you only the lusts of deviation in your bellies and your private parts, and the misleading tribulations.""**"
"12348","In another wording of the preceding narration the phrase concerning misleading desires was reported as misleading caprices in place of misleading tribulations. > **""And the misleading desires.""**"
"12349","Wathilah ibn al-Asqaʿ, may Allah be pleased with him, reported that the Messenger of Allah, peace and blessings of Allah be upon him, came out to them and corrected their assumption that he would be the last of them to die, stating he would be the first to die and they would follow him as separate bands destroying one another. > **""You claim that I shall be the last of you to die, yet I shall be the first of you to die, and you shall follow me as separate bands, destroying one another.""**"
"12350","Salamah ibn Nufayl al-Sakuni, may Allah be pleased with him, reported that while they sat with the Messenger of Allah, peace and blessings of Allah be upon him, a speaker asked whether food had come from heaven. He affirmed it came in a heated vessel, affirmed that something remained after him, and said it was raised while it was revealed to him that his stay among them was withheld and brief, and theirs after him brief, until they would ask how long, and they would come to him as bands annihilating one another, with two severe deaths before the Hour followed by years of earthquakes. > **""Yes, in a heated vessel. Yes, it was raised, and it is revealed to me that I am withheld, not remaining among you, and you shall not remain after me except a little, until you say: How long? And you shall come to me as bands, annihilating one another. Before the Hour are two severe deaths, and after them years of earthquakes.""**"
"12351","Muʿawiyah, may Allah be pleased with him, reported that the Messenger of Allah, peace and blessings of Allah be upon him, corrected the claim that he would be the last to die and foretold division into bands striking one another's necks. In the fuller wording from Muʿawiyah ibn Abi Sufyan, they sat in the mosque when the Prophet came out, repeated the correction and prediction, then quoted the verses on punishment from above and beneath and that every tidings has its appointed place, then gave glad tidings that a party of his community would continue fighting for truth, manifest, unharmed by abandonment or opposition until Allah's command comes, then quoted the verse on ʿIsa being taken up, purified, and his followers set above the disbelievers until Resurrection. > **""You claim that I shall be the last of you to die, yet I shall be the first of you to die, and you shall follow me as bands, striking the necks of one another. There shall not cease to be a party of my community fighting for the truth, manifest, not caring for the abandonment of whoever abandons them nor whoever opposes them, until the command of Allah comes upon that.""**"
"12352","Wathilah ibn al-Asqaʿ, may Allah be pleased with him, reported that he heard the Messenger of Allah, peace and blessings of Allah be upon him, correct their claim that he would be the last of them in death and foretell that they would come after him as bands killing one another. > **""You claim that I am the last of you in death, yet I am the first of you in departure. Then you shall come after me as bands, killing one another.""**"
"12353","Talhah ibn ʿUbaydillah, may Allah be pleased with him, reported that he heard the Messenger of Allah, peace and blessings of Allah be upon him, state that no prophethood ever existed except that killing and crucifixion followed it. > **""There was never any prophethood except that after it came killing and crucifixion.""**"
"12354","Al-Mustawrid ibn Shaddad, may Allah be pleased with him, reported that he heard the Messenger of Allah, peace and blessings of Allah be upon him, assign to every community a term and to his community a hundred years, after which would come what Allah, mighty and exalted, had promised them. Ibn Lahiʿah explained the intent as abundance of tribulations. > **""Every community has a term, and my community has a hundred years. When a hundred years pass over my community, there shall come to it what Allah, mighty and exalted, promised it.""**"
"12355","Bilal, may Allah be pleased with him, raised to the Messenger of Allah, peace and blessings of Allah be upon him, that he lifted his gaze to the sky and glorified the One who sends tribulations upon people as rain is sent. > **""Glory be to the One who sends tribulations upon them as rain is sent.""**"
"12356","Jarir, may Allah be pleased with him, reported from the Prophet, peace and blessings of Allah be upon him, that he raised his gaze to the sky and glorified the One who sends tribulations upon them as rain is sent. > **""Glory be to the One who sends upon them of tribulations as rain is sent.""**"
"12357","ʿAbdullah ibn ʿAmr, may Allah be pleased with them both, reported that the Messenger of Allah, peace and blessings of Allah be upon him, foretold a tribulation in which a man would part from his brother and father, with tribulation flying into the hearts of men among them until the Day of Resurrection, until a man would be taunted for it as an adulteress is taunted for her adultery. > **""There shall be a tribulation in which a man parts from his brother and his father. Tribulation shall fly into the hearts of men among them until the Day of Resurrection, until a man is taunted for it as the adulteress is taunted for her adultery.""**"
"12358","By the same chain it was reported that the Messenger of Allah, peace and blessings of Allah be upon him, announced the coming of the Quayʿa, and when asked what it was explained it as a tribulation therein like an egg in intensity and encompassing. > **""The Quayʿa has come to you. It is a tribulation therein like an egg.""**"
"12359","Khalid ibn al-Walid, may Allah be pleased with him, reported that the Commander of the Faithful, meaning ʿUmar ibn al-Khattab, may Allah be pleased with him, wrote to him when Syria had yielded its stores of fine wheat and honey, ordering him to march toward Hind, by which they then meant Basrah, a command Khalid disliked. A man stood and urged him to fear Allah, for tribulations had appeared. Khalid asked whether the son of al-Khattab was alive, for tribulations would only be after him, when people would be at Dhu Billiyan, a named place, where a man would look and consider whether he could find any spot untouched by the like of the tribulation and evil that had descended upon his own place, and would find none. Those, Khalid said, would be the days the Messenger of Allah, peace and blessings of Allah be upon him, mentioned before the Hour, the days of Harj, from which he sought refuge in Allah for himself and his hearers."
"12360","ʿImran ibn Husayn, may Allah be pleased with him, reported that the Messenger of Allah, peace and blessings of Allah be upon him, foretold four tribulations after him, though only three were enumerated in the wording, in which blood, then blood and wealth, then blood and wealth and private parts would be deemed lawful. > **""There shall be after me four tribulations: a tribulation in which blood is deemed lawful, and the second in which blood and wealth are deemed lawful, and the third in which blood and wealth and private parts are deemed lawful.""**"
"12361","ʿAli ibn Abi Talib, may Allah be pleased with him, reported that the Messenger of Allah, peace and blessings of Allah be upon him, foretold a tribulation at the end of time in which people would be sifted as gold and silver are sifted from ore. > **""There shall be in the end of time a tribulation in which people are sifted as gold and silver are sifted from ore.""**"
"12362","Abu Hurayrah, may Allah be pleased with him, reported that he heard the Messenger of Allah, peace and blessings of Allah be upon him, warn that his community would be struck by the disease of nations. When asked what it was, he listed arrogance, insolence, mutual turning away, rivalry, hatred, and miserliness, culminating in transgression then Harj. > **""My community shall be struck by the disease of nations: arrogance, insolence, mutual estrangement, rivalry, hatred, and miserliness, until there is transgression, then Harj.""**"
"12363","ʿAmmar ibn Yasir, may Allah be pleased with him, reported that they sat with the Prophet, peace and blessings of Allah be upon him, eight years after the Hijrah in the ninth year, in a company including Abu Bakr, ʿUmar, ʿUthman, ʿAli, Talhah, al-Zubayr, ʿAbd al-Rahman, Muʿadh, Hudhayfah, and Saʿd, may Allah be pleased with them. Hudhayfah asked, with ransom of father and mother, for a narration on tribulations, and was told of a time when the standing would be better than the walking and the seated better than the standing, with killer and killed in the Fire. > **""O Hudhayfah, there shall come upon people a time in which the standing is better than the walking, and the seated is better than the standing. The killer and the killed shall be in the Fire.""**"
"12364","Al-Hasan reported that al-Dahhak ibn Qays, may Allah be pleased with him, wrote to Qays ibn al-Haytham when Yazid ibn Muʿawiyah died with greetings, reporting what he had heard from the Messenger of Allah, peace and blessings of Allah be upon him, about tribulations before the Hour like portions of the dark night and like portions of smoke, in which a man's heart would die as his body dies, a man a believer in the morning and a disbeliever in the evening and conversely, with peoples selling their share and religion for worldly goods. He added that Yazid had died and that they were brothers and counterparts, urging them not to precede his side until they chose for themselves. > **""Before the Hour are tribulations like portions of the dark night, tribulations like portions of smoke. The heart of a man dies therein as his body dies. A man is a believer in the morning and a disbeliever in the evening, and a believer in the evening and a disbeliever in the morning. Peoples sell their share and their religion for goods of this world.""**"
"12365","Al-Nuʿman ibn Bashir, may Allah be pleased with him, reported that they accompanied the Messenger of Allah, peace and blessings of Allah be upon him, and heard him foretell tribulations before the Hour like portions of the dark night, with rapid inversion of faith and peoples selling their share for a small worldly price or worldly goods. Al-Hasan added, with an oath, that he had seen such people as mere forms without minds and bodies without discernment, moths of fire and wolves of greed, going out for two dirhams and returning for two dirhams, one selling his religion for the price of a goat. > **""Before the Hour are tribulations like portions of the dark night. A man is a believer in the morning and a disbeliever in the evening, and a believer in the evening, then a disbeliever in the morning. Peoples sell their share for goods of this world, a small price.""**"
"12366","A man of Syria called ʿAmmar reported that after campaigning for a year they returned with an aged man of Khathʿam, and when al-Hajjaj was mentioned the elder reviled him. When ʿAmmar asked why he reviled one fighting the Iraqis in obedience to the Commander of the Faithful, the elder replied that it was he who had rendered them disbelievers, then reported hearing the Messenger of Allah, peace and blessings of Allah be upon him, speak of five tribulations of this community, four already past and one remaining, the Saylam, which would be among the Syrians. If reached, one able to be as a stone should be so, should join neither of the two parties, and should take provision from the earth. > **""There shall be in this community five tribulations; four have passed and one remains, and it is the Saylam. It shall be among you, O people of Syria. If you reach it, then if you are able to be a stone, be so, and do not be with either of the two parties. Take a tunnel in the earth.""**"
"12367","In a variant of the preceding account of the five tribulations, those present asked the aged narrator whether he had himself heard it from the Prophet, peace and blessings of Allah be upon him, and he affirmed it."
"12368","Hudhayfah, may Allah be pleased with him, reported that the Messenger of Allah, peace and blessings of Allah be upon him, was asked about the Hour and replied that its knowledge is with his Lord and none manifests it in its time but He, but he would inform of its preconditions and what precedes it, namely tribulation and Harj. When told tribulation was known but Harj unknown, he explained in the tongue of Abyssinia it means killing, and added that mutual estrangement would be cast among people until hardly anyone knew another. > **""Its knowledge is with my Lord; none manifests it in its time but He. But I inform you of its preconditions and what precedes it. Before it are tribulation and Harj, in the tongue of Abyssinia, killing. And mutual estrangement shall be cast among people, so hardly anyone shall know anyone.""**"
"12369","Ibn ʿAbbas, may Allah be pleased with them both, reported that the Messenger of Allah, peace and blessings of Allah be upon him, said Islam began strange and shall return strange as it began, with blessedness for the strangers, and that before the Hour would be tribulations like portions of the dark night with rapid inversion of faith and peoples selling religion for worldly goods. > **""Islam began strange, and it shall return strange as it began, so blessedness is for the strangers. Before the Hour are tribulations like portions of the dark night. A man is a believer in the evening and a disbeliever in the morning, and a believer in the morning and a disbeliever in the evening. Peoples sell their religion for goods of this world.""**"
"12370","ʿAbdullah ibn ʿUmar, may Allah be pleased with them both, reported that the Messenger of Allah, peace and blessings of Allah be upon him, swore that his community after him would surely be tried with tribulations like portions of the dark night, with rapid inversion of faith and peoples selling religion for a small worldly price. > **""My community after me shall surely be tried with tribulations like portions of the dark night. A man is a believer in the morning and a disbeliever in the evening, and a believer in the evening and a disbeliever in the morning. Peoples sell their religion for a small price of this world.""**"
"12371","Maymunah, may Allah be pleased with her, reported that the Prophet of Allah, peace and blessings of Allah be upon him, said to them one day, asking what they would be when religion became confused, blood was shed, adornment appeared, buildings were raised high, brothers or, in a variant, scholars differed, and the Ancient House was burned. > **""What shall you be when religion is confused, blood is shed, adornment appears, buildings are raised high, brothers differ, and the Ancient House is burned.""**"
"12372","Abu Saʿid reported, without elevation to the Prophet in this chain, that the Hour shall not come until Harj increases among them, thrice repeated, and when asked what Harj was he said killing."
"12373","Fayruz al-Daylami, may Allah be pleased with him, reported that the Messenger of Allah, peace and blessings of Allah be upon him, foretold a cry in Ramadan. When asked in which part, he specified the middle, on a Friday night at mid-Ramadan, a cry from heaven stunning seventy thousand, muting seventy thousand, blinding seventy thousand, and deafening seventy thousand. When asked who among his community would be safe, he named one who stays in his house, seeks refuge in prostration, and proclaims Allah's greatness aloud. Then another cry would follow, the first the voice of Jibril and the second the voice of Satan, with the cry in Ramadan, turmoil in Shawwal, distinction of tribes in Dhu al-Qaʿdah, raiding of pilgrims in Dhu al-Hijjah, and Muharram whose beginning is trial for his community and whose end is relief, when a mounted camel with its saddle by which a believer escapes is better than a estate yielding a hundred thousand. > **""There shall be in Ramadan a cry, in the middle of Ramadan, on a Friday night at mid-month, a cry from heaven stunning seventy thousand, muting seventy thousand, blinding seventy thousand, and deafening seventy thousand. Whoever stays in his house, seeks refuge in prostration, and proclaims Allah's greatness aloud is safe. Then another cry follows; the first cry is the voice of Jibril and the second the voice of Satan. The cry is in Ramadan, turmoil in Shawwal, distinction of tribes in Dhu al-Qaʿdah, raiding of pilgrims in Dhu al-Hijjah, and Muharram, its beginning trial for my community and its end relief. A mount with its saddle upon which the believer escapes is better for him than an estate yielding a hundred thousand.""**"
"12374","Abu Hurayrah, may Allah be pleased with him, reported that the Messenger of Allah, peace and blessings of Allah be upon him, foretold a cry in the month of Ramadan, distinction of tribes in Dhu al-Qaʿdah, and despoiling of the pilgrim in Dhu al-Hijjah. > **""In the month of Ramadan is the cry, in Dhu al-Qaʿdah is distinction of tribes, and in Dhu al-Hijjah the pilgrim is despoiled.""**"
"12375","Samurah, may Allah be pleased with him, reported that the Messenger of Allah, peace and blessings of Allah be upon him, said Allah, mighty and exalted, would soon fill their hands with non-Arabs who would become lions that do not flee, killing their fighters and consuming their war gains. > **""Allah, mighty and exalted, shall soon fill your hands with non-Arabs, then they shall be lions that do not flee. They shall kill your fighters and consume your war gains.""**"
"12376","Anas, may Allah be pleased with him, reported that the Prophet, peace and blessings of Allah be upon him, said Allah would soon fill their hands with non-Arabs, then make them lions that do not flee, fighting their fighters and consuming their war gains. > **""Allah shall soon fill your hands with non-Arabs, then He shall make them lions that do not flee. They shall fight your fighters and consume your war gains.""**"
"12377","ʿAbdullah ibn ʿAmr, may Allah be pleased with them both, reported from the Prophet, peace and blessings of Allah be upon him, that Allah would soon fill their hands with non-Arabs, then make them lions that do not flee, killing their fighters and consuming their war gains. > **""Allah shall soon fill your hands with non-Arabs, then He shall make them lions that do not flee. They shall kill your fighters and consume your war gains.""**"
"12378","Hudhayfah, may Allah be pleased with him, reported from the Prophet, peace and blessings of Allah be upon him, that Allah would soon fill their hands with non-Arabs and make them lions that do not flee, striking their necks and consuming their war gains. > **""Allah shall soon fill your hands with non-Arabs, and make them lions that do not flee. They shall strike your necks and consume your war gains.""**"
"12379","Abu Hurayrah, may Allah be pleased with him, reported that the Messenger of Allah, peace and blessings of Allah be upon him, said lions from among the non-Arabs that do not flee would soon multiply among them, killing their fighters and consuming their war gains. > **""There shall soon multiply among you lions from among the non-Arabs that do not flee. They shall kill your fighters and consume your war gains.""**"
"12380","Al-Hasan reported as a mursal narration that he had received that the Messenger of Allah, peace and blessings of Allah be upon him, said the Hour would not come until they fought a people wearing hair footwear and until they fought a people broad of face, flat of nose, small of eye, as though their faces were hammered shields. > **""The Hour shall not come until you fight a people wearing hair footwear, and until you fight a people broad of face, flat of nose, small of eye, as though their faces are hammered shields.""**"
"12381","Buraydah, may Allah be pleased with him, reported that while seated with the Prophet, peace and blessings of Allah be upon him, he heard him say his community would be driven by a broad-faced, small-eyed people whose faces were as shields, thrice repeated, until they would be pushed back to the Arabian Peninsula. The first wave would spare whoever fled, the second would spare some and destroy some, and the third would wholly uproot those remaining. When asked who they were, he named the Turks and swore they would tie their horses to the pillars of Muslim mosques. Buraydah thereafter never parted from two or three camels with travel gear and waterskins prepared for flight because of what he had heard of trial from Turkish rulers. > **""My community shall be driven by a broad-faced, small-eyed people, as though their faces are shields, three times, until you attach them to the Arabian Peninsula. The first wave: whoever flees among them is saved. The second: some are saved and some perish. The third: all who remain among them are uprooted. They are the Turks. By Him in whose Hand is my soul, they shall surely tie their horses to the pillars of the mosques of the Muslims.""**"
"12382","Muʿawiyah ibn Hudayj reported that he was with Muʿawiyah ibn Abi Sufyan, may Allah be pleased with him, when a letter came from his governor reporting an attack upon the Turks, their defeat, many slain, and much booty. Muʿawiyah grew angry and ordered a reply acknowledging the report but forbidding any repetition or further fighting without his command. When asked why, he reported hearing the Messenger of Allah, peace and blessings of Allah be upon him, foretell Turkish ascendancy over the Arabs until they would push them back to the growths of wormwood and southernwood, and said he therefore disliked fighting them. > **""The Turks shall surely prevail over the Arabs until they attach them to the growths of wormwood and southernwood.""**"
"12383","ʿAbdullah ibn Masʿud, may Allah be pleased with him, reported that the Messenger of Allah, peace and blessings of Allah be upon him, commanded leaving the Turks while they leave them, for the first to strip his community of its kingdom and what Allah entrusted to them would be the sons of Qantura. > **""Leave the Turks while they leave you. The first to strip my community of its kingdom and what Allah entrusted to them are the sons of Qantura.""**"
"12384","ʿAbdullah ibn al-Saʾib, may Allah be pleased with him, reported that the Messenger of Allah, peace and blessings of Allah be upon him, said the Arabs would reach the birthplace of their forefathers at the growths of wormwood and southernwood. > **""The Arabs shall reach the birthplace of their forefathers at the growths of wormwood and southernwood.""**"
"12385","Abu Hurayrah, may Allah be pleased with him, reported from the Prophet, peace and blessings of Allah be upon him, a coming fight with a broad-faced, small-eyed people whose faces were as hammered shields and whose eyes were as locusts' eyes, wearing hair, taking leather shields, and tying their horses to date-palms. > **""You shall fight a broad-faced, small-eyed people, as though their faces are hammered shields, and as though their eyes are the eyes of locusts. They wear hair, take leather shields, and tie their horses to date-palms.""**"
"12386","Ibn Sirin reported that Ibn Masʿud, may Allah be pleased with him, used to say it was as though he saw the Turks coming to them upon mules with cropped ears until they would tie them at the bank of the Euphrates."
"12387","Yazid ibn Muʿawiyah al-ʿAmiri reported that he heard ʿAbdullah ibn Masʿud, may Allah be pleased with him, ask how they would be when they saw, or when there came to them, a flat-faced people."
"12388","Abu al-Aswad al-Dayli reported that he and Zurʿah ibn Damrah accepted Islam with al-Ashʿari and met ʿAbdullah ibn ʿAmr, may Allah be pleased with them both, sitting to his right and left respectively. ʿAbdullah ibn ʿAmr said soon no non-Arab would remain in Arab land except slain or captive whose blood awaited judgment. When Zurʿah asked whether polytheists would prevail over Muslims and stated his lineage from Banu ʿAmir ibn Saʿsaʿah associated with Dhu al-Khalasah in pagan times, the narrator mentioned the statement to ʿUmar ibn al-Khattab, may Allah be pleased with him, who thrice affirmed ʿAbdullah ibn ʿAmr knew best what he said, then preached on Friday quoting the Prophet, peace and blessings of Allah be upon him, on the enduring victorious party upon truth until Allah's command. When this was mentioned to ʿAbdullah ibn ʿUmar, he confirmed the Prophet had spoken truth and that his own prediction would follow that stage. > **""There shall not cease to be a party of my community upon the truth, victorious, until the command of Allah comes.""**"
"12389","Hudhayfah, may Allah be pleased with him, reported that he heard the Messenger of Allah, peace and blessings of Allah be upon him, say this clan of Mudar would not leave any righteous servant of Allah without tempting and destroying him, until Allah reached them with soldiers from Himself and abased them until they could not defend even the tail of a mountain stream. > **""This clan of Mudar shall not leave any righteous servant of Allah without tempting him and destroying him, until Allah reaches them with soldiers from Himself and abases them until they cannot defend the tail of a mountain stream.""**"
"12390","In another wording, Mudar would not leave any believing servant of Allah without tempting or killing him. Hudhayfah addressed the assembly of Mudar to draw near, swearing they would persist in tempting and killing every believer until Allah and His angels and the believers struck them so they could not defend a valley's belly. When asked why he had brought them forward despite this, he replied that among them were the chief of Adam's descendants, peace and blessings of Allah be upon him, and forerunners like the forerunners of horses. > **""Mudar shall not leave any believing servant of Allah without tempting him or killing him.""**"
"12391","Abu Saʿid al-Khudri, may Allah be pleased with him, reported that the Messenger of Allah, peace and blessings of Allah be upon him, said Mudar would surely strike the servants of Allah until no name of Allah was worshipped, or until the believers struck them so they could not defend the tail of a mountain stream. > **""Mudar shall surely strike the servants of Allah until no name of Allah is worshipped, or the believers shall surely strike them until they cannot defend the tail of a mountain stream.""**"
"12392","ʿUmar ibn al-Khattab, may Allah be pleased with him, reported that a boy was born to the brother of Umm Salamah, wife of the Prophet, peace and blessings of Allah be upon him, and they named him al-Walid. The Prophet, peace and blessings of Allah be upon him, said they had named him after their Pharaohs, and that there would be in this community a man called al-Walid more evil to it than Pharaoh was to his people. > **""You have named him after your Pharaohs. There shall be in this community a man called al-Walid, more evil to this community than Pharaoh was to his people.""**"
"12393","Abu Saʿid al-Khudri, may Allah be pleased with him, reported that the Messenger of Allah, peace and blessings of Allah be upon him, gave glad tidings of the Mahdi, sent amid division and earthquakes, who would fill the earth with equity and justice as it had been filled with tyranny and wrong. Dwellers of heaven and earth would be pleased with him, he would distribute wealth soundly, meaning equally among people, Allah would fill the hearts of Muhammad's community with richness, and his justice would encompass them. A crier would call for any in need of wealth, only one man would rise claiming need, he would be sent to the treasurer with the Mahdi's order to give, the treasurer would tell him to scoop, he would fill his lap and garment, then regret his greed above all the community or his inability to accept what sufficed them, seek to return it, be refused on the ground that gifts given are not taken back, and this order would last seven or eight or nine years, after which there would be no good in life. > **""I give you glad tidings of the Mahdi. He shall be sent amid division of people and earthquakes. He shall fill the earth with equity and justice as it was filled with tyranny and wrong. The dweller of heaven and the dweller of earth shall be pleased with him. He shall distribute wealth soundly, equally among people. Allah shall fill the hearts of the community of Muhammad, peace and blessings of Allah be upon him, with richness, and his justice shall encompass them, until he commands a crier to call: Who is in need of wealth? None shall rise except one man. He shall say: Go to the treasurer and say: The Mahdi commands you to give me wealth. He shall say: Scoop. When he has placed it in his lap and wrapped it, he shall regret and say: I was the greediest of the community of Muhammad, or what sufficed them failed me. He shall return it and it shall not be accepted, and it shall be said: We do not take back anything we gave. It shall be so seven years or eight years or nine years. Then there is no good in life after him.""**"
"12394","From Abu Saʿid, may Allah be pleased with him, it was reported that the Messenger of Allah, peace and blessings of Allah be upon him, said that at a break in time and emergence of tribulations a man called al-Saffah would appear whose giving of wealth would be by scoops. > **""There shall appear at a break in time and emergence of tribulations a man called al-Saffah. His giving of wealth shall be by scoops.""**"
"12395","From Abu Saʿid, may Allah be pleased with him, from the Messenger of Allah, peace and blessings of Allah be upon him, that there would surely rise over his community from his Household one aquiline of nose and bright of forehead, filling the earth with justice as it had been filled with wrong and tyranny, ruling seven years. > **""There shall surely rise over my community from my Household one aquiline and bright. He shall fill the earth with justice as it was filled with wrong and tyranny. He shall rule seven years.""**"
"12396","Qurrah ibn Iyas, may Allah be pleased with him, reported that the Messenger of Allah, peace and blessings of Allah be upon him, said the earth would surely be filled with wrong and tyranny, and when so filled Allah would send a man from him whose name matched his name and whose father's name matched his father's name, filling it with justice and equity as it had been filled, with heaven withholding nothing of rain nor earth anything of growth, remaining among them seven or eight or nine years. > **""The earth shall surely be filled with wrong and tyranny. When it is filled with tyranny and wrong, Allah shall send a man from me, his name my name and his father's name my father's name. He shall fill it with justice and equity as it was filled with tyranny and wrong. Heaven shall withhold nothing of its rain, nor earth anything of its growth. He shall remain among you seven or eight or nine, meaning years.""**"
"12397","Umm Salamah, may Allah be pleased with her, reported that the Messenger of Allah, peace and blessings of Allah be upon him, said pledge would be given to a man between the Corner and the Station by a number like the people of Badr, the bands of Iraq and the substitutes of Syria would come to him, an army from Syria would raid them and be swallowed at Bayda, then a Qurashi man whose maternal uncles were of Kalb would raid, they would meet, Allah would defeat them, and the loser would be whoever lost the spoils of Kalb. > **""Pledge shall be given to a man between the Corner and the Station, a number like the people of Badr. The bands of the people of Iraq and the substitutes of the people of Syria shall come to him. An army from the people of Syria shall raid them, until at Bayda they are swallowed. Then a man of Quraysh whose maternal uncles are of Kalb shall raid them. They shall meet and Allah shall defeat them. The loser is whoever loses the spoils of Kalb.""**"
"12398","From Umm Salamah, may Allah be pleased with her, it was reported that the Messenger of Allah, peace and blessings of Allah be upon him, said the king of the east would march to the king of the west and kill him, send an army to Madinah which would be swallowed, send another and forget some Medinans, a refuge-seeker would take refuge at the Sanctuary, people would gather to him like scattered arriving birds until three hundred and fourteen men including women gathered, he would prevail over every tyrant and son of tyrant, manifest such justice that the living would wish their dead present, live seven years, after which what is beneath the earth would be better than what is above it. > **""The king of the east shall march to the king of the west and kill him. He shall send an army to Madinah and it is swallowed. Then he sends an army and some Medinans are overlooked. A refuge-seeker takes refuge at the Sanctuary, and people gather to him like scattered arriving birds, until three hundred and fourteen men, including women, gather to him. He prevails over every tyrant and son of tyrant, manifests such justice that the living wish their dead alive, lives seven years, then what is beneath the earth is better than what is above it.""**"
"12399","From Umm Salamah, may Allah be pleased with her, it was reported that she heard the Messenger of Allah, peace and blessings of Allah be upon him, say there would be dispute at the death of a caliph, a man of Banu Hashim would emerge to Makkah, people would bring him out from his house between the Corner and the Station, an army from Syria would be dispatched until at Bayda they are swallowed, the bands of Iraq and substitutes of Syria would come to him, a Syrian whose maternal uncles were of Kalb would arise and dispatch an army, Allah would defeat them and the rout would be upon them, that being the Day of Kalb, the loser whoever lost the spoils of Kalb. He would open treasures, divide wealth, settle Islam firmly upon the earth, and they would live so seven years, or he said nine. > **""There shall be dispute at the death of a caliph. A man of Banu Hashim shall emerge and come to Makkah. People shall bring him out from his house between the Corner and the Station. An army from Syria shall be dispatched toward him, until at Bayda they are swallowed. The bands of Iraq and the substitutes of Syria shall come to him. A man in Syria whose maternal uncles are of Kalb shall arise and an army shall be dispatched toward him, and Allah shall defeat them, and the rout shall be upon them. That is the Day of Kalb. The loser is whoever loses the spoils of Kalb. He shall open treasures, divide wealth, settle Islam upon the earth, and they shall live so seven years, or nine.""**"
"12400","Abu Hurayrah, may Allah be pleased with him, reported that he heard the Messenger of Allah, peace and blessings of Allah be upon him, say the deprived is whoever is deprived of the spoils of Kalb. > **""The deprived is whoever is deprived of the spoils of Kalb.""**"
"12401","From Abu Hurayrah, may Allah be pleased with him, it was reported that his intimate friend Abu al-Qasim, peace and blessings of Allah be upon him, told him the Hour would not come until a man of his Household rose against them and struck them until they returned to truth. When asked how long he would rule, he said five and two, and when asked what five and two meant his transmitter said he did not know. > **""The Hour shall not come until a man of my Household rises against them and strikes them until they return to the truth. He shall rule five and two.""**"
"12402","Umm Habibah, may Allah be pleased with her, reported that she heard the Messenger of Allah, peace and blessings of Allah be upon him, say people from the east would come seeking a man at the House, until at a desert plain they would be swallowed, those lagging catching up would share their fate, and when asked about one brought under compulsion he said they would share the fate then Allah would raise each upon his intention. Umm Salamah, may Allah be pleased with her, reported that while the Messenger, peace and blessings of Allah be upon him, reclined in her house he sat up uttering the return to Allah. When asked why, he spoke of an army from his community coming from Syria aiming for the House against a man prevented from them, until at Bayda of Dhu al-Hulayfah they would be swallowed though their starting points differed. When asked how all could be swallowed despite differing starting points, he thrice affirmed some among them were compelled. > **""People from the east shall come seeking a man at the House, until at a desert plain they are swallowed, and those who lag join them and share what befell them. They share what befell people, then Allah raises each upon his intention. An army of my community shall come from Syria aiming for the House against a man prevented from them, until at Bayda of Dhu al-Hulayfah they are swallowed, though their starting points differ. Among them are those compelled, among them are those compelled.""**"
"12403","By the same chain it was similarly reported from ʿA'ishah, may Allah be pleased with her, from the Prophet, peace and blessings of Allah be upon him, the like of the preceding account."
"12404","Anas, may Allah be pleased with him, reported that the Messenger of Allah, peace and blessings of Allah be upon him, slept in the house of Umm Salamah, may Allah be pleased with her, then awoke uttering the return to Allah. When asked why, he spoke of an army from Iraq seeking a man of Madinah whom Allah would prevent from them, until at Bayda of Dhu al-Hulayfah they would be swallowed, upper never reaching lower nor lower upper until Resurrection though their starting points differed. When asked how despite differing starting points, he said among them were the compelled. > **""An army shall come from Iraq seeking a man of Madinah, and Allah shall prevent him from them. When they ascend Bayda of Dhu al-Hulayfah they are swallowed. Their upper shall never reach their lower, nor their lower their upper, until the Day of Resurrection, though their starting points differ. Among them are those compelled.""**"
"12405","ʿAbdullah, meaning Ibn Masʿud, may Allah be pleased with him, reported that the Messenger of Allah, peace and blessings of Allah be upon him, said black banners would come from the east and horses would wade in blood to their breastplates, then mentioned the full narration. > **""Black banners shall come from the east, and horses shall wade in blood to their breastplates.""**"
"12406","Abu Hurayrah, may Allah be pleased with him, reported that the Messenger of Allah, peace and blessings of Allah be upon him, mentioned the Mahdi and said if shortened it would be seven, otherwise eight, otherwise nine, and he would surely fill the earth with justice and equity as it had been filled with tyranny and wrong. > **""If shortened, seven, otherwise eight, otherwise nine. He shall surely fill the earth with justice and equity as it was filled with tyranny and wrong.""**"
"12407","Jābir (radiya Allahu ʿanhu) reported that the Messenger of Allah ﷺ foretold a caliph in his Ummah distinguished by unstinted generosity, then added an oath about a return. > **""There will be in my Ummah a caliph who scoops wealth among the people in handfuls without counting it. By the One in Whose Hand is my soul, it will surely return.""**"
"12408","Ṭalḥah ibn ʿUbaydillāh (radiya Allahu ʿanhu) reported from the Prophet ﷺ a restless tribulation shifting from side to side until a heavenly proclamation appoints a leader. > **""There will be a fitnah, no side of it calms but another side of it surges, until a caller from heaven calls out: Your leader is so-and-so.""**"
"12409","ʿAlī ibn Abī Ṭālib (radiya Allahu ʿanhu) reported that he asked the Messenger of Allah ﷺ whether the Mahdī would be from them or others, received an answer about sealing and unity through his house, then asked about belief and received a stern reply. > **""Rather from us. Through us Allah seals as through us He began, and through us they are rescued from shirk, and through us Allah joins their hearts after open enmity, as through us He joined their hearts after the enmity of shirk. Tested and disbelieving.""**"
"12410","ʿAlī ibn Abī Ṭālib (radiya Allahu ʿanhu) reported that the Messenger of Allah ﷺ described a sifting latter-day fitnah, forbade reviling Shām except its wicked, mentioned the Abdāl and a dispersing flood, then foretold a man of his house with banners and the killing of seven rival claimants. > **""There will be in the latter time a fitnah sifting people as gold is sifted in the mine. Do not revile the people of Shām, but revile their wicked, for among them are the Abdāl. A flood is about to be sent upon the people of Shām dispersing their unity, until if foxes fought them they would defeat them. At that a man from my household emerges with three banners. The exaggerator says fifteen thousand, the minimizer says twelve thousand. Their sign is: Kill, kill. They meet seven banners, under each a man seeking kingship. Allah kills them all and restores to the Muslims their affection and blessing, far and near.""**"
"12411","Abū Hurayrah (radiya Allahu ʿanhu) reported from the Prophet ﷺ the advent of the Mahdī, his possible tenure, unmatched bounty, abundant rain and vegetation, heaped wealth, and openhanded giving. > **""There will be in my Ummah the Mahdī. If shortened, then seven, otherwise eight, otherwise nine. My Ummah enjoys therein a bliss never enjoyed before. Heaven sends down abundant rain upon them, earth withholds nothing of vegetation, wealth lies heaped. A man stands saying: O Mahdī, give me. He says: Take.""**"
"12412","Abū Saʿīd al-Khudrī (radiya Allahu ʿanhu) reported that he heard the Messenger of Allah ﷺ foretell a man of his Ummah ruling by his Sunnah with rain, blessing, equity, and residence at Jerusalem. > **""A man from my Ummah emerges speaking by my Sunnah. Allah sends down rain for him from heaven, and Allah sprouts for him the earth in its blessing. The earth is filled through him with equity and justice as it was filled with tyranny and injustice. He governs this Ummah seven years and settles at Bayt al-Maqdis.""**"
"12413","ʿAbdullāh ibn ʿUmar (radiya Allahu ʿanhuma) reported that the Prophet ﷺ sat among Muhājirūn and Anṣār with ʿAlī on his left and al-ʿAbbās on his right, a quarrel arose with an Anṣārī speaking harshly to al-ʿAbbās, whereupon the Prophet ﷺ took their hands and foretold two youths and enjoined adherence to the Tamīmī vanguard from the east. > **""From the loins of this a youth will emerge filling the earth with tyranny and injustice, and from this a youth will emerge filling the earth with equity and justice. When you see that, keep to the Tamīmī youth, for he advances from the east, and he is the bearer of the Mahdī's banner.""**"
"12414","ʿAbdullāh ibn al-Ḥārith ibn Jazʾ al-Zubaydī (radiya Allahu ʿanhu) reported that the Messenger of Allah ﷺ foretold a people from the east establishing dominion for the Mahdī. > **""A people emerge from the east preparing his dominion for the Mahdī.""**"
"12415","ʿAbdullāh ibn ʿUmar (radiya Allahu ʿanhuma) reported from the Messenger of Allah ﷺ that rule by two freedmen precedes great slaughter. > **""When the two freedmen rule, the freedman of the Arabs and the freedman of the Romans, at their hands will be the great battles.""**"
"12416","Abū Mūsā al-Ashʿarī (radiya Allahu ʿanhu) reported that the Messenger of Allah ﷺ assigned the great battles to a specific descendant of Heraclius. > **""The great battles will be at the hands of the fifth from the household of Heraclius.""**"
"12417","Abū Dharr (radiya Allahu ʿanhu) reported that he heard the Messenger of Allah ﷺ foretell an Umayyad ruler in Egypt, his deposition, flight to Rome, and return with Romans as the first of the battles. > **""There will be a man from Banū Umayyah in Egypt holding power, then he is overpowered in his power, or it is stripped from him, so he flees to Rome and brings the Romans against the people of Islam. That is the first of the great battles.""**"
"12418","ʿAbd al-Raḥmān ibn Sannah (radiya Allahu ʿanhu) reported that he heard the Messenger of Allah ﷺ describe Islam retreating between Makkah and Madīnah, an Arab blaze, and a fateful encounter with Rome. > **""Islam will contract between Makkah and Madīnah as the snake contracts to its hole. While they are thus, the fire of the Arabs blazes with its bedouins. Then emerges like the righteous of those past and the best of those remaining until they meet the Romans and fight one another.""**"
"12419","Abū Umāmah (radiya Allahu ʿanhu) reported that the Messenger of Allah ﷺ foretold four truces with Rome, the fourth through a Heraclian lasting seven years, whereupon al-Mustawrid ibn Khaylān of ʿAbd al-Qays asked about the peoples leader and received a detailed description of a forty-year-old descendant, his reign, treasures, and conquests. > **""There will be between you and Rome four truces, the fourth at the hand of a man from the household of Heraclius lasting seven years. From my progeny, a man of forty years, as if his face is a shining star, on his right cheek a black mole, upon him two white Qaṭwānī cloaks, as if from the men of Banū Isrāʾīl. He rules twenty years, extracts the treasures, and conquers the cities of shirk.""**"
"12420","ʿAbd al-Raḥmān ibn Abī Bakrah reported that he visited ʿAbdullāh ibn ʿAmr at his house amid two rows of people, sat by his feet, a red large-bellied man entered and questioned his lineage through Abū Bakrah who descended from the wall of Ṭāʾif, then began narrating thrice about the son of Ḥamal al-Ḍaʾn, his origin, Roman dominion, vast host by land and sea, encampment at ʿAmīq, burning of ships, severance from Rome and Constantinople, Muslim mutual reinforcement from ʿAdan Abyan, a month of slaughter with blood in hooves, doubled reward except the Companions, divine aid on the final day, conquest of Constantinople, division of gold and silver, and the cry of Dajjāl among dwellings. > **""It is imminent that the son of Ḥamal al-Ḍaʾn emerges. A man one of whose parents is a devil, ruling Rome, coming with a thousand thousands, five hundred thousand by land and five hundred thousand by sea, alighting at land called al-ʿAmīq. He says to his companions: I have a remnant in your ships, then burns them with fire and says: No Rome for you nor Constantinople for you, whoever wishes may flee. The Muslims reinforce one another until the people of ʿAdan Abyan reinforce them. The Muslims say to them: Join them and be one weapon. They fight a month until blood flows in their hooves. For the believer that day is a double share of reward over those before him, except what belonged to the Companions of Muḥammad. When the last day of the month comes Allah says: Today I draw My sword, aid My religion, and avenge My enemy. Allah turns the circle against them, Allah routs them until Constantinople is opened. Their leader says: No misappropriation today. While they divide gold and silver in their shields, it is called among them: Dajjāl has taken your place in your homes.""**"
"12421","Abū Hurayrah (radiya Allahu ʿanhu) reported that Saʿd approached the Prophet ﷺ, whose face showed news, announced the killing of Khosrau, whereupon the Prophet ﷺ cursed him and stated the order of ruin. > **""May Allah curse Khosrau. The first of people to perish are the Arabs, then the people of Persia.""**"
"12422","Maymūnah (radiya Allahu ʿanha) reported that the Messenger of Allah ﷺ asked how they would fare when religion decays, craving and fear appear, and the Ancient House is burned, with an addition about lofty buildings and brothers differing. > **""How will you be when religion is confused, craving and fear appear, the Ancient House is burned, buildings are raised high, and brothers differ.""**"
"12423","Abū Bakr ibn Abī al-Jahm reported that he and Yazīd ibn Ḥasan came with Ibn Rummānah, freedman of ʿAbd al-ʿAzīz ibn Marwān, leaning upon their raised hands into the mosque of the Messenger ﷺ, where Ibn Niyār, a Companion, summoned Abū Bakr, remarked on the youth leaning between them, then quoted the Prophet ﷺ on the worlds transfer to the vilest. > **""The world will not pass until it is with Lukaʿ ibn Lukaʿ.""**"
"12424","The narrator reported a variant wording of the same Prophetic statement that the world will not end until it belongs to the vilest of the vile. > **""The world will not pass until it belongs to Lukaʿ ibn Lukaʿ.""**"
"12425","Some Companions of the Prophet (radiya Allahu ʿanhum) reported the imminent dominance of the vilest and the excellence of a believer born of two noble parents. > **""It is imminent that Lukaʿ ibn Lukaʿ overcomes the world, and the best of people is a believer between two noble parents.""**"
"12426","Al-Mustawrid ibn Shaddād (radiya Allahu ʿanhu) reported that the Messenger of Allah ﷺ likened the successive departure of the righteous to refuse and divine disregard for the remnant. > **""The righteous depart, first then first, and there remains refuse like the refuse of dates. Allah pays them no heed.""**"
"12427","ʿUmar ibn al-Khaṭṭāb (radiya Allahu ʿanhu) reported that the Messenger of Allah ﷺ stated what is first taken and what last remains, with worthless worshippers. > **""The first taken from people is trust, and the last remaining is prayer, and many a worshipper in whom is no good.""**"
"12428","Abū Hurayrah (radiya Allahu ʿanhu) reported that the Messenger of Allah ﷺ stated that modesty and trust are first lifted from this Ummah while prayer last remains, with a doubtful addition about prayer without portion. > **""The first lifted from this Ummah is modesty and trust, and the last remaining is prayer. A people may pray having no share.""**"
"12429","ʿAbdullāh ibn ʿAmr (radiya Allahu ʿanhuma) reported that the Messenger of Allah ﷺ likened the signs to strung beads following one another when the thread breaks. > **""The signs are beads strung on a thread. If the thread is cut, some follow others.""**"
"12430","Abū Hurayrah (radiya Allahu ʿanhu) reported from the Prophet ﷺ that the signs emerge consecutively like beads in a string. > **""The signs emerge, some upon the traces of others, following as beads follow in a string.""**"
"12431","ʿAbdullāh ibn ʿAmr (radiya Allahu ʿanhuma) reported that he entered upon the Prophet ﷺ while he performed ablution deliberately, was told of six matters amid his Ummah beginning with his Prophets death which shook his heart, then affluence resented despite ten thousand, universal fitnah, plague like sheep murrain, truce and treachery with Banū al-Aṣfar after nine months, and a city asked about and named. > **""Six are among you, O Ummah: the death of your Prophet, one. Wealth overflows among you until a man is given ten thousand yet resents it, two. A fitnah entering the house of every man among you, three. Death like the murrain of sheep, four. A truce between you and Banū al-Aṣfar, they gather against you nine months like a womans pregnancy, then they are more treacherous than you, five. And conquest of a city, six. Constantinople.""**"
"12432","Muʿādh ibn Jabal (radiya Allahu ʿanhu) reported that the Messenger of Allah ﷺ enumerated six among the portents of the Hour from his death to Roman hosts under eighty banners. > **""Six are among the portents of the Hour: my death, conquest of Bayt al-Maqdis, death seizing people like the murrain of sheep, fitnah whose violence enters the house of every Muslim, a man given a thousand dīnārs yet resenting it, and Rome treacherously marching with eighty banners, under each twelve thousand.""**"
"12433","Jābir ibn ʿAbdillāh (radiya Allahu ʿanhuma) reported scarcity of locusts in a year of ʿUmars rule, his distress and dispatch of riders to Yemen, Shām, and Iraq, the arrival of a handful from Yemen, three takbīrs, then a Prophetic statement about a thousand communities and the locusts perishing first followed by succession like a severed string. > **""Allah created a thousand communities, six hundred in the sea and four hundred on land. The first of these communities to perish is the locust. When it perishes, they follow like a string when its thread is cut.""**"
"12434","ʿUtayy al-Saʿdī reported that he journeyed seeking knowledge to Kūfah, found ʿAbdullāh ibn Masʿūd among its people in its great mosque, sought beneficial knowledge, was questioned about his Basran Saʿdī origin, heard a Prophetic commendation of Banū Saʿd after a mans offer of abundant wealth, was invited to ask, inquired about a recognizable sign of the Hour, saw Ibn Masʿūd sit upright, heard that he had asked the same of the Messenger ﷺ, then received a long enumeration of signs and moral inversion ending with four mosques, alliances sundered, and hopes with works following. > **""Yes, O Ibn Masʿūd. The Hour has landmarks and portents. Among its landmarks and portents are that the child is anger, rain is scorching heat, the wicked overflow, the treacherous is trusted, the trustworthy is betrayed, the liar is believed, the truthful is denied, every tribe is led by its hypocrites, every market by its wicked, the mosque is adorned, pulpits are raised, hearts are estranged, increase traverses kinship, knowledge spreads while fornication and usury appear, the Qurʾān is taken as flutes, trust is taken as spoil, charity as loss, kinship visitation as favor, the liar is called truthful, the truthful called liar, the treacherous called trustworthy, the trustworthy called treacherous, rain increases while trees decrease, scholars decrease while reciters increase, hopes are pursued with works, the poor is scorned, knowledge is sought for other than Allah, the child angers his parents, a man satisfies his friend and angers his father, rain comes in unsuitable season, beggars circumambulate finding none to give, a man is honored from fear of his evil, nations call one another as diners to a bowl from every horizon, Mosque, mosque, and people hope. Alliances are cut, the year is like a month, a thousand dīnārs profit nothing, blood is lawful, the strong devours the weak, trade increases, women assist husbands, markets draw near, desires are followed, judgments by conjecture, earthquakes, enmity and envy and stinginess appear. O Ibn Masʿūd, among its landmarks are that the shepherds compete in building, brothers differ. This is the dawn whose morning is the Hour. Then four mosques are built: the Sacred Mosque, the mosque of Madīnah or the mosque of the Messenger, the mosque of Bayt al-Maqdis, and a mosque on a market of theirs called Quds. Alliances are sundered, unsheathed swords are many, hopes are pursued with works.""**"
"12435","ʿAwf ibn Mālik al-Ashjaʿī (radiya Allahu ʿanhu) reported that the Messenger of Allah ﷺ asked how he would fare when the Ummah splits into seventy-three sects, then upon inquiry detailed police, slave-mothers ruling, lowly on pulpits, Qurʾān as flutes, adorned mosques, raised pulpits, spoils as private wealth, zakāh as fine, trust as spoil, knowledge for other than Allah, disobedience to parents, cursing of predecessors, wicked chiefs, honored evil, refuge in Shām and Damascus, swift conquest then dark dusty fitnahs, and finally a man of his house called Mahdī to be followed. > **""How are you, O ʿAwf, when this Ummah splits into seventy-three sects, one in Paradise and the rest in Fire. When police increase, slave-girls own, litters sit upon pulpits, the Qurʾān is taken as flutes, mosques are adorned, pulpits raised, spoils taken as private wealth, zakāh as fine, trust as spoil, knowledge in religion sought for other than Allah, a man obeys his wife, disobeys his mother, distances his father, the last of this Ummah curses its first, the tribe is led by its profligate, the chief of the people is their vilest, a man is honored from fear of his evil. Then that occurs, people flee to Shām and to a city therein called Damascus, among the best cities of Shām, fortifying them from their enemy. Yes, soon. Then fitnahs occur after its conquest, then comes a dusty dark fitnah, then fitnahs follow one another until a man from my household emerges called Mahdī. If you reach him, follow him and be among the guided.""**"
"12436","Abū Mūsā (radiya Allahu ʿanhu) reported that the Messenger of Allah ﷺ was asked about the Hour in his presence, declared its knowledge with Allah alone, promised portents including fitnah and harj, explained harj in the Abyssinian tongue as killing, and described mutual estrangement, hardened hearts, and a wavering remnant unrecognizing good or evil. > **""None knows it but Allah, none manifests it in its time but He. But I tell you its conditions and what precedes it. Before it are fitnahs and harj. Harj in the Abyssinian tongue is killing. Mutual estrangement is cast among people so none knows another, hearts of people dry, and there remains a trembling remnant knowing no good nor denying evil.""**"
"12437","Abū Mūsā (radiya Allahu ʿanhu) reported that the Messenger of Allah ﷺ listed approaching inversions including dishonored scripture, contracted time, broken bonds, diminished years and fruits, trusted accused, liars believed, abundant harj explained as killing, then manifest transgression, envy, stinginess, discord, desire, conjecture, vanishing knowledge, ignorance, angry child, scorching winter, open lewdness, and earth soaked in blood. > **""The Hour will not come until the Book of Allah is dishonor, time draws near, its bonds break, years and fruits diminish, the accused are trusted, the trustworthy accused, the liar believed, the truthful denied, harj abounds. Killing, manifest transgression, envy, stinginess, matters differ among people, desire is followed, judgment by conjecture, knowledge is taken, ignorance appears, the child is anger, winter is scorch, lewdness is proclaimed, earth is soaked in blood.""**"
"12438","Abū Hurayrah (radiya Allahu ʿanhu) reported from the Messenger of Allah ﷺ an oath that the Hour awaits manifest lewdness and miserliness, inverted trust, perishing eminent and emerging lowly, explained as notable faces versus those beneath feet unknown. > **""By the One in Whose Hand is my soul, the Hour will not come until lewdness and miserliness appear, the trustworthy is betrayed, the traitor trusted, the eminent perish, the lowly appear. The eminent are faces and nobles of people, the lowly those beneath peoples feet unknown.""**"
"12439","Umm al-Ḍirāb (radiya Allahu ʿanha) reported her fathers death leaving her and a brother without wealth, her uncles arrival from Madīnah taking them to ʿĀʾishah, her admission behind the curtain as a girl while the boy remained outside, her uncles complaint of need, ʿĀʾishahs order of two griddle-cakes, two provision sacks, and two seats, then her quotation of the Prophet ﷺ on disobedient children, withheld rain, dominant mean, vanishing noble, and daring of small against great. > **""The Hour will not come until the child is anger, rain is scorch, the mean overflow, the noble ebb, the small dares against the great and the mean against the noble.""**"
"12440","Abū Dharr al-Ghifārī (radiya Allahu ʿanhu) reported from the Prophet ﷺ a comprehensive description of the nearing time including Ṭayālisah garments, trade, wealth, miserly rich, lewdness, child rule, many women, unjust sultan, short measure, preference for pups over children, disrespect to elders, mercilessness to young, abundant illegitimacy with roadside indecency mildly rebuked, wolf hearts in sheep skins, and exemplary dissemblers. > **""When time draws near, wearing of Ṭayālisah increases, trade increases, wealth increases, the owner of wealth is magnified, lewdness increases, rule is by children, women increase, the sultan tyrannizes, measure and scale are diminished. A man raising a pup is better for him than raising a child. The elder is not revered, the young not pitied. Children of adultery increase until a man assaults a woman on the roadside. The best among them then says: Had you withdrawn from the road. They wear sheep skins over wolf hearts. The best among them then is the flatterer.""**"
"12441","Anas ibn Mālik (radiya Allahu ʿanhu) reported in marfūʿ form from the Prophet ﷺ three among the portents including the crescent appearing enlarged, mosques taken as thoroughfares, and sudden death. > **""Among the portents of the Hour are that the crescent is seen for a night and said to be of two nights, mosques are taken as roads, and sudden death appears.""**"
"12442","ʿUmar ibn al-Khaṭṭāb (radiya Allahu ʿanhu) reported that the Messenger of Allah ﷺ counted among the portents the dominance of the vilest and the excellence of a believer between two noble parents. > **""Among the portents of the Hour is that Lukaʿ ibn Lukaʿ overcomes the world. The best of people then is a believer between two noble parents.""**"
"12443","Anas ibn Mālik (radiya Allahu ʿanhu) reported that the Messenger of Allah ﷺ declared that days and nights will not pass until the happiest with the world is the vilest of the vile. > **""Days and nights will not pass until the happiest of people with the world is Lukaʿ ibn Lukaʿ.""**"
"12444","Abū Dharr (radiya Allahu ʿanhu) reported that he heard the Messenger of Allah ﷺ condition the Hour upon vilest dominance and identify the nobly-born believer as most excellent. > **""The Hour will not come until Lukaʿ ibn Lukaʿ overcomes the world, and the best of people is a believer between two noble parents.""**"
"12445","Ibn ʿAbbās (radiya Allahu ʿanhuma) reported that the Messenger of Allah ﷺ foretold latter-day peoples with human faces and devil hearts, wolf-like rapacity, merciless bloodshed, shamelessness, flattery and backbiting, lying and betrayal, corrupt young and heedless elders, humiliating honor, impoverishing greed, misguided clemency, accused reformers, weakened believers, honored profligates, Sunnah deemed innovation and innovation Sunnah, then tyrannical overlords and unanswered supplication of the best. > **""There will come in the latter time peoples whose faces are human faces and hearts devil hearts, like rapacious wolves, no mercy in their hearts, shedders of blood, unrestrained from ugliness. If you follow them they conceal you, if you hide from them they backbite you, if they speak they lie to you, if you trust them they betray you. Their child is insolent, youth profligate, elder enjoins no good nor forbids evil. Honor through them is humiliation, seeking what is in their hands is poverty. The forbearing among them is astray, the enjoiner of good among them accused, the believer among them weakened, the profligate among them honored, Sunnah among them innovation, innovation among them Sunnah. Then Allah empowers their wicked over them, their best supplicate and are not answered.""**"
"12446","ʿAbdullāh ibn ʿAmr (radiya Allahu ʿanhuma) reported from the Messenger of Allah ﷺ that nearness of the Hour includes raising the wicked, lowering the good, ugly speech, withheld action, and recitation of al-Mathnāh, explained as writings besides the Book of Allah. > **""Among the nearness of the Hour is that the wicked are raised, the good lowered, speech is fouled, action is withheld, and al-Mathnāh is recited among the people. What is written besides the Book of Allah.""**"
"12447","Samurah (radiya Allahu ʿanhu) reported that the Messenger of Allah ﷺ conditioned the Hour upon mountains shifting and the sight of unprecedented mighty matters. > **""The Hour will not come until mountains shift from their places, and you see mighty matters you never saw.""**"
"12448","Samurah (radiya Allahu ʿanhu) reported that the Messenger of Allah ﷺ warned of dreadful unprecedented sights before the Hour bidding remembrance of Allah, mountains shifting before the Blast, and a stone directing a Muslim pursuer to a hidden Jew concealed ahead. > **""You will see before the Hour comes things you deny as mighty, saying: Were we told of this. When you see that, remember Allah and know they are the beginnings of the Hour. You will see mountains shift before the Blast. The Hour will not come until the stone directs to the Jew hiding, pursued by a Muslim man, appearing before him and hiding, and the stone says: O servant of Allah, this is what you seek.""**"
"12449","Abū Hurayrah (radiya Allahu ʿanhu) reported as a portent that stinginess and lewdness appear, trust inverts, women clad yet naked appear in garments, and the lowly rise above the eminent, whereupon he asked ʿAbdullāh ibn Masʿūd whether he heard it likewise from his beloved, who swore by the Lord of the Kaʿbah affirmatively, and explained the lowly as base men of obscure houses raised above their righteous, and the eminent as people of righteous houses. > **""Among the portents of the Hour are that stinginess and lewdness appear, the traitor is trusted, the trustworthy betrayed, garments appear worn by women clad yet naked, and the lowly rise above the eminent. The lowly are base men and people of obscure houses raised above their righteous, and the eminent are people of righteous houses.""**"
"12450","Abū Hurayrah (radiya Allahu ʿanhu) reported that the Messenger of Allah ﷺ conditioned the Hour upon manifest fitnahs, abundant lying, near markets, near time, and abundant harj explained as killing. > **""The Hour will not come until fitnahs appear, lying increases, markets draw near, time draws near, harj increases. Killing.""**"
"12451","ʿAbdullāh ibn ʿAmr (radiya Allahu ʿanhuma) reported that the Messenger of Allah ﷺ conditioned the Hour upon manifest lewdness, severed kinship, evil neighbor, and betrayed trust, then upon inquiry likened the believer to a palm unspoiled when fallen and unbroken when eaten yielding wholesomeness, and to gold emerging purer from fire. > **""The Hour will not come until lewdness, severance of kinship, evil neighbor appear, and the trustworthy is betrayed. Like the palm, fallen yet unspoiled, eaten yet unbroken, yielding wholesomeness, and like a piece of gold entering fire and emerging only purer.""**"
"12452","ʿAbdullāh ibn ʿAmr (radiya Allahu ʿanhuma) reported that the Messenger of Allah ﷺ conditioned the Hour upon public roadside indecency like donkeys. > **""The Hour will not come until they mate in the road mating as donkeys.""**"
"12453","Anas ibn Mālik (radiya Allahu ʿanhu) reported that the Messenger of Allah ﷺ counted among the portents lewdness and foul speech, severed kinships, and trusting the traitor, with a doubtful addition about betraying the trustworthy or similar wording. > **""Among the portents of the Hour are lewdness and foul speech, severance of kinships, trusting the traitor, and betraying the trustworthy.""**"
"12454","ʿAbdullāh ibn Masʿūd (radiya Allahu ʿanhu) reported that the Messenger of Allah ﷺ conditioned the Hour upon every tribe being led by its hypocrites. > **""The Hour will not come until every tribe is led by its hypocrites.""**"
"12455","Abū Bakrah (radiya Allahu ʿanhu) reported that the Messenger of Allah ﷺ conditioned the Hour upon every tribe being led by its hypocrites. > **""The Hour will not come until every tribe is led by its hypocrites.""**"
"12456","ʿAlī ibn Abī Ṭālib (radiya Allahu ʿanhu) reported that the Messenger of Allah ﷺ led them in dawn prayer, a man called asking about the Hour, was rebuked to silence, then at brightness the Prophet ﷺ gazed to heaven praising its Raiser and Disposer and to earth praising its Spreader and Creator, summoned the inquirer who knelt in filial devotion, then linked the Hour to unjust leaders, belief in stars, denial of decree, trust as spoil, charity as fine, and increased lewdness bringing ruin to his people. > **""Be silent. Blessed is its Raiser and Disposer. Blessed is its Spreader and Creator. That is at injustice of leaders, belief in stars, denial of decree, until trust is taken as spoil, charity as fine, lewdness as increase. Then your people perish.""**"
"12457","ʿAbdullāh ibn Masʿūd (radiya Allahu ʿanhu) enjoined obedience and community as the rope of Allah, preferring disliked unity to beloved division, observing that all creation has an appointed end and Islām after firm advent nears its end fluctuating until the Hour, whose sign is spreading want with severed provision so the poor finds none to sustain him and the rich deems his own insufficient, kinsmen withholding complaint, beggars walking between Fridays receiving nothing, then earth convulsing each courtyard supposing its own, calming by divine will, then suddenly disgorging its liver-pieces, explained when asked as pillars of gold and silver after which gold and silver benefit none until the Hour."
"12458","Wāthilah ibn al-Asqaʿ (radiya Allahu ʿanhu) reported that he heard the Messenger of Allah ﷺ condition the Hour upon ten signs spanning east and west, Arabia, Dajjāl, descent of ʿĪsā ibn Maryam, Gog and Magog, the Beast, sunrise from the west, and a fire from the depths of ʿAdan driving people including ants to the gathering. > **""The Hour will not come until ten signs: swallowing in the east, swallowing in the west, swallowing in the Arabian Peninsula, Dajjāl, descent of ʿĪsā ibn Maryam, Gog and Magog, the Beast, sunrise from its west, a fire emerging from the depths of ʿAdan driving people to the gathering, gathering ants and small creatures.""**"
"12459","Ṭāriq ibn Shihāb reported that they sat with ʿAbdullāh ibn Masʿūd, a man announced prayer, they entered finding people bowing at the mosques front, bowed and advanced likewise, a hurrying man greeted Abū ʿAbd al-Raḥmān, who replied that Allah and His Messenger spoke truth and messengers conveyed, and after prayer and return his companions sought explanation through Ṭāriq, who asked and received a Prophetic statement about exclusive greeting, spreading trade with wifely aid, severed kinship, false witness, concealed true witness, and manifest knowledge. > **""Before the Hour are exclusive greeting, spread of trade when the wife aids her husband in trade, severance of kinship, false witness, concealment of true witness, appearance of knowledge.""**"
"12460","The narrator reported a Prophetic variant that among the portents a man greets only for acquaintance, with an addition that a man passes the mosque without praying therein. > **""Among the portents of the Hour is that a man greets, greeting only for acquaintance. A man passes the mosque and does not pray therein.""**"
"12461","The narrator reported that he heard the Messenger of Allah ﷺ condition the Hour upon greeting by acquaintance, noting the greeter recognized him among them, and added mosques taken as roads without prostration, youths dispatching elders as couriers between horizons, and merchants traversing horizons without profit. > **""The Hour will not come until greeting is by acquaintance. Mosques are taken as roads and Allah is not prostrated to therein, the boy sends the elder as courier between horizons, the merchant reaches between horizons finding no profit.""**"
"12462","The narrator reported a variant in his version that women and horses grow costly then cheap never to be costly until the Hour, and man and wife trade together. > **""Women and horses grow costly then cheap and are never costly until the Hour. Man and wife trade together.""**"
"12463","Al-ʿAddāʾ ibn Khālid (radiya Allahu ʿanhu) reported that he heard the Messenger of Allah ﷺ condition the Hour upon greeting only acquaintances, mosques as roads, joint trade of woman and husband, and cheapening of women and horses never costly until the Hour. > **""The Hour will not come until a man greets only whom he knows, mosques are taken as roads, the wife and her husband trade, women and horses cheapen and are never costly until the Hour.""**"
"12464","Ibn ʿUmar (radiya Allahu ʿanhuma) reported that the Messenger of Allah ﷺ counted among the signs of tribulation and the Hour the withdrawal of minds, decrease of forbearance, increase of killing, removal of marks of good, and appearance of fitnahs. > **""Among the signs of tribulation and portents of the Hour are that minds are withdrawn, forbearance decreases, killing increases, marks of good are lifted, fitnahs appear.""**"
"12465","ʿAbdullāh ibn Masʿūd (radiya Allahu ʿanhu) stated that the first lost of religion is trust and the last remaining is prayer, that a people will pray without religion, and that the Qurʾān will be removed from among them, explaining upon astonishment at recitation and codices that it will travel by night leaving mens breasts with nothing upon earth."
"12466","Al-Qāsim reported that the Euphrates was complained of to Ibn Masʿūd with fear of inundation and a request to dam it, he refused to dam, swore of a coming time when a basin of water is unfound, all water returns to its origin containing water while Muslims are in Shām."
"12467","ʿUrwah ibn Muḥammad al-Saʿdī reported from his father from the Messenger of Allah ﷺ that three sights signal imminence including ruin of flourishing, flourishing of ruin, warfare as stipend, and a man abrading his trust as a camel abrades a tree. > **""Three, when you see them then imminence is at hand: ruin of the flourishing, flourishing of the ruin, warfare as stipend, a man abrading with his trust as the camel abrades the tree.""**"
"12468","Kaʿb ibn ʿUjrah (radiya Allahu ʿanhu) reported that the Messenger of Allah ﷺ conditioned the Hour upon a single man managing the affairs of fifty women. > **""The Hour will not come until a man manages the affairs of fifty women.""**"
"12469","ʿAwf ibn Mālik (radiya Allahu ʿanhu) reported that the Messenger of Allah ﷺ foretold deceptive years before Dajjāl with abundant rain but scant vegetation, truthful denied and liar believed, traitor trusted and trustworthy betrayed, and speech by Ruwaybiḍah, explained upon inquiry as the disregarded. > **""Before Dajjāl are deceptive years, rain therein abundant, vegetation therein scant, the truthful therein denied, the liar therein believed, the traitor therein trusted, the trustworthy therein betrayed, and Ruwaybiḍah speaks therein. One given no heed.""**"
"12470","Anas (radiya Allahu ʿanhu) reported that the Messenger of Allah ﷺ conditioned the Hour upon general rain without growth, with a variant discussion about fifty women under one guardian and a woman passing a man seized and inspected with remark about her former husband, transmitted through Ḥammād from Thābit without doubt. > **""The Hour will not come until heaven rains a general rain and earth sprouts nothing, until fifty women have one guardian, until a woman passes a man who seizes her and looks at her saying: She once had a husband.""**"
"12471","Anas (radiya Allahu ʿanhu) reported that the Messenger of Allah ﷺ conditioned the Hour upon cessation of Allah Allah upon earth, rain without growth, one guardian for fifty women, and a shod woman remarking about a former husband. > **""The Hour will not come until Allah Allah is no longer said upon earth, people are rained upon yet earth sprouts not, fifty women have one guardian, a woman passes in sandals saying: She once had a husband.""**"
"12472","ʿAbd al-Raḥmān al-Anṣārī (radiya Allahu ʿanhu) reported that the Messenger of Allah ﷺ counted among the nearness abundant rain, scant vegetation, many reciters, few jurists, many leaders, few trustworthy. > **""Among the nearness of the Hour are abundant rain, scant vegetation, many reciters, few jurists, many leaders, few trustworthy.""**"
"12473","Abū Hurayrah (radiya Allahu ʿanhu) reported that the Messenger of Allah ﷺ conditioned the Hour upon sheltering rain that mud-brick houses cannot withstand, only hair houses withstand. > **""The Hour will not come until heaven rains a rain that mud-brick houses do not shelter from, and only hair houses shelter from it.""**"
"12474","Abū Hurayrah (radiya Allahu ʿanhu) reported that the Messenger of Allah ﷺ conditioned the Hour upon Arabia returning to meadows and rivers, safe riding between Iraq and Makkah fearing only straying, and abundant harj explained as killing. > **""The Hour will not come until the land of the Arabs returns to meadows and rivers, a rider travels between Iraq and Makkah fearing only straying from the road, harj increases. Killing.""**"
"12475","Abū Hurayrah (radiya Allahu ʿanhu) reported that the Messenger of Allah ﷺ conditioned the Hour upon nearness of time with the year like the month, month like the week, week like the day, day like the burning of a palm frond. > **""The Hour will not come until time draws near, the year is like the month, the month like the week, the week like the day, the day like the burning of the palm-leaf.""**"
"12476","Abū Hurayrah (radiya Allahu ʿanhu) reported from the Prophet ﷺ an oath that this Ummah will not perish until a man approaches a woman spreading her in the road, whose best then suggests concealment behind a wall. > **""By the One in Whose Hand is my soul, this Ummah will not perish until a man rises to a woman and spreads her in the road. The best among them then says: Had you concealed her behind this wall.""**"
"12477","ʿAbd al-Raḥmān ibn Shibl (radiya Allahu ʿanhu) reported that the Messenger of Allah ﷺ declared that night and day will not pass until a sandal is found in rubbish attributed to Quraysh. > **""Night and day will not pass until a sandal is found in rubbish and said to be as if the sandal of a Qurashī.""**"
"12478","Abū Hurayrah (radiya Allahu ʿanhu) reported that the Messenger of Allah ﷺ conditioned the Hour upon many mines inhabited only by the vilest of people. > **""The Hour will not come until many mines appear inhabited only by the vilest of people.""**"
"12479","Anas ibn Mālik (radiya Allahu ʿanhu) reported that the Messenger of Allah ﷺ warned that when his Ummah deems six lawful, ruin befalls including mutual cursing, wine, silk, singing girls, and same-sex contentment of men with men and women with women. > **""When my Ummah deems six lawful, ruin is upon them: when mutual cursing appears among them, they drink wine, wear silk, take singing girls, men suffice with men and women with women.""**"
"12480","Abū Hurayrah (radiya Allahu ʿanhu) reported that the Messenger of Allah ﷺ foretold a time when a sturdy camel and two waterskins are dearer to a man than his possessions. > **""There comes upon people a time when a sturdy camel and two waterskins are dearer to a man than what he owns.""**"
"12481","Ḥudhayfah (radiya Allahu ʿanhu) reported that the Prophet of Allah ﷺ foretold twenty-seven liars and impostors in his Ummah including four women, affirming himself the seal with no prophet after him. > **""In my Ummah are twenty-seven liars and impostors, among them four women. I am the seal of prophets, no prophet after me.""**"
"12482","Abū Bakrah رضي الله عنه reported that people spoke excessively about Musaylimah before the Messenger of Allah ﷺ had said anything concerning him, until the Messenger of Allah ﷺ stood among them to deliver a khuṭbah about him, opening with ammā baʿd. > **""Ammā baʿd, concerning this man about whom you have spoken much, he is a liar among thirty liars who emerge before the Hour. There is no land but the terror of the Masīḥ reaches it, except Madīnah, upon every pass of its passes are two angels repelling from it the terror of the Masīḥ.""**"
"12483","Jābir رضي الله عنه reported that the Messenger of Allah ﷺ said that before the Hour there will be liars, including the man of al-Yamāmah and the ʿAnsī of Ṣanʿāʾ and the man of Ḥimyar and the Dajjāl, with Jābir adding that some narrate the wording as near thirty liars. > **""Before the Hour there will be liars, among them the man of al-Yamāmah, and among them the ʿAnsī of Ṣanʿāʾ, and among them the man of Ḥimyar, and among them the Dajjāl, and he is the greatest of them in fitnah.""**"
"12484","ʿAbdullāh ibn ʿUmar رضي الله عنهما reported that a man of Kūfah was with him narrating about al-Mukhtār, whereupon Ibn ʿUmar replied that if the matter were as described, he had heard the Messenger of Allah ﷺ speak of thirty impostors before the Hour. > **""Before the Hour there will be thirty Dajjāls, liars.""**"
"12485","ʿAbd al-Raḥmān ibn Abī Nuʿaym, with doubt of Abū al-Walīd whether Nuʿaym al-Aʿrajī, reported while present that a man asked Ibn ʿUmar رضي الله عنهما about mutʿah with women, and Ibn ʿUmar swore they were neither fornicators nor takers of secret lovers in the time of the Messenger of Allah ﷺ, then swore he heard the Messenger of Allah ﷺ foretell the Dajjāl and more than thirty liars, with a further narration that before the Dajjāl are thirty or more liars whose mark was described and avoidance commanded. > **""Before the Day of Resurrection there will be the Dajjāl and thirty liars or more. Before the Hour is the Dajjāl, and before the Dajjāl are thirty liars or more. Their sign is that they bring you a practice you were not upon, altering thereby your Sunnah and your dīn. When you see them, avoid them and oppose them.""**"
"12486","Abū al-Jallās reported that he heard ʿAlī رضي الله عنه say to ʿAbdullāh al-Sabāʾī, woe to you, swearing that nothing was confided to him concealing it from people, but that he heard the Prophet ﷺ speak of thirty liars before the Hour, addressing the man as one of them. > **""Before the Hour there will be thirty liars, and you are indeed one of them.""**"
"12487","Unaysah bint Zayd ibn Arqam رضي الله عنها reported that Zayd ibn Arqam رضي الله عنه entered upon al-Mukhtār, who said, O Abū ʿĀmir, had you come earlier you would have seen Jibrīl and Mīkāʾīl, whereupon Zayd belittled him and declared him more insignificant before Allah than that. > **""You are petty and base. You are more insignificant before Allah than that, a liar fabricating against Allah and His Messenger.""**"
"12488","Abū Isḥāq reported that he said to ʿAbdullāh ibn ʿUmar رضي الله عنهما that al-Mukhtār claimed revelation came to him, whereupon Ibn ʿUmar affirmed it in the sense of the verse that the devils inspire their allies. > **""He spoke the truth, for the devils indeed inspire their allies.""**"
"12489","Anas رضي الله عنه reported that the Messenger of Allah ﷺ foretold more than seventy impostors before the emergence of the Dajjāl. > **""Before the emergence of the Dajjāl there will be seventy and some Dajjāls.""**"
"12490","ʿAbdullāh ibn ʿAmr رضي الله عنهما reported that the Messenger of Allah ﷺ conditioned the Hour upon the emergence of seventy liars. > **""The Hour will not stand until seventy liars emerge.""**"
"12491","ʿAbdullāh ibn al-Zubayr رضي الله عنهما reported from the Prophet ﷺ that before the Hour there will be thirty liars, among them al-Aswad al-ʿAnsī and the man of Ṣanʿāʾ and the man of al-Yamāmah. > **""Before the Hour there will be thirty liars, among them al-Aswad al-ʿAnsī and the man of Ṣanʿāʾ and the man of al-Yamāmah.""**"
"12492","Shuʿayb ibn ʿUmar reported that during ḥajj they lost the road at Munqadir, met a Bedouin who named the place al-Dhawāʾib and the white mound and the grave of Kulayb, then led him to a leather tent to a man with brow bandaged, identified as al-ʿAddāʾ ibn Khālid ibn ʿAmr ibn ʿĀmir, horseman of al-Ḍaḥyāʾ in Jāhiliyyah, whom he asked for a ḥadīth heard from the Messenger of Allah ﷺ, and the man told of the Prophet ﷺ rising alarmed while Ibn Masʿūd رضي الله عنه questioned him about the one-eyed Dajjāl and the greatest liar and the third liar. > **""Beware the three Dajjāls. A man emerges among a people whose first is destroyed and whose last is cut off, upon them lasting curse, in a fitnah called al-Ḥāriqah, and he is the gilded Dajjāl devouring the servants of Allah.""**"
"12493","al-Nuʿmān ibn Bashīr رضي الله عنه reported that he heard the Messenger of Allah ﷺ foretell impostors before the Hour. > **""Before the Hour there will be liars.""**"
"12494","Salāmah bint Abjar رضي الله عنها reported that she heard the Messenger of Allah ﷺ foretell two men in Thaqīf, a liar and a shedder of blood. > **""In Thaqīf there will be a liar and a destroyer.""**"
"12495","ʿAlī رضي الله عنه reported from the Prophet ﷺ that they mentioned the Dajjāl in his presence while he slept, then he awoke with reddened complexion and said something else was more fearful for them, uttering a word. > **""Other than that is more fearful for you from me.""**"
"12496","ʿAbdullāh ibn Ḥawālah رضي الله عنه reported that the Messenger of Allah ﷺ thrice repeated that whoever is saved from three is saved, naming his death and the Dajjāl and the killing of a rightly patient caliph who renders what is due. > **""Whoever is saved from three is saved, whoever is saved from three is saved, whoever is saved from three is saved: my death, and the Dajjāl, and the killing of a rightly patient caliph who gives the right.""**"
"12497","ʿUqbah ibn ʿĀmir رضي الله عنه reported that the Messenger of Allah ﷺ named three trials from which salvation is true salvation, at the killing of a believer and at the killing of a wronged patient caliph who gives the right from himself and at the fitnah of the Dajjāl. > **""Three, whoever is saved from them is saved: whoever is saved at the killing of a believer is saved, and whoever is saved at the killing of a caliph killed wronged while he is patient giving the right from himself is saved, and whoever is saved from the fitnah of the Dajjāl is saved.""**"
"12498","Ḥudhayfah رضي الله عنه reported that the Dajjāl was mentioned before the Messenger of Allah ﷺ, who said he feared for them the fitnah of some among them more than the Dajjāl, that none escapes the prior trial but escapes the later, and that every fitnah since the world began serves the Dajjāl. > **""I fear for you the fitnah of some of you more than the fitnah of the Dajjāl. None is saved from what is before it but is saved from it. No fitnah has been wrought since the world began, small or great, except for the fitnah of the Dajjāl.""**"
"12499","Rāshid ibn Saʿd reported that when Iṣṭakhr was conquered a caller cried that the Dajjāl had emerged, whereupon al-Ṣaʿb ibn Jaththāmah رضي الله عنه met them and said, but for what they were saying, he would tell them what he heard from the Messenger of Allah ﷺ about people growing heedless of his mention and imams abandoning it on the minbars. > **""The Dajjāl will not emerge until people grow heedless of his mention, and until the imams abandon his mention upon the minbars.""**"
"12500","ʿĀʾishah رضي الله عنها reported that the Messenger of Allah ﷺ mentioned the hardship before the Dajjāl, and when asked about the best wealth then, named a strong youth watering his folk while there is no food, and when asked about the believers food, named glorification and magnification and declaration of unity, and when she asked about the Arabs then, said they will be few. > **""A strong youth watering his folk with water, and as for food, there is none. The food of the believers then is tasbīḥ and takbīr and tahlīl. The Arabs then will be few.""**"
"12501","ʿAbdullāh ibn Mughaffal رضي الله عنه reported that the Messenger of Allah ﷺ said Allah sent no greater fitnah from the creation of Ādam to the Hour than the Dajjāl, then spoke a word none before him spoke, describing him wheat-coloured curly-haired with left eye effaced and thick film over it, healing the blind and leprous and claiming lordship, with safety for whoever says my Lord is Allah and trial for whoever accepts him, remaining as Allah wills, then ʿĪsā ibn Maryam descending affirming Muḥammad ﷺ upon his creed as guided imam and just judge, killing the Dajjāl, with al-Ḥasan holding that to be at the Hour. > **""Allah has sent down to earth since He created Ādam until the Hour no fitnah greater than the fitnah of the Dajjāl. I shall say concerning him a word none before me said: he is wheat-coloured curly-haired, the left eye effaced, upon his eye a thick film, healing the blind and the leprous, saying, I am your lord. Whoever says, my Lord is Allah, there is no fitnah upon him, and whoever says, you are my lord, is tried. He remains among you as Allah wills, then ʿĪsā ibn Maryam descends affirming Muḥammad upon his creed as guided imam and just judge, and kills the Dajjāl.""**"
"12502","ʿAbdullāh ibn al-Ḥārith ibn Jazʾ رضي الله عنه reported that they heard no fright nor clamour in Madīnah but thought it the Dajjāl, because of what the Messenger of Allah ﷺ narrated to them about him and drew near to them of his affair."
"12503","Sahl ibn Ḥunayf رضي الله عنه reported a dispute between Salmān al-Fārisī رضي الله عنه and a man, in which Salmān prayed that if the man lied Allah not cause him to die until overtaken by one of three, and when his anger subsided and he was asked what he invoked, he named the trials to be disclosed. > **""I tell you of the fitnah of the Dajjāl, and the fitnah of a ruler like the fitnah of the Dajjāl, and miserly avarice cast upon people, when a man gains wealth caring not from what he gained it.""**"
"12504","Samurah ibn Jundab رضي الله عنه reported that the Messenger of Allah ﷺ said the Dajjāl will emerge blind in the left eye with thick film, healing the blind and leprous and giving life to the dead and saying to people I am your lord, with whoever affirms him tried and whoever holds to my Lord is Allah protected until death, remaining as Allah wills, then ʿĪsā ibn Maryam emerging before the west affirming Muḥammad ﷺ and killing the Dajjāl, and that is the standing of the Hour. > **""The Dajjāl will emerge blind in the left eye, upon it a thick film, healing the blind and the leprous, giving life to the dead, saying to people, I am your lord. Whoever says, you are my lord, is tried, and whoever says, my Lord is Allah, until dying upon that is protected from the fitnah of the Dajjāl and no fitnah is upon him. He remains in the earth as Allah wills, then ʿĪsā ibn Maryam emerges before the west affirming Muḥammad, and kills the Dajjāl, and that is the standing of the Hour.""**"
"12505","Abū Saʿīd al-Khudrī رضي الله عنه reported that the Messenger of Allah ﷺ warned his people as every prophet warned, describing the Dajjāl one-eyed bulging unhidden like spittle beside a wall with left eye like a glittering star, with a likeness of garden and fire whose garden is a smoky spring and whose fire is a green meadow, two men warning villagers before him, and power over one man alone whom he slaughters and strikes and raises, asking his companions am I not your lord while they testify shirk, while the slaughtered man calls out to people identifying the Masīḥ al-Dajjāl the Messenger warned of, repeated a second and third time with only insight increased, then a fourth attempt when Allah places a copper plate upon his throat so slaughter fails, after which people plant and sow, with Abū Saʿīd adding they thought the man ʿUmar. > **""Every prophet warned his people of the Dajjāl, and I warn you of him. He is one-eyed with bulging pupil unhidden, like spittle beside a wall, and his left eye like a glittering star. With him is a likeness of garden and fire: his garden is a smoky spring and his fire a green meadow. Before him are two men warning the villagers; whenever they leave a village his vanguard enters it. He is given power over a man, over none besides him: he slaughters him, strikes him with his staff, says, stand, says to his companions, what do you see, am I not your lord, and they testify to him with shirk. The slaughtered man says, O people, this is the Masīḥ al-Dajjāl whom the Messenger of Allah warned us of. This has only increased me in insight concerning you. Then Allah places upon his throat a plate of copper and he cannot slaughter him.""**"
"12506","Saʿd ibn Abī Waqqāṣ رضي الله عنه reported that the Messenger of Allah ﷺ said every prophet described the Dajjāl to his Ummah, and he would describe a trait none before him described, that he is one-eyed while Allah is not one-eyed. > **""Every prophet described the Dajjāl to his Ummah, and I shall describe him with a trait none before me described: he is one-eyed, and Allah is not one-eyed.""**"
"12507","Abū Bakrah رضي الله عنه reported that the Messenger of Allah ﷺ said the Dajjāl is blind in the left eye with kāfir written between his eyes, read by the illiterate and the literate. > **""The Dajjāl is blind in the left eye, between his eyes is written kāfir, read by the illiterate and the literate.""**"
"12508","Ubayy ibn Kaʿb رضي الله عنه reported that the Messenger of Allah ﷺ mentioned the Dajjāl, likening one of his eyes to green glass, and commanded seeking refuge from the torment of the grave. > **""One of his eyes is as though green glass. Seek refuge with Allah from the torment of the grave.""**"
"12509","Ibn ʿAbbās رضي الله عنهما reported from the Prophet ﷺ concerning the Dajjāl that he is one-eyed fair ruddy as though his head a viper, most resembling ʿAbd al-ʿUzzā ibn Qaṭan, while the perishing perish, yet their Lord is not one-eyed. > **""One-eyed fair ruddy, as though his head a viper, most resembling ʿAbd al-ʿUzzā ibn Qaṭan. As for the destruction of the destroyed, your Lord is not one-eyed.""**"
"12510","Ibn ʿAbbās رضي الله عنهما reported in a narration that the Prophet ﷺ said he saw the Dajjāl fair huge elephantine as though his hair tree branches, one-eyed as though his eyes the morning star, most resembling ʿAbd al-ʿUzzā ibn Qaṭan of Khuzāʿah. > **""I saw the Dajjāl fair huge elephantine, as though his hair tree branches, one-eyed as though his eyes the morning star, most resembling ʿAbd al-ʿUzzā ibn Qaṭan, a man of Khuzāʿah.""**"
"12511","ʿAbdullāh ibn ʿUmar رضي الله عنهما reported that they spoke of the Farewell Ḥajj unaware it was farewell, until in that ḥajj the Messenger of Allah ﷺ preached and dwelt at length on the Masīḥ al-Dajjāl, declaring every prophet warned his Ummah including Nūḥ and those after him, and that whatever is hidden of his affair, their Lord is not one-eyed. > **""Allah sent no prophet but he warned his Ummah of him. Nūḥ warned of him and the prophets after him. Whatever is hidden to you of his affair, let it not be hidden to you that your Lord is not one-eyed.""**"
"12512","ʿĀʾishah رضي الله عنها reported that the Messenger of Allah ﷺ entered upon her weeping, asked what made her weep, and she said remembrance of the Dajjāl, whereupon he said if he emerges while among them he suffices them, and if after him their Lord is not one-eyed, then told of emergence from the Jewry of Iṣbahān to Madīnah with seven gates each pass guarded by two angels, its worst joining him to Shām at Lud of Palestine, where ʿĪsā ibn Maryam descends and kills him and remains forty years a just imam and equitable judge. > **""What makes you weep. If he emerges while I am among you, you are sufficed against him, and if he emerges after me, your Lord is not one-eyed. He emerges from the Jewry of Iṣbahān until coming to Madīnah, alighting at its side, with seven gates that day, upon every pass two angels. Its worst go out to him until he comes to Shām, to Lud of Palestine. ʿĪsā ibn Maryam descends and kills him, and ʿĪsā remains in the earth forty years a just imam and equitable judge.""**"
"12513","Anas ibn Mālik رضي الله عنه reported that the Messenger of Allah ﷺ said the Dajjāl emerges from the Jews of Iṣbahān, with an addition that seventy thousand Jews wearing green cloaks are with him. > **""The Dajjāl emerges from the Jews of Iṣbahān, with him seventy thousand Jews wearing green cloaks.""**"
"12514","Junādah ibn Umayyah رضي الله عنه reported that people visited Muʿādh ibn Jabal رضي الله عنه ill and asked for an undoubted ḥadīth, so he sat up and narrated only what he heard from the Messenger of Allah ﷺ, that every prophet warned his Ummah of the Dajjāl, one-eyed with kāfir between his eyes read by literate and illiterate, with garden and fire whose fire is garden and whose garden is fire. ʿImrān ibn Ḥuṣayn رضي الله عنه separately reported the Dajjāl emerges from Iṣbahān. > **""Every prophet warned his Ummah of the Dajjāl, and I warn you of the Dajjāl. He is one-eyed, written between his eyes is kāfir, read by the literate and the illiterate. With him are a garden and a fire: his fire is a garden and his garden a fire. The Dajjāl emerges from Iṣbahān.""**"
"12515","Fāṭimah bint Qays رضي الله عنها reported that she heard the Messenger of Allah ﷺ call for congregational prayer, went with Anṣārī women, prayed Ẓuhr, then he ascended the minbar raising his hands till his armpits showed, thrice declaring this is Ṭaybah, then first placing him in the sea of Shām, then after a spell and relief correcting to the sea of al-ʿIrāq, emerging from Iṣbahān at Rustaqābādh with seventy thousand in cloaks before him, with a river of water and a river of fire, commanding whoever is told enter water not to enter for it is fire, and whoever is told enter fire to enter for it is water. > **""Shall I not tell you this is Ṭaybah. Shall I not tell you he is in the sea of Shām. Rather he is in the sea of al-ʿIrāq, rather he is in the sea of al-ʿIrāq. When he emerges, he emerges from a town called Iṣbahān, from a village of its villages called Rustaqābādh. When he emerges, at his vanguard are seventy thousand wearing cloaks. With him are two rivers, a river of water and a river of fire. Whoever of you reaches that and is told, enter the water, let him not enter, for it is fire, and when told, enter the fire, let him enter, for it is water.""**"
"12516","Salamah ibn al-Akwaʿ رضي الله عنه reported that returning with the Messenger of Allah ﷺ from al-ʿAqīq at Thaniyyat al-Ḥawḍ, the Prophet ﷺ gestured east and said he beheld the stations of the Masīḥ, his advance and alighting, the rabble joining him, every Madīnah pass guarded by one or two angels, two images of garden and fire with him, devils in the likeness of the dead telling the living I am your brother or father or kinsman, this is our lord so follow him, then Allah decreeing, and Allah sending a Muslim man silencing and refuting him and warning people he is a liar speaking falsehood and their Lord is not one-eyed, asked will you follow me and refusing, so split in two then restored more denouncing, challenging return him again, then ordered to the fire which is the image of garden, then setting out toward Shām. > **""I behold the stations of the enemy of Allah, the Masīḥ. He advances until alighting at such-and-such, until the rabble go out to him. No pass of Madīnah but upon it an angel or two angels guarding it. With him are two images, the image of garden and the image of fire. With him are devils resembling the dead, saying to the living, do you know me, I am your brother or your father or your kinsman, have I not gone before, this is our lord so follow him. O people, let him not deceive you, for he is a liar speaking falsehood, and your Lord is not one-eyed. O people, what you have seen is a trial you are tried with and a fitnah you are afflicted with. He is a liar. He goes out toward Shām.""**"
"12517","Safīnah رضي الله عنه reported that the Messenger of Allah ﷺ preached that no prophet before him but warned his Ummah of the Dajjāl, blind in the left eye with thick film over the right, kāfir between his eyes, with two valleys a garden and a fire whose garden is fire and whose fire is garden, with two angels resembling two prophets at his right and left as trial, saying am I not your lord giving life and death, while one angel says you lie heard by none but his fellow, who says you spoke truth heard by people who think he affirmed the Dajjāl, then journeying to Madīnah refused entry and calling it the town of that man, then to Shām where Allah destroys him at ʿAqabat Afīq. > **""No prophet before me but warned his Ummah of the Dajjāl. He is blind in his left eye, upon his right eye a thick film, written between his eyes is kāfir. With him emerge two valleys, one a garden and the other a fire: his garden is a fire and his fire a garden. With him are two angels resembling two prophets, one at his right and the other at his left, and that is the trial of people. He says, am I not your lord, giving life and death. One of the two angels says, you lie, none of people hears it but his fellow, who says to him, you spoke truth, and people hear it and think he affirmed the Dajjāl, and that is a trial. Then he journeys until coming to Madīnah, not permitted therein, then says, this is the town of that man. Then he journeys until coming to Shām, and Allah destroys him at ʿAqabat Afīq.""**"
"12518","Sulaymān ibn Shihāb reported that ʿAbdullāh ibn Muʿtam رضي الله عنه, a Companion, stayed with him and narrated from the Prophet ﷺ that the Dajjāl is unhidden, coming from the east calling and followed, raising a banner and fighting and prevailing, until reaching al-Kūfah displaying the dīn of Allah and acting by it so followed and loved, then claiming I am a prophet so every sound mind flees and leaves him, then saying I am Allah, whereupon his eye is covered and his ear cut and kāfir written between his eyes unhidden to any Muslim, so all with a mustard grain of faith leave him, his companions the Magians Jews Christians and non-Arab polytheists, then a man summoned and killed and severed limb by limb before people, then gathered and struck with his staff and standing, claiming I am Allah who gives life and death, all sorcery upon eyes working nothing. > **""The Dajjāl is unhidden. He comes from the east and calls on my behalf and is followed. He sets up for people, fights them, prevails over them, until coming to al-Kūfah. He displays the dīn of Allah and acts by it, and is followed and loved for that. Then after that he says, I am a prophet, and every sound mind flees from that and leaves him. He remains after that until saying, I am Allah. His eye is covered, his ear is cut, written between his eyes is kāfir, hidden to no Muslim. Every one of creation with a mustard grain of faith in his heart leaves him. His companions and troops are the Magians and the Jews and the Christians and these non-Arabs of the polytheists. All that is sorcery bewitching peoples eyes, working nothing of that.""**"
"12519","Thaʿlabah ibn ʿAbbād al-ʿAbdī of Baṣrah reported attending a khuṭbah of Samurah ibn Jundab رضي الله عنه in which the eclipse ḥadīth was cited until the sun cleared at sitting in the second rakʿah, then salām and praise and testimony that he is Allahs servant and Messenger, then adjuring whether he fell short in conveying, the men testifying conveyance and counsel and duty, then ammā baʿd refuting claims of eclipse for deaths of great men as signs testing servants for repentance, swearing he saw since praying what they will meet of world and hereafter, that the Hour stands not till thirty liars emerge last the one-eyed Dajjāl effaced left eye like the eye of Abū Taḥyā, an aged Anṣārī then between him and ʿĀʾishahs chamber, claiming divinity when he emerges, nullifying prior good of believers in him and excusing prior ill of deniers, appearing over all earth except the Ḥaram and Bayt al-Maqdis, believers besieged therein shaken severely, then Allah destroying him till wall base or tree root calls O believer, O Muslim, this Jew or kāfir, come kill him, only after grave matters and mutual questioning and mountains moved, then seizure after that, repeated verbatim in another khuṭbah, with a variant that holding to Allah and saying my Lord is Allah living undying brings no torment, and saying you are my lord is fitnah. > **""O people, I adjure you by Allah, if you know I fell short in anything of conveying the messages of my Lord, tell me that. Ammā baʿd, men claim this eclipse of sun and eclipse of moon and falling of stars from their risings is for deaths of great men of earth, and they lied. They are signs of Allah testing thereby His servants, seeing who brings forth repentance. The Hour will not stand until thirty liars emerge, last of them the one-eyed Dajjāl, effaced left eye as the eye of Abū Taḥyā. When he emerges he claims he is Allah. Whoever believes him and affirms him and follows him, no prior righteous deed benefits him, and whoever disbelieves him and belies him is not punished for any prior deed. He appears over all the earth except the Ḥaram and Bayt al-Maqdis. The believers are besieged in Bayt al-Maqdis and shaken severely, then Allah destroys him, until the base of a wall or the root of a tree calls, O believer, O Muslim, this Jew, this kāfir, come kill him. Whoever holds fast to Allah and says, my Lord is Allah, living undying, there is no torment upon him, and whoever says, you are my lord, is tried.""**"
"12520","Abū Naḍrah reported that they came to ʿUthmān ibn Abī al-ʿĀṣ رضي الله عنه on Friday to collate their muṣḥaf, were ordered to bathe and perfume and sit in the mosque, where he said he heard the Messenger of Allah ﷺ foretell three Muslim garrisons at the junction of two seas and at al-Ḥīrah and in Shām, three frights, the Dajjāl among people repelled from the east, first reaching the junction town whose people split into examiners and desert-joiners and joiners of the next town, with seventy thousand cloaked and most followers Jews and women, then the next town likewise splitting toward western Shām, Muslims withdrawing to ʿAqabat Afīq, their herds struck, severe hunger till eating bowstrings, then a dawn caller thrice relief has come, deemed a satiated voice, then ʿĪsā ibn Maryam at Fajr, his commander inviting him forward while he says this Ummah are commanders over one another, the commander leading, then ʿĪsā taking his spear toward the Dajjāl who melts like lead, placing it between his clavicles killing him, his companions routed, nothing sheltering them till tree and stone say O believer, this kāfir. > **""There will be three garrisons of the Muslims: a garrison at the junction of the two seas, and a garrison at al-Ḥīrah, and a garrison in Shām. People are seized by three frights. The Dajjāl emerges among people and is repelled from the east. The first garrison he comes to is the garrison at the junction of the two seas: its people become three bands, a band remaining saying, we examine him and see what he is, a band joining the Bedouins, and a band joining the garrison next to them. With the Dajjāl are seventy thousand wearing cloaks, most of his followers the Jews and the women. The Muslims withdraw to ʿAqabat Afīq. O people, relief has come to you. This Ummah are commanders, some over others. O believer, this kāfir.""**"
"12521","Hishām ibn ʿĀmir رضي الله عنه reported that the Messenger of Allah ﷺ described the Dajjāls head braided behind, with whoever says you are my lord tried, and whoever says you lie, my Lord is Allah, upon Him I rely, unharmed, or no fitnah upon him. > **""The head of the Dajjāl from behind is braided, braided. Whoever says, you are my lord, is tried, and whoever says, you lie, my Lord is Allah, upon Him I rely, he is not harmed. There is no fitnah upon him.""**"
"12522","Abū Qilābah reported seeing a man in Madīnah surrounded by people narrating that the Messenger of Allah ﷺ said, and he was a Companion, heard saying after them comes the misleading liar with knotted hair behind, claiming I am your lord, with safety for whoever disowns him and holds to Allah and turns to Him and seeks refuge from shirk. > **""After you is the misleading liar, his head from behind knotted, knotted, knotted. He will say, I am your lord. Whoever says, you are not our lord but our Lord is Allah, upon Him we rely and to Him we turn, we seek refuge with Allah from shirk, he has no power over him.""**"
"12523","Junādah ibn Abī Umayyah رضي الله عنه reported that he and others came to an Anṣārī Companion and pressed him to narrate only what he heard from the Messenger of Allah ﷺ, not from people, whereupon he told that the Prophet ﷺ stood among them warning of the Masīḥ wiped in the left eye, with mountains of bread and rivers of water, remaining forty mornings with dominion reaching every watering place, not entering four mosques, and Allah not one-eyed, with Ibn ʿAwn adding power over one man alone killed then revived. > **""I warn you of the Masīḥ, wiped in the eye, the left. With him journey mountains of bread and rivers of water. His mark is remaining in the earth forty mornings, his dominion reaching every watering place. He comes to no four mosques: the Kaʿbah and the mosque of the Messenger and al-Masjid al-Aqṣā and al-Ṭūr. Whatever is dubious to you, know Allah is not one-eyed. He is given power over a man, killing him then giving him life, and given power over none besides him.""**"
"12524","Junādah ibn Abī Umayyah al-Azdī reported that he and an Anṣārī went to a Companion asking for a ḥadīth heard from the Messenger of Allah ﷺ about the Dajjāl, who told that the Prophet ﷺ preached thrice warning, every prophet warned, he is among this Ummah, curly wheat-hued wiped left eye with garden and fire and bread mountains and water river, raining without growing, power over one soul alone, forty mornings reaching every watering place, not nearing four mosques, and their Lord not one-eyed. > **""I warn you of the Dajjāl, I warn you of the Dajjāl, I warn you of the Dajjāl. No prophet but warned of him. He is among you, O Ummah. He is curly wheat-hued, wiped left eye, with him a garden and a fire, with him mountains of bread and a river of water. He rains rain yet grows no trees. He is given power over a soul, killing it, and given power over none besides it. He remains in the earth forty mornings reaching every watering place, not approaching four mosques: the Sacred Mosque and the mosque of Madīnah and the mosque of al-Ṭūr and al-Masjid al-Aqṣā. Whatever is dubious to you, your Lord is not one-eyed.""**"
"12525","Jābir ibn ʿAbdillāh رضي الله عنهما reported that the Messenger of Allah ﷺ said the Dajjāl emerges amid faltering religion and retreating knowledge, with forty nights roaming earth, a day like a year and like a month and like a week then as their days, riding a donkey forty cubits between ears, saying I am your lord while one-eyed and their Lord not one-eyed, kāfir spelled between his eyes read by every believing literate and illiterate, reaching every water except Madīnah and Makkah forbidden him with angels at gates, mountains of bread while people hunger except followers, two rivers whose true nature the Prophet knows better, his garden fire and his fire garden, devils speaking to people with great fitnah, commanding sky to rain before eyes and asking who but a lord does this, people fleeing to Jabal al-Dukhān in Shām besieged in dire hardship, then ʿĪsā calling at dawn who prevents going out to the vile liar, deemed a jinnī, finding ʿĪsā at prayer, inviting Rūḥ Allah forward while he bids their imam lead, then after Fajr going out, the liar melting like salt in water, ʿĪsā killing him till trees and stones call, no follower left but pursued. > **""The Dajjāl emerges amid faltering religion and retreating knowledge. He has forty nights roaming the earth: a day of them like a year, and a day of them like a month, and a day of them like a week, then the rest of his days as these days of yours. He has a donkey he rides, forty cubits between its ears. He says to people, I am your lord, while he is one-eyed and your Lord is not one-eyed. Written between his eyes is kāfir, spelled, read by every believer literate and illiterate. He comes to every water and watering place except Madīnah and Makkah, forbidden him, with angels standing at their gates. With him are mountains of bread, and people are in hardship except whoever follows him. With him are two rivers, I know them better than he: a river he calls garden and a river he calls fire. Whoever enters what he names garden, it is fire, and whoever enters what he names fire, it is garden. With him are sent devils speaking to people, and with him a great fitnah. He commands the sky and it rains as people see. O people, does any but a lord do the like of this. O people, what prevents you going out to this vile liar. The imam of yours should advance and pray with you. When the liar sees him he melts as salt melts in water. This is a Jew.""**"
"12526","Asmāʾ bint Yazīd al-Anṣāriyyah رضي الله عنها reported that the Messenger of Allah ﷺ was in her house mentioning the Dajjāl, telling of three years before him withholding a third then two-thirds then all rain and growth till every cloven and molar beast perishes, his severest trial coming to a Bedouin offering to revive his camels for lordship acknowledged, devils imaged as finest camels, and to a bereaved man offering father and brother, devils imaged as them, then the Prophet ﷺ left for a need and returned to grieving people, grasped the doorposts and said mahyam, Asmāʾ saying he tore their hearts, he answering if he emerges while alive he contends him else his Lord suffices every believer, and when she asked how believers eat while dough hungers them before baking, he said glorification and sanctification as suffices heaven-dwellers suffices them. > **""Before him are three years: the sky withholds a third of its rain and the earth a third of its growth, then a second withholds two-thirds of its rain and the earth two-thirds of its growth, then a third withholds all its rain and the earth all its growth. No cloven nor molar beast remains but perishes. Among his severest fitnah is coming to a Bedouin saying, if I revive your camels for you, do you not know I am your lord. The devils image for him like his camels finest in udders and greatest in humps. He comes to a man whose father and brother died, saying, if I revive your father and your brother for you, do you not know I am your lord. Mahyam, O Asmāʾ. If he emerges while I am alive, I contend him, else my Lord suffices every believer. What suffices the heaven-dwellers of tasbīḥ and taqdīs suffices them.""**"
"12527","Asmāʾ bint Yazīd رضي الله عنها reported in a narration that the Messenger of Allah ﷺ once sat and spoke of the one-eyed Dajjāl, saying mahyam, his word when asked, commanding witnesses to convey to the absent, declaring Allah sound not one-eyed while the Dajjāl is wiped with kāfir between his eyes read by every believing literate and illiterate, with one route adding five barren years before emergence. > **""Mahyam. Whoever of you attends my gathering and hears my speech, let the witness convey to the absent. Know Allah is sound, not one-eyed, and the Dajjāl is one-eyed wiped, between his eyes written kāfir, read by every believer literate and illiterate. Before his emergence are five barren years.""**"
"12528","Abū Hurayrah رضي الله عنه reported that he heard the Messenger of Allah ﷺ swear the Dajjāl will alight at Khūz and Kirmān among seventy thousand faces like hammered shields. > **""The Dajjāl will alight at Khūz and Kirmān among seventy thousand, their faces like hammered shields.""**"
"12529","Abū Hurayrah رضي الله عنه reported that the Messenger of Allah ﷺ rode to the confluence of torrents and asked shall he tell of the Dajjāls station from Madīnah, pointing to his station. > **""Shall I not tell you of the Dajjāls station from Madīnah. This is his station.""**"
"12530","Abū Hurayrah رضي الله عنه reported that the Messenger of Allah ﷺ said he came out with Laylat al-Qadr and the Masīḥ of misguidance made clear, then two men quarrelled at the mosque bench and his intervening made him forget them, retaining to seek the Night in the last ten, describing the misguidance Masīḥ one-eyed clear-browed broad-chested stooping like Qaṭan ibn ʿAbd al-ʿUzzā, and when asked does his resemblance harm, answered no, the questioner Muslim and he kāfir. > **""I came out to you with Laylat al-Qadr and the Masīḥ of misguidance made clear. Two men quarrelled at the mosque bench, I came to separate them and was made to forget them. Seek Laylat al-Qadr in the last ten. As for the Masīḥ of misguidance, he is one-eyed, clear-browed, broad-chested, stooping, like Qaṭan ibn ʿAbd al-ʿUzzā. No, you are a Muslim man and he is a kāfir man.""**"
"12531","Asmāʾ bint ʿUmays رضي الله عنها reported that the Prophet ﷺ entered upon her for a need then left, she complained of want, and he asked how they will fare tried by a servant with earths rivers and fruits subjected, feeding followers and disbelieving them while depriving opponents, and when she said a slave-girl at the oven an hour for bread nearly unsettles her prayer, how then, he said Allah will protect believers that day as He protected angels, with kāfir between his eyes read by every believing literate and illiterate. > **""How will you fare when tried by a servant with the rivers of the earth and its fruits subjected to him. Whoever follows him, he feeds him and disbelieves him, and whoever disobeys him, he deprives and withholds from him. Allah will protect the believers that day with what He protected the angels thereby of tasbīḥ. Between his eyes is kāfir, read by every believer literate and illiterate.""**"
"12532","Jābir رضي الله عنه reported that the Messenger of Allah ﷺ stood upon the minbar saying he gathered them for no heavenly news, then mentioned the ḥadīth of al-Jassāsah adding he is the Masīḥ for whom earth is folded in forty days except Ṭaybah, which is Madīnah with an unsheathed angel at every gate barring him, and likewise Makkah. > **""O people, I gathered you for no news from heaven. He is the Masīḥ, earth is folded for him in forty days except Ṭaybah. Ṭaybah is Madīnah: no gate of its gates but upon it an angel baring his sword barring him, and likewise at Makkah.""**"
"12533","Abū al-Waddāk reported that Abū Saʿīd رضي الله عنه asked him whether the Khawārij affirm the Dajjāl, and hearing no, cited the Messenger of Allah ﷺ as seal of a thousand prophets or more, every followed prophet warning his Ummah, with clarification to him never given another, one-eyed while their Lord not one-eyed, right eye blind bulging unhidden like spittle on plastered wall, left like glittering star, with him of every tongue and a green image of garden with flowing water and a black smoking image of fire. > **""I am the seal of a thousand prophets or more. No prophet followed but warned his Ummah of the Dajjāl. Clarified to me concerning him what was clarified to none. He is one-eyed, and your Lord is not one-eyed. His right eye is blind bulging unhidden, like spittle on a plastered wall, and his left eye like a glittering star. With him is of every tongue, and with him an image of garden green with water flowing therein and an image of fire black smoking.""**"
"12534","Ibn ʿUmar رضي الله عنهما reported that the Messenger of Allah ﷺ said the Dajjāl alights in this salt flat at Marr Qanāh, most going out to him women, till a man returns to his kinswoman and mother and daughter and sister and aunt binding her fearing her exit, then Allah empowers Muslims killing him and his party till a Jew hides under tree or stone and it says to the Muslim, this Jew beneath me, kill him. > **""The Dajjāl alights in this salt flat at Marr Qanāh. Most going out to him are women, till a man returns to his kinswoman and to his mother and his daughter and his sister and his aunt, binding her fearing she goes out to him. Then Allah empowers the Muslims over him, killing him and killing his party, till a Jew hides under a tree or a stone, and the stone or the tree says to the Muslim, this Jew is beneath me, kill him.""**"
"12535","Asmāʾ bint Yazīd رضي الله عنها reported hearing the Messenger of Allah ﷺ among his Companions warning of the Masīḥ as every prophet warned, describing an unmentioned trait, five barren years before emergence destroying every hoofed creature, asked how believers live, answering as angels live, one-eyed while Allah not one-eyed, kāfir between eyes read by every believing literate and illiterate, followers most Jews women Bedouins, sky seen raining yet not raining and earth sprouting yet not sprouting, saying to Bedouins did I not send copious rain and revive cattle lofty humps bulging flanks flowing milk, devils sent in images of dead fathers brothers kin urging this is your lord follow him, remaining forty years with year like month and month like week and week like day and day like hour and hour like palm-frond burning, reaching every watering place but the two mosques, then the Prophet ﷺ rising for wuḍūʾ hearing weeping, returning and giving glad tidings that if he emerges while among them Allah and His Messenger suffice, and after him Allah is his caliph over every Muslim. > **""I warn you of the Masīḥ and warn you of him. Every prophet warned his people of him. He is among you, O Ummah. I shall tell you of his description untold by prophets before me to their peoples. Before his emergence are five barren years destroying every hoofed creature. Believers live by what angels live by. He is one-eyed, and Allah is not one-eyed. Between his eyes is kāfir, read by every believer literate and illiterate. Most following him are Jews and women and Bedouins. You see sky raining while it rains not and earth sprouting while it sprouts not. He says to the Bedouins, what do you seek from me. Did I not send heaven copiously upon you and revive your cattle lofty humps, bulging flanks, flowing milk. Rejoice, if he emerges while I am among you, Allah and His Messenger suffice you, and if he emerges after me, Allah is my caliph over every Muslim.""**"
"12536","Jābir رضي الله عنه reported that the Messenger of Allah ﷺ said he is the seal of a thousand prophets or more, none but warned his people of the Dajjāl, with clarity to him given none of them, one-eyed while their Lord not one-eyed. > **""I am the seal of a thousand prophets or more. None of them a prophet but warned his people of the Dajjāl. Clarified to me what was clarified to none of them: he is one-eyed, and your Lord is not one-eyed.""**"
"12537","Jubayr ibn Nufayr from his father reported that the Messenger of Allah ﷺ mentioned the Dajjāl and said if he emerges while among them he contends him, and if not among them every man contends himself with Allah his caliph over every Muslim. > **""If he emerges while I am among you, I contend you against him, and if he emerges while I am not among you, every man contends himself and Allah is my caliph over every Muslim.""**"
"12538","Abū Hurayrah رضي الله عنه reported that the Messenger of Allah ﷺ was asked about the Dajjāl and, as the narrator recalls, said he emerges from the east. > **""He emerges from the east.""**"
"12539","al-Falatān ibn ʿĀṣim رضي الله عنه reported that the Messenger of Allah ﷺ said he was shown Laylat al-Qadr then made to forget it, and saw the Masīḥ of misguidance while two men quarrelled at someones threshing floor and his separating them made him forget, so seek it in the last ten, describing the misguidance Masīḥ clear-browed wiped left eye broad-chested like ʿAbd al-ʿUzzā ibn Qaṭan. > **""I was shown Laylat al-Qadr then made to forget it. I saw the Masīḥ of misguidance: two men at someones threshing floor quarrelling, I separated them and was made to forget it. Seek it in the last ten. As for the Masīḥ of misguidance, a man clear-browed, wiped left eye, broad-chested, like ʿAbd al-ʿUzzā ibn Qaṭan.""**"
"12540","ʿAmr ibn ʿAwf رضي الله عنه reported that the Messenger of Allah ﷺ said the world ends not till a Muslim frontier post at Būlān fights Banū al-Aṣfar striving heedless of blame till Allah opens Constantinople and Rome by tasbīḥ and takbīr, demolishing its fortress, dividing wealth in shields, then a crier shouting O people of Islām, the Masīḥ al-Dajjāl emerged in your lands, they ask who cries unknowing, send scouts finding nothing heard, swear the cry was from heaven or earth, resolve to march all together, if the Masīḥ there fight till Allah judges, He best of judges, else return to lands troops kin. > **""The world will not end until a frontier post of Muslims at a place called Būlān fights Banū al-Aṣfar, striving in Allahs path heedless of any blamer concerning Allah, until Allah opens Constantinople and Rome by tasbīḥ and takbīr. Its fortress is demolished, wealth divided in shields. Then a crier cries, O people of Islām, the Masīḥ al-Dajjāl emerged in your lands and homes. They say, who is this crier. They know not who he is. They send scouts to see, is he the Masīḥ. They return saying, we saw nothing and heard it not. They say, the crier cried only from heaven or from earth. Come, we march all together: if the Masīḥ is there, we fight him until Allah judges between us and him, He best of judges, and if the other, they are your lands and troops and kin, you return to them.""**"
"12541","ʿUbādah ibn al-Ṣāmit رضي الله عنه reported that the Messenger of Allah ﷺ said he had spoken of the Dajjāl at length, describing him short bow-legged curly one-eyed with eye effaced neither standing nor deep, and that confusion is removed by knowing they see not their Lord until death. > **""I spoke to you of the Dajjāl at length. He is a man short bow-legged curly one-eyed, eye effaced neither standing nor deep. If confused over him, know you shall not see your Lord until you die.""**"
"12542","Nuhayk ibn Ṣuraym al-Sakūnī رضي الله عنه reported that the Messenger of Allah ﷺ swore they will fight the polytheists until their remnant fights the Dajjāl at the Jordan river, they east of it and they west, with the narrator unsure where the Jordan then lies. > **""You shall fight the polytheists until your remnant fights the Dajjāl at the Jordan river, you east of it and they west of it.""**"
"12543","Abū Hurayrah رضي الله عنه reported hearing Abū al-Qāsim the truthful believed ﷺ tell of the one-eyed Dajjāl of misguidance emerging east amid division, traversing what Allah wills in forty days whose measure Allah knows, believers in dire hardship, then ʿĪsā ibn Maryam descending from heaven leading people, raising from bowing with tasmiʿ while Allah kills the Dajjāl and Muslims prevail, swearing it truth and near as all coming is near. > **""The one-eyed Dajjāl, Masīḥ of misguidance, emerges east in a time of peoples division and discord. He reaches what Allah wills him to reach of earth in forty days, Allah knows their measure. The believers meet dire hardship. Then ʿĪsā ibn Maryam descends from heaven and leads people. When he raises his head from his bowing saying, Allah hears who praises Him, Allah kills the Masīḥ al-Dajjāl and the Muslims prevail. It is truth, and that it is near, all coming is near.""**"
"12544","Abū Hurayrah رضي الله عنه reported that the Messenger of Allah ﷺ foretold opening the city of Heraclius or Caesar and dividing its wealth in shields, then a cry that the Dajjāl succeeded them among families, so casting aside holdings they march to fight. > **""You shall open the city of Heraclius or Caesar and divide its wealth in shields. A cry reaches them that the Dajjāl succeeded them among their families. They cast what is with them and go out fighting.""**"
"12545","Abū Hurayrah رضي الله عنه reported that the Messenger of Allah ﷺ said the Dajjāl alights not in Madīnah but at the trench, angels guarding every pass, the first following him women who harm him, so returning angry to the trench, whereupon ʿĪsā ibn Maryam descends. > **""The Dajjāl alights not in Madīnah but between the trench, upon every pass thereof angels guarding it. The first following him are women, harming him. He returns angry until alighting at the trench. Thereupon ʿĪsā ibn Maryam descends.""**"
"12546","Jābir رضي الله عنه reported that the Messenger of Allah ﷺ mentioning the Masīḥ al-Dajjāl promised a word no prophet before him spoke, that he is one-eyed while Allah is not, with kāfir written between his eyes, adding every believing literate and illiterate reads it, roaming earth forty days reaching every land but Madīnah and Makkah forbidden him, a day like a year and like a month and like a week, the rest as their days, remaining only forty days. > **""I shall say to you concerning him a word no prophet before me said: he is one-eyed, and Allah is not one-eyed. Between his eyes is written kāfir. Every believer literate and illiterate reads it. He roams the earth forty days, coming to every land but these two cities: Madīnah and Makkah, forbidden him. A day of his days like a year, and a day like a month, and a day like a week, and the rest of his days as these days of yours. He remains only forty days.""**"
"12547","Anas رضي الله عنه reported that the Messenger of Allah ﷺ said he is first to enter Garden on Resurrection Day and to intercede, and men of his Ummah will reach ʿĪsā ibn Maryam and witness the battle against the Dajjāl. > **""I am first to enter Garden on Resurrection Day and to intercede. Men of my Ummah shall reach ʿĪsā ibn Maryam and witness the battle against the Dajjāl.""**"
"12548","ʿAbdullāh ibn Busr رضي الله عنه reported hearing the Messenger of Allah ﷺ swear those who reached him will reach the Dajjāl, or emergence stands near his death. > **""Those who reached me shall reach the Dajjāl, or it shall be near my death.""**"
"12549","al-ʿUryān ibn al-Haytham reported entering upon Yazīd ibn Muʿāwiyah where a man took his cushion and reclined, identified as ʿAbdullāh ibn ʿAmr رضي الله عنهما, who reproached Iraqis for taking ḥadīth from lower levels not upper, then when the Dajjāl was mentioned asked whether their land held a salt flat with palms called Kūthā, and on assent named it his place of emergence. > **""In your land is there a land called Kūthā of salt flats and palms. He emerges from it.""**"
"12550","ʿAbdullāh ibn ʿAmr رضي الله عنهما reported from the Messenger of Allah ﷺ concerning the Dajjāl that whatever is dubious, Allah is not one-eyed, emerging forty mornings reaching every watering place but the Kaʿbah and Bayt al-Maqdis and Madīnah, month like week and week like day, with garden and fire reversed, mountain of bread and river of water, summoning a man over whom alone power is granted, asking what he says, answered enemy of Allah lying Dajjāl, sawn from head till fallen, revived and questioned again, answering insight only sharpened identifying the enemy the Messenger foretold, then sword lunged powerless and order to remove him. > **""Whatever is dubious to you of him, Allah is not one-eyed. He emerges remaining in the earth forty mornings, coming to every watering place but the Kaʿbah and Bayt al-Maqdis and Madīnah. The month like the week, and the week like the day. With him a garden and a fire: his fire a garden and his garden a fire. With him a mountain of bread and a river of water. He calls a man, Allah granting him power over him alone, saying, what do you say of me. He says, you are Allahs enemy, the lying Dajjāl. He calls for a saw placed at his head, sawing him till fallen upon earth. Then he revives him saying, what do you say. He says, I was never more insightful concerning you than now: you are Allahs enemy, the Dajjāl whom the Messenger of Allah told us of. He lunges at him with his sword yet cannot prevail over him, saying, keep him from me.""**"
"12551","Jubayr ibn Nufayr from his father reported that the Messenger of Allah ﷺ mentioned the Dajjāl and said if he emerges while among them he contends him else every man contends himself with Allah his caliph, describing him eyeless like ʿAbd al-ʿUzzā ibn Qaṭan al-Khuzāʿī with kāfir between eyes read by every Muslim, commanding whoever meets him to recite the Opening of the Book, seeing him emerge from a pass between Shām and al-ʿIrāq ravaging right and left, thrice commanding O servants of Allah stand firm, and when asked his speed answering like cloud driven by wind, and his stay forty days a day like a year and like a month and like a week and rest as their days, and when asked about prayer therein commanding estimation. > **""If he emerges while I am among you, I contend you against him, and if he emerges while I am not among you, every man contends himself and Allah is my caliph over every Muslim. He is eyeless as ʿAbd al-ʿUzzā ibn Qaṭan al-Khuzāʿī. Written between his eyes is kāfir, read by every Muslim. Whoever of you meets him, let him recite over him the Opening of the Book. I saw him emerging from a pass between Shām and al-ʿIrāq, ravaging right and left. O servants of Allah, stand firm. Like a cloud driven by wind. Forty days: a day of them like a year, and a day of them like a month, and a day like a week, and the rest as these days of yours. Rather estimate for it.""**"
"12552","ʿUrwah ibn al-Zubayr reported that Umm Salamah رضي الله عنها said she mentioned the Dajjāl one night and sleep left her, and in the morning told the Messenger of Allah ﷺ, who forbade such fear, saying if he emerges while among them his Lord suffices through him, and after his death suffices through the righteous, then stood mentioning the Dajjāl warning as every prophet warned, one-eyed while Allah not one-eyed, his eye like a floating grape. > **""Do not do so. If he emerges while I am among you, my Lord suffices you through me, and if he emerges after I die, He suffices you through the righteous. Every prophet warned his Ummah, and I warn you of him. He is one-eyed, and Allah is not one-eyed. The Masīḥ al-Dajjāl, as though his eye a floating grape.""**"
"12553","Abū Ṣādiq reported that ʿAbdullāh ibn Masʿūd رضي الله عنه said he knew households frightened by the Dajjāl, and when asked who, O Abū ʿAbd al-Raḥmān, named the houses of Kūfah. > **""I know households frightened by the Dajjāl: the houses of the people of al-Kūfah.""**"
"12554","Abū al-Shaʿthāʾ reported that the Dajjāl was mentioned before ʿAbdullāh ibn Masʿūd رضي الله عنه, who said speak not much of him, for a decree in heaven descends fastest when upon peoples tongues, asking how they will fare when foes secure and they fearful, foes in shade and they in sun, with the narration adding the Prophetic word that he emerges not till people forget him and imams abandon him on minbars. > **""Speak not much of him. When a matter is decreed in heaven, fastest for its descent to earth is its appearing upon peoples tongues. How will you fare when the people are secure and you fearful. How will you fare when the people are in shade and you in sun. The Dajjāl emerges not until people forget his mention and imams abandon his mention upon the minbars.""**"
"12555","Khaythamah reported that the Dajjāl was mentioned before ʿAbdullāh رضي الله عنه, some saying had he emerged they would pelt him with stones, whereupon ʿAbdullāh answered were he at Bābil in the morning some would in the morning complain to him of bare feet from haste. > **""Were he at Bābil in the morning, some of them in the morning would complain to him of bare feet from haste.""**"
"12556","Abū Hurayrah رضي الله عنه reported that the Dajjāl was mentioned before the Prophet ﷺ, who told of his mother bearing him cast aside in her grave, and when she bears him women conceive by erring men. > **""His mother bears him cast aside in her grave. When she bears him, women conceive by erring men.""**"
"12557","From ʿImran ibn Husayn, may Allah be pleased with him, who said: The Messenger of Allah ﷺ said, meaning the Dajjal: He has eaten food and walked in the markets. > **""He has eaten food, and walked in the markets.""**"
"12558","From Maʿqil ibn Yasar, may Allah be pleased with him, that the Messenger of Allah ﷺ said, meaning the Dajjal: He has eaten food and walked in the markets. > **""He has eaten food, and walked in the markets.""**"
"12559","From Abu Dharr, may Allah be pleased with him, who said: That I swear ten times that Ibn Sayyad is the Dajjal is dearer to me than that I swear once that he is not he. He also said: The Messenger of Allah ﷺ sent me to his mother and said: Ask her how long she bore him. He said: So I came to her and asked her, and she said: I bore him for twelve months. He said: Then he sent me again to her and said: Ask her about his cry when he fell at birth. He said: So I returned to her and asked her, and she said: He cried with the cry of a one-month-old boy. Then the Messenger of Allah ﷺ said to him: I have concealed for you something hidden. He said: You concealed for me the rope of a dusty sheep and smoke. He intended to say al-dukhan but could not manage it, so he said: Al-dukh. So the Messenger of Allah ﷺ said: Begone, for you will never exceed your measure. > **""Ask her how long she bore him?""**"
"12560","From Jabir ibn ʿAbdillah, may Allah be pleased with them, who said: A woman of the Jews in Madinah bore a boy whose eye was wiped out, protruding and bulging, so the Messenger of Allah ﷺ feared that he was the Dajjal. He found him beneath a velvet cloth muttering, and his mother alerted him, saying: O ʿAbdullah, here is Abu al-Qasim come, so go out to him. He came out from the cloth, so the Messenger of Allah ﷺ said: What is wrong with her, may Allah destroy her? Had she left him it would have become clear. Then he said: O Ibn Sayyad, what do you see? He said: I see truth, and I see falsehood, and I see a throne upon water, so the matter became confused for him. He said: Do you testify that I am the Messenger of Allah? So he said: Do you testify that I am the messenger of Allah? So the Messenger of Allah ﷺ said: I believe in Allah and His messengers. Then he left and abandoned him. Then he came to him a second time and found him in his date-palms muttering, and his mother alerted him, saying: O ʿAbdullah, here is Abu al-Qasim come. So the Messenger of Allah ﷺ said: What is wrong with her, may Allah destroy her? Had she left him it would have become clear. The Messenger of Allah ﷺ loved to hear something of his speech so as to know whether he was he or not. He said: O Ibn Sayyad, what do you see? He said: Do you testify that I am the messenger of Allah? So the Messenger of Allah ﷺ said: I believe in Allah and His messengers. So the matter became confused for him, and he left and abandoned him. Then he came a third or fourth time, with Abu Bakr and ʿUmar, may Allah be pleased with them, among a party of the Muhajirun and Ansar, and I was with him. He said: So the Messenger of Allah ﷺ hastened before us, hoping to hear something of his speech, but his mother raced ahead of him and said: O ʿAbdullah, here is Abu al-Qasim come. So the Messenger of Allah ﷺ said: What is wrong with her, may Allah destroy her? Had she left him it would have become clear. He said: O Ibn Sayyad, what do you see? He said: I see truth, and I see falsehood, and I see a throne upon water. He said: Do you testify that I am the Messenger of Allah? He said: Do you testify that you are the messenger of Allah? So the Messenger of Allah ﷺ said: I believe in Allah and His messengers. So the matter became confused for him. So the Messenger of Allah ﷺ said: O Ibn Sayyad, I have concealed for you something hidden. He said: It is al-dukh. So the Messenger of Allah ﷺ said: Begone, begone. Then ʿUmar ibn al-Khattab, may Allah be pleased with him, said: Permit me, O Messenger of Allah. So the Messenger of Allah ﷺ said: If he is he, you are not his man; his man is only ʿIsa ibn Maryam, peace be upon him, and if he is not he, it is not for you to kill a man of the covenant people. He said: The Messenger of Allah ﷺ remained convinced that he was the Dajjal. > **""O Ibn Sayyad, what do you see?""**"
"12561","From Abu al-Tufayl, may Allah be pleased with him, when he was asked: Did you see the Messenger of Allah ﷺ? He said: Yes. It was said: Did you speak to him? He said: No, but I saw him set out for such-and-such a place with ʿAbdullah ibn Masʿud, may Allah be pleased with him, and men of his Companions, until he came to a spacious house, and said: Open this door. So it was opened, and the Prophet ﷺ entered, and I entered with him, and there was a velvet cloth in the middle of the house. He said: Lift this cloth. So they lifted the cloth, and there was a one-eyed boy beneath the cloth. He said: Rise, O boy. So the boy rose, and he said: O boy, do you testify that I am the Messenger of Allah ﷺ? The boy said: Do you testify that I am the messenger of Allah? The Messenger of Allah ﷺ said twice: Seek refuge in Allah from the evil of this. > **""Seek refuge in Allah from the evil of this.""**"
"12562","From Zayd ibn Harithah, may Allah be pleased with him, who said: The Prophet ﷺ said to some of his Companions: Go forth. So the Messenger of Allah ﷺ went forth with his Companions with him, until they entered between two walls in a long lane, and when they reached the house there was a woman sitting, and there was a small waterskin full of water. The Prophet ﷺ said: I see a waterskin, but I do not see its bearer. So the woman pointed to a velvet cloth in a corner of the house. They went to the cloth and uncovered it, and beneath it was a man. He raised his head, so the Prophet ﷺ said: Disfigured be the faces. He said: O Muhammad, why do you speak harshly to me? The Prophet ﷺ said: I have concealed for you something hidden, so tell me what it is. The Prophet ﷺ had concealed for him Surat al-Dukhan, so he said: Al-dukh. He said: Begone, what Allah wills shall be. Then he departed. > **""I have concealed for you something hidden, so tell me what it is?""**"
"12563","From Abu Saʿid al-Khudri, may Allah be pleased with him, that the Messenger of Allah ﷺ said to Ibn Sayyad: What do you see? He said: I see a throne upon the sea and snakes around it. The Messenger of Allah ﷺ said: You see the throne of Iblis. > **""What do you see?""**"
"12564","From Abu Saʿid al-Khudri, may Allah be pleased with him, who said: Ibn Sayyad was mentioned before the Messenger of Allah ﷺ, so ʿUmar, may Allah be pleased with him, said: He claims that he does not pass by anything except that it speaks to him. > **""He does not pass by anything except that it speaks to him.""**"
"12565","From ʿAbdullah ibn Masʿud, may Allah be pleased with him, who said: That I swear by Allah nine times that Ibn Sayyad is the Dajjal is dearer to me than that I swear once that he is not he. And that I swear nine times that the Messenger of Allah ﷺ was slain as a martyr is dearer to me than that I swear that he was not slain; and that is because Allah made him a Prophet and took him as a martyr. > **""That Ibn Sayyad is the Dajjal.""**"
"12566","From al-Husayn ibn ʿAli, may Allah be pleased with them, that the Prophet ﷺ concealed smoke for Ibn al-Sayyad, then asked him about what he had concealed for him, so he said: Dukh. He said: Begone, you will never exceed your measure. When he turned away, the Prophet ﷺ said: What did he say? Some said: Wakh. And some said: Rather he said: Dukh. So the Prophet ﷺ said: You have differed while I am among you, so after me you will differ more severely. > **""Begone, you will never exceed your measure.""**"
"12567","From al-Mughirah ibn Shuʿbah, may Allah be pleased with him, who said: None asked the Prophet ﷺ about the Dajjal more than I asked him, so he said: What will you do with him? He will not harm you. I said: Shall I not kill Ibn Sayyad? He said: What would you do by killing him? If he is the Dajjal you will never attain to killing him, and if he is not the Dajjal then what will you do with him? > **""He will not harm you.""**"
"12568","From Abu Hurayrah, may Allah be pleased with him, who said: The Messenger of Allah ﷺ said: The Messiah ʿIsa ibn Maryam, peace be upon him, is about to descend as a just judge and equitable Imam, so he will kill the pig, and break the cross, and the call will be one, so convey to him – or convey to him – greeting from the Messenger of Allah ﷺ, and I shall speak to him and he will believe me. When death approached him he said: Convey to him greeting from me. > **""Convey to him greeting from the Messenger of Allah.""**"
"12569","From Abu Hurayrah, may Allah be pleased with him, from the Prophet ﷺ who said: I do hope, if my life is prolonged, to meet ʿIsa ibn Maryam, peace be upon him, but if death is hastened for me, then whoever of you meets him, let him convey to him greeting from me. > **""Whoever of you meets him, let him convey to him greeting from me.""**"
"12570","From Ibn Harmalah – and he is Khalid ibn ʿAbdillah ibn Harmalah – from his maternal aunt, may Allah be pleased with them, who said: The Messenger of Allah ﷺ gave a sermon while his head was bandaged from the sting of a scorpion, and said: You say: There is no enemy, yet you will not cease fighting an enemy until Yaʾjuj and Maʾjuj come, broad of face, small of eye, reddish of hair, hastening from every mound, as though their faces were hammered shields. > **""You will not cease fighting an enemy until Yaʾjuj and Maʾjuj come.""**"
"12571","From ʿAbdullah ibn ʿAmr, may Allah be pleased with them, from the Prophet ﷺ who said: Yaʾjuj and Maʾjuj are from the children of Adam, and were they released they would corrupt the livelihoods of people, and none of them dies until he leaves among his progeny a thousand or more, and beyond them are three nations: Tawil, and Taris, and Mansak. > **""Yaʾjuj and Maʾjuj are from the children of Adam.""**"
"12572","From Hudhayfah ibn al-Yaman, may Allah be pleased with him, who said: I asked the Messenger of Allah ﷺ about Yaʾjuj and Maʾjuj, so he said: Yaʾjuj is a nation, and Maʾjuj is a nation, each nation consisting of four hundred thousand nations, and no man dies until he looks upon a thousand males before him from his own loins, each bearing arms. I said: O Messenger of Allah, describe them for us. He said: They are three kinds: a kind of them like al-arz. I said: What is al-arz? He said: A tree in Sham, the tree being one hundred and twenty cubits tall into the sky. Then the Messenger of Allah ﷺ said: These are they whom no horses nor iron can withstand, and a kind of them who spread one ear as a bed and wrap themselves in the other, who pass by no elephant nor wild beast nor camel nor pig except that they eat it, and whoever of them dies they eat him, their vanguard in Sham and their rear in Khurasan, drinking the rivers of the east and the Lake of Tiberias. > **""Yaʾjuj is a nation, and Maʾjuj is a nation.""**"
"12573","From Abu Umamah, may Allah be pleased with him, raising it to the Prophet ﷺ who said: The Beast will emerge and mark people upon their noses, then they will remain engrossed therein, until a man buys a camel and says: From whom did you buy it? So he says: I bought it from one of the bridled ones. > **""The Beast will emerge and mark people upon their noses.""**"
"12574","From Abu Umamah, may Allah be pleased with him, in a variant: Then they will dwell among you, meaning the marked people will remain and mingle among you after the emergence of the Beast. > **""Then they will dwell among you.""**"
"12575","From Ibn ʿUmar, may Allah be pleased with them, who said: Shall I not show you the place of which the Messenger of Allah ﷺ said: I deem that the Beast will emerge therefrom. So he struck with his staff the cleft that is in al-Safa and said: It is possessed of feathers and down, and a third of it will emerge at the pace of a swift horse for three days and three nights, and it will pass upon them while they flee from it to the mosques, so it will say to them: Do you deem the mosques will save you from me? Then it will mark them, they will be driven in the markets, and it will say: O disbeliever, O believer. > **""Do you deem the mosques will save you from me?""**"
"12576","From Abu Sarihah – meaning Hudhayfah ibn Asid, may Allah be pleased with him – from the Messenger of Allah ﷺ that he said: The Beast has three emergences in time: an emergence in the farthest Yemen, until its mention spreads in the desert while its mention does not enter the town, then it hides for a long time after that, then it emerges an emergence near Makkah, so its mention spreads among the people of the desert, and its mention spreads in Makkah, then it remains for a long time, then it surprises people in the greatest mosque in sanctity before Allah and the best and most honoured before Allah, the Sacred Mosque, none being startled except at the side of the mosque, coming between the Corner and the Station toward the door of Banu Makhzum, to the right of one exiting the mosque, so the people scatter from it in groups and together, and a band of Muslims stands firm for it, knowing they cannot escape Allah, so it emerges upon them shaking dust from its head, then it appears and brightens their faces until it leaves them as though brilliant stars, then it turns into the earth, no seeker overtakes it and no fleer escapes it, until a man rises seeking refuge from it in prayer, so it comes to him and says: O so-and-so, now you pray? Then it turns to him with its face, marks him in his face, and departs. And people will associate in their homes, and in their journeys, and share in wealth, and the disbeliever will be known from the believer, until the believer says to the disbeliever: O disbeliever, fulfill my right, and until the disbeliever says to the believer: O believer, fulfill my right. > **""O so-and-so, now you pray?""**"
"12577","From Abu Hurayrah, may Allah be pleased with him, who said: The Messenger of Allah ﷺ said: Wretched is the mountain path Jiyad. He said it twice or thrice. He said: For what, O Messenger of Allah? He said: The Beast will emerge, and cry three cries, so all between the two horizons will hear it. > **""The Beast will emerge, and cry three cries.""**"
"12578","From Hudhayfah ibn Asid, may Allah be pleased with him – I deem he raised it – who said: The Beast will emerge from the greatest mosque, and while they are there the earth will tremble, and while they are thus it will split open. Ibn ʿUyaynah said: It will emerge when the Imam hastens from Muzdalifah, and the vanguard of the pilgrims was appointed only to inform people that the Beast has not emerged. And from ʿAbdullah ibn ʿUmar, may Allah be pleased with them, who said: The Messenger of Allah ﷺ said: When the sun rises from its west, Iblis will fall prostrate, calling aloud: My God, command me to prostrate to whomsoever You will. He said: So his devils gather to him and say: O their master, what is this supplication? He says: I asked my Lord, mighty and majestic, to grant me respite till the known time, and this is the known time. He said: Then the Beast of the earth will emerge from a cleft in al-Safa, its first step falling at Antioch, then it will come to Iblis and strike him. > **""When the sun rises from its west, Iblis will fall prostrate.""**"
"12579","From Abu Zurʿah ibn ʿAmr ibn Jarir, may Allah have mercy on him, who said: Three men of the Muslims sat with Marwan in Madinah and heard him speaking about the Signs, that the first of them is the emergence of the Dajjal. So the people went to ʿAbdullah ibn ʿAmr, may Allah be pleased with them, and told him what they had heard from Marwan about the Signs. So ʿAbdullah said: Marwan said nothing. I have memorized from the Messenger of Allah ﷺ in like manner a hadith I have not yet forgotten, I heard the Messenger of Allah ﷺ say: The first of the Signs to emerge is the rising of the sun from its west, and the Beast in the forenoon, so whichever of the two is before its companion, the other follows upon its trace. Then ʿAbdullah – and he used to read the books – said: And I deem the first of them to emerge is the rising of the sun from its west; and that is because whenever it sets it comes beneath the Throne, prostrates, and seeks permission to return, so it is permitted to return, until when it appears to Allah that it rise from its west, it does as it used to do, comes beneath the Throne, prostrates, and seeks permission to return, but nothing is returned to it, then it seeks permission to return, but nothing is returned to it, until when as much of the night as Allah wills to pass has passed, and it knows that if it were permitted to return it would not reach the east, it says: My Lord, how distant is the east, who is for the people? Until when the horizon becomes as a ring, it seeks permission to return, so it is said to it: Rise from your place. So it rises upon people from its west. Then ʿAbdullah recited this verse: On the day when some of the signs of your Lord come, no soul will benefit from its faith if it had not believed before or earned good in its faith. > **""The first of the Signs to emerge is the rising of the sun from its west.""**"
"12580","From Abu Sarihah Hudhayfah ibn Asid, may Allah be pleased with him, who said: The Messenger of Allah ﷺ said: There will come the wind in which Allah takes the soul of every believer, then the sun will rise from its west, and it is the Sign which Allah mentioned in His Book. > **""Then the sun will rise from its west.""**"
"12581","From Abu Umamah, may Allah be pleased with him, who said: The Messenger of Allah ﷺ said: The first of the Signs is the rising of the sun from its west. > **""The first of the Signs is the rising of the sun from its west.""**"
"12582","From Suhar al-ʿAbdi, may Allah be pleased with him, who said: The Messenger of Allah ﷺ said: The Hour will not stand until tribes are swallowed, so it is said: Who remains of Banu Fulan? He said: So I knew when he said tribes that they are the Arabs; for the non-Arabs are ascribed to their villages. > **""The Hour will not stand until tribes are swallowed.""**"
"12583","From Buqayrah, wife of al-Qaʿqaʿ, may Allah be pleased with her, who said: I was sitting in the women's portico, and I heard the Messenger of Allah ﷺ preaching while gesturing with his left hand, saying: O people, when you hear of a swallowing here, then the Hour has loomed. > **""O people, when you hear of a swallowing here, then the Hour has loomed.""**"
"12584","From Abu Saʿid al-Khudri, may Allah be pleased with him, that the Messenger of Allah ﷺ said: Thunderbolts will increase near the approach of the Hour, until a man comes and says: Who was struck before you this morning? So they say: So-and-so and so-and-so were struck. > **""Thunderbolts will increase near the approach of the Hour.""**"
"12585","From Junadah ibn Umayyah, who heard ʿUbadah ibn al-Samit, may Allah be pleased with him, mention that a man came to the Prophet ﷺ and said: O Messenger of Allah, what is the duration of ease for your ummah? He did not return anything to him, until he asked him three times, each time he did not answer him, then the man departed, then the Prophet ﷺ said: Where is the questioner? So they returned him to him, and he said: You asked me about a thing none of my ummah asked me about, the duration of ease for my ummah is one hundred years. He said it twice or thrice. The man said: O Messenger of Allah, is there for that a portent or sign or verse? He said: Yes, swallowing, and quaking, and dispatch of raiding devils upon people. > **""The duration of ease for my ummah is one hundred years.""**"
"12586","From Farqad al-Sabakhi who said: Habib Abu Habib al-Shami narrated to me, from Abu ʿAtaʾ, from ʿUbadah ibn al-Samit, from the Messenger of Allah ﷺ. He said: And Shahr ibn Hawshab narrated to me, from ʿAbd al-Rahman ibn Ghanm, from the Messenger of Allah ﷺ. He said: ʿAsim ibn ʿAmr al-Bajali narrated to me, from Abu Umamah, from the Messenger of Allah ﷺ. He said: And Saʿid ibn al-Musayyab narrated to me – or it was narrated to me from him – from Ibn ʿAbbas, from the Messenger of Allah ﷺ who said: By Him in Whose Hand is the soul of Muhammad, men of my ummah will spend the night upon insolence and wantonness and play and amusement, then in the morning become apes and pigs, for deeming the unlawful lawful, and taking singing women, and drinking wine, and devouring usury, and wearing silk. > **""They will in the morning become apes and pigs.""**"
"12587","From Anas, may Allah be pleased with him, who said: The Messenger of Allah ﷺ said: There will be in this ummah swallowing, and transformation, and quaking, and pelting. > **""There will be in this ummah swallowing, and transformation, and quaking, and pelting.""**"
"12588","From Abu Hurayrah, may Allah be pleased with him, from the Prophet ﷺ who said: By Him Who sent me with the truth, the world will not pass until swallowing, and pelting, and transformation befall them. They said: And when is that, O Messenger of Allah? He said: When you see women riding saddles, and singing women increase, and false testimony spreads, and men suffice themselves with men, and women with women. And he added: And the crucified one drinks in vessels of idolatry, gold and silver. He said: And men suffice themselves with men, and women with women, and they draw close and prepare. And he gestured with his hand, placing it upon his forehead, covering his face. > **""When you see women riding saddles, and singing women increase.""**"
"12589","From Sahl ibn Saʿd, may Allah be pleased with him, that the Messenger of Allah ﷺ said: There will be in the latter days swallowing, and pelting, and transformation. It was said: And when is that, O Messenger of Allah? He said: When musical instruments and singing women appear, and wine is deemed lawful. > **""When musical instruments and singing women appear, and wine is deemed lawful.""**"
"12590","From Ibn ʿAbbas, may Allah be pleased with them, who said: The Messenger of Allah ﷺ said: A people of this ummah will spend the night over food and drink and amusement, then in the morning they will have been transformed into apes and pigs. > **""They will have been transformed into apes and pigs.""**"
"12591","From Abu Saʿid al-Khudri, may Allah be pleased with him, from the Prophet ﷺ who said: There will be in this ummah swallowing, and transformation, and pelting, among users of singing women, and drinkers of wine, and wearers of silk. > **""There will be in this ummah swallowing, and transformation, and pelting.""**"
"12592","From ʿAbdullah ibn Bishr, Companion of the Messenger of Allah ﷺ, may Allah be pleased with him, who said: I heard him say: There will be in the latter part of this ummah a people, while they are drinking wine and striking instruments, until Allah overturns upon them, and they return as apes and pigs. > **""They return as apes and pigs.""**"
"12593","From Saʿid ibn Abi Rashid, may Allah be pleased with him, who said: I heard the Messenger of Allah ﷺ say: In my ummah there is swallowing, and transformation, and pelting. > **""In my ummah there is swallowing, and transformation, and pelting.""**"
"12594","From Anas ibn Malik, may Allah be pleased with him, who said: Umm Sulaym, may Allah be pleased with her, used to tend the wounded in the army of the Messenger of Allah ﷺ, so she said: O Messenger of Allah, would that you supplicate for my son. The Messenger of Allah ﷺ said: Unays? She said: Yes. So he seated me before him and wiped over my head and said: O Unays, the Muslims after me will found cities, among what they found a city called al-Basrah, so if you come to it, beware its places of dissipation and its market and the door of its ruler, for therein will be swallowing, and transformation, and pelting, the sign of that being that justice dies, and tyranny spreads therein, and adultery increases therein, and false testimony spreads therein. > **""Beware its places of dissipation and its market and the door of its ruler.""**"
"12595","From Umm Salamah, may Allah be pleased with her, who said: I heard the Messenger of Allah ﷺ say: After me there will be a swallowing in the east, and a swallowing in the west, in the Arabian Peninsula. I said: O Messenger of Allah, will the earth be swallowed while the righteous are among it? The Messenger of Allah ﷺ said to her: When its people increase in filth. > **""When its people increase in filth.""**"
"12596","From Umm Salamah, Mother of the Believers, may Allah be pleased with her, who said: I asked the Messenger of Allah ﷺ about one transformed, will he have progeny? He said: No one ever transformed then had progeny nor descendants. > **""No one ever transformed then had progeny nor descendants.""**"
"12597","From ʿAbdullah ibn ʿUmar, may Allah be pleased with them, who said: The Messenger of Allah ﷺ said: No nation ever transformed then has progeny. > **""No nation ever transformed then has progeny.""**"
"12598","From ʿAyyash ibn Abi Rabiʿah, may Allah be pleased with him, who said: I heard the Messenger of Allah ﷺ say: There will emerge a wind before the Hour, in which the souls of every believer will be taken. > **""There will emerge a wind before the Hour.""**"
"12599","From Anas, may Allah be pleased with him, who said: The Messenger of Allah ﷺ said: The Hour will not stand until it is not said on earth: There is no god but Allah. > **""The Hour will not stand until it is not said on earth: There is no god but Allah.""**"
"12600","From Abu Dharr, may Allah be pleased with him, who said: We came with the Messenger of Allah ﷺ and saw Dhu al-Hulayfah, so men hastened to Madinah, and the Messenger of Allah ﷺ spent the night, and we spent it with him, then in the morning he asked about them, so it was said: They hastened to Madinah. He said: They hastened to Madinah and women, yet they will leave it at its best as it was. Then he said: Would that I knew when a fire will emerge from Yemen, from Mount al-Warraq, from which the necks of camels kneeling at Busra will be lit as the light of day. > **""They will leave it at its best as it was.""**"
"12601","From Rafiʿ ibn Busr or Bishr al-Sulami, from his father, may Allah be pleased with them, that the Messenger of Allah ﷺ said: A fire is about to emerge from Habs Sayl, moving like a slow camel, moving by day and resting by night, going in the morning and evening, it being said: The fire went in the morning, O people, so go, the fire rested at midday, O people, so rest, the fire went in the evening, O people, so go, whomever it overtakes it devours him. > **""Whomever it overtakes it devours him.""**"
"12602","From ʿAbdullah ibn ʿAmr, may Allah be pleased with them, who said: The Messenger of Allah ﷺ said: A fire will be dispatched upon the people of the east, gathering them to the west, spending the night with them where they spend, and resting at midday with them where they rest, whatever falls from them and lags belongs to it, driving them as a broken camel is driven. > **""A fire will be dispatched upon the people of the east, gathering them to the west.""**"
"12603","From ʿAbdullah ibn Salam, may Allah be pleased with him, from the Prophet ﷺ that he was asked about the first portents of the Hour, so the Prophet ﷺ said: The first of the portents of the Hour is a fire emerging from the east, gathering them to the west. > **""A fire emerging from the east, gathering them to the west.""**"
"12604","From ʿAsim ibn ʿAdi al-Ansari, may Allah be pleased with him, who said: The Messenger of Allah ﷺ asked us soon after arriving, saying: Where is Habs Sayl? We said: We do not know. Then a man of Banu Sulaym passed by me, so I said: Whence did you come? He said: From Habs Sayl. So I called for my sandals and hastened down to the Messenger of Allah ﷺ and said: O Messenger of Allah, you asked us about Habs Sayl, so we said: We have no knowledge of it, and this man passed by me, so I asked him, and he claimed his family is there. So the Messenger of Allah ﷺ asked him: Where is your family? He said: In Habs Sayl. He said: Remove your family therefrom, for a fire is about to emerge therefrom lighting the necks of camels at Busra. > **""Remove your family therefrom.""**"
"12605","From ʿIlbaʾ al-Sulami, may Allah be pleased with him, who said: I heard the Messenger of Allah ﷺ say: The Hour will not stand except upon the vilest of people. > **""The Hour will not stand except upon the vilest of people.""**"
"12606","From ʿAbdullah ibn ʿAmr, may Allah be pleased with them, who said: The Messenger of Allah ﷺ said: The Hour will not stand until Allah takes His elect from the people of the earth, so there remains therein a rabble who know no good nor deny evil. > **""There remains therein a rabble who know no good nor deny evil.""**"
"12607","From ʿAli, may Allah be pleased with him, that the Prophet ﷺ said: Among the worst of people are those whom the Hour overtakes while they are alive, and those who take graves as mosques, and those who bear testimony before they are asked for it. > **""Among the worst of people are those whom the Hour overtakes while they are alive.""**"
"12608","From ʿAbdullah – meaning Ibn Masʿud, may Allah be pleased with him – from the Prophet ﷺ who said: Among the worst of people are those whom the Hour overtakes while they are alive, and those who take graves as mosques. > **""Among the worst of people are those whom the Hour overtakes while they are alive.""**"
"12609","From Muʿawiyah, may Allah be pleased with him, who said: I heard the Messenger of Allah ﷺ say: The affair will only increase in severity, and people will only increase in miserliness, and the Hour will not stand except upon the worst of people. > **""The Hour will not stand except upon the worst of people.""**"
